const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"The King of Navarre's park.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ferdinand, King of Navarre, Berowne, Longaville and Dumaine.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Let fame, that all hunt after in their lives,","Live registered upon our brazen tombs,","And then grace us in the disgrace of death;","When, spite of cormorant devouring time,","Th' endeavour of this present breath may buy","That honour which shall bate his scythe's keen edge,","And make us heirs of all eternity.","Therefore, brave conquerors, for so you are","That war against your own affections","And the huge army of the world's desires,","Our late edict shall strongly stand in force.","Navarre shall be the wonder of the world;","Our court shall be a little academe,","Still and contemplative in living art.","You three, Berowne, Dumaine and Longaville,","Have sworn for three years' term to live with me,","My fellow-scholars, and to keep those statutes","That are recorded in this schedule here.","Your oaths are passed, and now subscribe your names,","That his own hand may strike his honour down","That violates the smallest branch herein.","If you are armed to do as sworn to do,","Subscribe to your deep oaths, and keep it too."],"id":2,"m":"Let the fame that everyone chases in life be carved on our brass tombs, and honour us even when death has disgraced us. In spite of greedy, devouring time, what we achieve while we are alive can buy the kind of honour that blunts time's sharp scythe and makes us heirs to eternity. So, brave conquerors (and you are conquerors, since you are at war with your own feelings and the huge army of worldly desires), our recent decree will stand firm. Navarre will be the wonder of the world; our court will be a little academy, quiet and thoughtful, devoted to the art of living. You three, Berowne, Dumaine and Longaville, have sworn to live with me for three years as fellow students, and to keep the rules written down in this document. You have taken the oath; now sign your names, so that anyone who breaks the smallest rule strikes down his own honour with his own hand. If you are ready to do what you swore, sign your solemn oaths, and keep them too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["I am resolved. 'Tis but a three years' fast.","The mind shall banquet, though the body pine.","Fat paunches have lean pates, and dainty bits","Make rich the ribs, but bankrupt quite the wits."],"id":3,"m":"I've made up my mind. It's only a three-year fast. The mind will feast, though the body starves. Fat bellies have thin brains, and rich food fattens the ribs but bankrupts the wits."},{"t":"sd","text":"He signs.","id":4},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["My loving lord, Dumaine is mortified.","The grosser manner of these world's delights","He throws upon the gross world's baser slaves.","To love, to wealth, to pomp, I pine and die,","With all these living in philosophy."],"id":5,"m":"My dear lord, Dumaine has given up the flesh. He hands the cruder pleasures of this world to the world's lower slaves. I die to love, wealth and show, and find them all in philosophy instead."},{"t":"sd","text":"He signs.","id":6},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I can but say their protestation over.","So much, dear liege, I have already sworn,","That is, to live and study here three years.","But there are other strict observances:","As not to see a woman in that term,","Which I hope well is not enrolled there;","And one day in a week to touch no food,","And but one meal on every day beside,","The which I hope is not enrolled there;","And then to sleep but three hours in the night,","And not be seen to wink of all the day,","When I was wont to think no harm all night,","And make a dark night too of half the day,","Which I hope well is not enrolled there.","O, these are barren tasks, too hard to keep,","Not to see ladies, study, fast, not sleep."],"id":7,"m":"I can only repeat what they've promised. I've already sworn this much, my lord: to live and study here for three years. But there are other strict rules too: not to see a woman for that time, which I very much hope isn't written down there; to eat nothing one day a week, and only one meal on every other day, which I hope isn't written down there either; and to sleep only three hours a night and never be seen to nod off during the day, when I used to sleep soundly all night and turn half the day into night as well. I hope that isn't written down there. Oh, these are empty tasks, too hard to keep: no ladies, study, fasting, no sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Your oath is passed to pass away from these."],"id":8,"m":"You swore an oath to give up those things."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Let me say no, my liege, an if you please.","I only swore to study with your Grace","And stay here in your court for three years' space."],"id":9,"m":"Let me say no to that, my lord, if you don't mind. I only swore to study with Your Grace and stay at your court for three years."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["You swore to that, Berowne, and to the rest."],"id":10,"m":"You swore to that, Berowne, and to all the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["By yea and nay, sir, then I swore in jest.","What is the end of study, let me know?"],"id":11,"m":"Then, by yes and no, sir, I swore as a joke. What is the purpose of study? Tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, that to know which else we should not know."],"id":12,"m":"Why, to know what we otherwise wouldn't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Things hid and barred, you mean, from common sense?"],"id":13,"m":"You mean things hidden and closed off from ordinary understanding?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay, that is study's god-like recompense."],"id":14,"m":"Yes, that is the god-like reward of study."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Come on, then, I will swear to study so,","To know the thing I am forbid to know:","As thus, to study where I well may dine,","When I to feast expressly am forbid;","Or study where to meet some mistress fine,","When mistresses from common sense are hid;","Or, having sworn too hard-a-keeping oath,","Study to break it, and not break my troth.","If study's gain be thus, and this be so,","Study knows that which yet it doth not know.","Swear me to this, and I will ne'er say no."],"id":15,"m":"All right then, I'll swear to study in that way, to know what I am forbidden to know. For example: to study where I can find a good dinner when I'm strictly forbidden to feast; or where to meet a lovely mistress when mistresses are hidden from ordinary view; or, having sworn an oath too hard to keep, to study how to break it without breaking my word. If that's what study gains, then study teaches what it doesn't yet know. Swear me to that and I'll never say no."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["These be the stops that hinder study quite,","And train our intellects to vain delight."],"id":16,"m":"Those are exactly the obstacles that block real study and train our minds to idle pleasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Why, all delights are vain, but that most vain","Which, with pain purchased, doth inherit pain:","As painfully to pore upon a book","To seek the light of truth, while truth the while","Doth falsely blind the eyesight of his look.","Light seeking light doth light of light beguile;","So, ere you find where light in darkness lies,","Your light grows dark by losing of your eyes.","Study me how to please the eye indeed","By fixing it upon a fairer eye,","Who dazzling so, that eye shall be his heed,","And give him light that it was blinded by.","Study is like the heaven's glorious sun,","That will not be deep-searched with saucy looks;","Small have continual plodders ever won,","Save base authority from others' books.","These earthly godfathers of heaven's lights,","That give a name to every fixed star,","Have no more profit of their shining nights","Than those that walk and wot not what they are.","Too much to know is to know naught but fame,","And every godfather can give a name."],"id":17,"m":"Why, all pleasures are empty, but the emptiest is the one bought with pain that only earns more pain: like painfully poring over a book to find the light of truth, while all that time the truth is blinding your eyes. Eyes looking for light rob themselves of light; so before you find where light lies hidden in the darkness, your own light goes dark because you've lost your eyesight. Teach me instead how to please the eye by fixing it on a lovelier eye, a woman's: even if that dazzles it, that eye will be its guide and give back the light it was blinded by. Study is like the glorious sun in the sky, which won't let impudent eyes search it deeply. Constant plodders have never gained much except second-hand authority from other people's books. Astronomers, those earthly godfathers who name every fixed star, get no more good from their starry nights than people who walk about not knowing what the stars are. Knowing too much just means knowing names, and any godfather can give a name."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How well he's read, to reason against reading."],"id":18,"m":"How learned he is, at arguing against learning!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Proceeded well, to stop all good proceeding."],"id":19,"m":"He argues well, to stop all good progress."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["He weeds the corn, and still lets grow the weeding."],"id":20,"m":"He weeds out the corn and lets the weeds keep growing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["The spring is near when green geese are a-breeding."],"id":21,"m":"Spring is coming, when baby geese are hatching."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["How follows that?"],"id":22,"m":"What does that have to do with anything?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Fit in his place and time."],"id":23,"m":"It fits its place and its time."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["In reason nothing."],"id":24,"m":"It makes no sense."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Something then in rhyme."],"id":25,"m":"Then it makes a rhyme."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Berowne is like an envious sneaping frost","That bites the first-born infants of the spring."],"id":26,"m":"Berowne is like a spiteful, nipping frost that kills the first-born buds of spring."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Well, say I am. Why should proud summer boast","Before the birds have any cause to sing?","Why should I joy in any abortive birth?","At Christmas I no more desire a rose","Than wish a snow in May's new-fangled shows,","But like of each thing that in season grows.","So you, to study now it is too late,","Climb o'er the house to unlock the little gate."],"id":27,"m":"Suppose I am. Why should proud summer show off before the birds have any reason to sing? Why should I be glad of something born too early? At Christmas I don't want a rose any more than I want snow in May's fresh blossoms; I like each thing that grows in its own season. You're starting to study too late, like climbing over the roof to unlock the little garden gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Well, sit you out. Go home, Berowne. Adieu."],"id":28,"m":"Well, stay out of it, then. Go home, Berowne. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["No, my good lord, I have sworn to stay with you,","And though I have for barbarism spoke more","Than for that angel knowledge you can say,","Yet confident I'll keep what I have sworn","And bide the penance of each three years' day.","Give me the paper, let me read the same,","And to the strictest decrees I'll write my name."],"id":29,"m":"No, my good lord, I've sworn to stay with you. And though I've spoken more for ignorance than you can say for that angel, knowledge, I'm still confident I'll keep my oath and suffer the penance of every day of the three years. Give me the paper; let me read it, and I'll sign my name to the strictest rules."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How well this yielding rescues thee from shame."],"id":30,"m":"How well your giving in saves you from disgrace."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["[Reads] Item, That no woman shall come within a mile of my court.","Hath this been proclaimed?"],"id":31,"m":"[Reads] 'Item: no woman shall come within a mile of my court.' Has this been announced?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Four days ago."],"id":32,"m":"Four days ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Let's see the penalty. [Reads] On pain of losing her tongue. Who","devised this penalty?"],"id":33,"m":"Let's see the penalty. [Reads] 'On pain of losing her tongue.' Who thought up this penalty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Marry, that did I."],"id":34,"m":"I did, indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Sweet lord, and why?"],"id":35,"m":"My dear lord, why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["To fright them hence with that dread penalty."],"id":36,"m":"To scare them away with that terrible punishment."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A dangerous law against gentility.","[Reads] Item, If any man be seen to talk with a woman within the","term of three years, he shall endure such public shame as the rest of","the court can possibly devise.","This article, my liege, yourself must break,","For well you know here comes in embassy","The French King's daughter, with yourself to speak—","A mild of grace and complete majesty—","About surrender up of Aquitaine","To her decrepit, sick, and bedrid father.","Therefore this article is made in vain,","Or vainly comes th' admired Princess hither."],"id":37,"m":"A law dangerous to good manners. [Reads] 'Item: if any man is seen talking with a woman during the three years, he shall suffer whatever public shame the rest of the court can think up.' You'll have to break this rule yourself, my lord, because you know very well that the French King's daughter is coming here as an ambassador to speak with you, a lady of gentle grace and perfect majesty, about handing over Aquitaine to her old, sick, bedridden father. So either this rule is pointless, or the admired Princess is coming here for nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What say you, lords? Why, this was quite forgot."],"id":38,"m":"What do you say, lords? Why, we completely forgot about this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["So study evermore is overshot.","While it doth study to have what it would,","It doth forget to do the thing it should;","And when it hath the thing it hunteth most,","'Tis won as towns with fire: so won, so lost."],"id":39,"m":"That's how study always overshoots its target. While it studies to get what it wants, it forgets to do what it ought to do; and when it gets the thing it was hunting most, it's like a town won by burning it: won and lost at the same moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["We must of force dispense with this decree.","She must lie here on mere necessity."],"id":40,"m":"We'll have to set this rule aside. She has to stay here; we have no choice."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Necessity will make us all forsworn","Three thousand times within this three years' space;","For every man with his affects is born,","Not by might mastered, but by special grace.","If I break faith, this word shall speak for me:","I am forsworn on mere necessity.","So to the laws at large I write my name,","And he that breaks them in the least degree","Stands in attainder of eternal shame.","Suggestions are to other as to me;","But I believe, although I seem so loath,","I am the last that will last keep his oath."],"id":41,"m":"Necessity will make us all break our oaths three thousand times in these three years. Every man is born with his passions, and they can't be mastered by force, only by God's special grace. If I break my word, this will be my defence: I broke my oath because I had to. So I sign my name to all the laws, and whoever breaks the smallest of them is condemned to eternal shame. Temptations come to others as they come to me; but I believe, though I seem so unwilling, I will be the last to keep his oath to the last."},{"t":"sd","text":"He signs.","id":42},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["But is there no quick recreation granted?"],"id":43,"m":"But is there no lively entertainment allowed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay, that there is. Our court, you know, is haunted","With a refined traveller of Spain,","A man in all the world's new fashion planted,","That hath a mint of phrases in his brain;","One who the music of his own vain tongue","Doth ravish like enchanting harmony,","A man of complements, whom right and wrong","Have chose as umpire of their mutiny.","This child of fancy, that Armado hight,","For interim to our studies shall relate","In high-born words the worth of many a knight","From tawny Spain lost in the world's debate.","How you delight, my lords, I know not, I,","But I protest I love to hear him lie,","And I will use him for my minstrelsy."],"id":44,"m":"Yes, there is. Our court, as you know, is haunted by an elegant traveller from Spain, a man dressed in all the world's newest fashions, who has a whole mint of phrases in his head; a man who is enchanted by the music of his own vain voice as if it were magic harmony; a man of such fine manners that right and wrong would choose him as referee in their quarrel. This creature of fantasy, called Armado, will entertain us between our studies by telling us, in high-flown words, about the worth of many knights from sunburnt Spain who were lost in the world's wars. I don't know what you enjoy, my lords, but I confess I love to hear him lie, and I'll use him as my entertainer."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Armado is a most illustrious wight,","A man of fire-new words, fashion's own knight."],"id":45,"m":"Armado is a most famous fellow, a man of brand-new words, fashion's own knight."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Costard the swain and he shall be our sport,","And so to study three years is but short."],"id":46,"m":"He and Costard the country lad will be our fun, and so three years of study will feel short."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dull, a Constable, with a letter, and Costard.","id":47},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["Which is the Duke's own person?"],"id":48,"m":"Which one is the Duke himself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This, fellow. What wouldst?"],"id":49,"m":"This one, fellow. What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["I myself reprehend his own person, for I am his Grace's farborough. But","I would see his own person in flesh and blood."],"id":50,"m":"I myself represent his person, for I am His Grace's constable. But I would like to see his own person in flesh and blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This is he."],"id":51,"m":"This is him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["Signior Arm… Arm… commends you. There's villainy abroad. This letter","will tell you more."],"id":52,"m":"Signior Arm... Arm... sends his regards. There's villainy about. This letter will tell you more."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Sir, the contempts thereof are as touching me."],"id":53,"m":"Sir, the contents of it concern me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["A letter from the magnificent Armado."],"id":54,"m":"A letter from the magnificent Armado."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["How long soever the matter, I hope in God for high words."],"id":55,"m":"However trivial the matter, I hope to God for high-flown words."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["A high hope for a low heaven. God grant us patience!"],"id":56,"m":"That's a lofty hope for a lowly kind of heaven. God give us patience!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["To hear, or forbear laughing?"],"id":57,"m":"Patience to listen, or to stop ourselves laughing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["To hear meekly, sir, and to laugh moderately, or to forbear both."],"id":58,"m":"To listen meekly, sir, and laugh in moderation, or to do neither."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Well, sir, be it as the style shall give us cause to climb in the","merriness."],"id":59,"m":"Well, sir, let's see whether the style gives us cause to climb up to laughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["The matter is to me, sir, as concerning Jaquenetta. The manner of it","is, I was taken with the manner."],"id":60,"m":"The matter concerns me, sir, and Jaquenetta. The way of it is, I was caught in the act."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["In what manner?"],"id":61,"m":"In what way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["In manner and form following, sir, all those three. I was seen with her","in the manor-house, sitting with her upon the form, and taken following","her into the park, which, put together, is \"in manner and form","following\". Now, sir, for the manner. It is the manner of a man to","speak to a woman. For the form—in some form."],"id":62,"m":"In the manner and form following, sir, all three of them. I was seen with her in the manor house, sitting with her on a bench, and caught following her into the park. Put together, that's 'in manner and form following'. Now, sir, as for the manner: it's the manner of a man to speak to a woman. As for the form: in some form or other."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["For the \"following\", sir?"],"id":63,"m":"And the 'following', sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["As it shall follow in my correction, and God defend the right!"],"id":64,"m":"That will follow in my punishment; and God defend the right!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Will you hear this letter with attention?"],"id":65,"m":"Will you listen carefully to this letter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["As we would hear an oracle."],"id":66,"m":"As we would listen to an oracle."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Such is the simplicity of man to hearken after the flesh."],"id":67,"m":"That's how simple man is, listening to the flesh."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] Great deputy, the welkin's vicegerent and sole dominator of","Navarre, my soul's earth's god and body's fostering patron—"],"id":68,"m":"[Reads] 'Great deputy, the sky's representative and sole ruler of Navarre, my soul's god on earth and my body's nourishing patron...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Not a word of Costard yet."],"id":69,"m":"Not a word about Costard yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] So it is—"],"id":70,"m":"[Reads] 'So it is...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["It may be so; but if he say it is so, he is, in telling true, but so."],"id":71,"m":"It may be so; but if he says it is so, he is, to tell the truth, only so-so."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Peace!"],"id":72,"m":"Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Be to me, and every man that dares not fight."],"id":73,"m":"Be quiet yourself, and so should every man who daren't fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["No words!"],"id":74,"m":"No words!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Of other men's secrets, I beseech you."],"id":75,"m":"About other men's secrets, I beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] So it is, besieged with sable-coloured melancholy, I did","commend the black-oppressing humour to the most wholesome physic of thy","health-giving air; and, as I am a gentleman, betook myself to walk. The","time when? About the sixth hour, when beasts most graze, birds best","peck, and men sit down to that nourishment which is called supper. So","much for the time when. Now for the ground which? Which, I mean, I","walked upon. It is ycleped thy park. Then for the place, where? Where,","I mean, I did encounter that obscene and most preposterous event that","draweth from my snow-white pen the ebon-coloured ink, which here thou","viewest, beholdest, surveyest, or seest. But to the place where? It","standeth north-north-east and by east from the west corner of thy","curious-knotted garden. There did I see that low-spirited swain, that","base minnow of thy mirth—"],"id":76,"m":"[Reads] 'So it is: besieged by black melancholy, I entrusted my dark, oppressive mood to the healthy medicine of your health-giving air, and, as I am a gentleman, went for a walk. The time when? About the sixth hour, when animals graze most, birds peck best and men sit down to the nourishment called supper. So much for the time when. Now for the ground which: which, I mean, I walked on. It is called your park. Then for the place where: where, I mean, I came upon that obscene and most unnatural event that draws the ebony ink from my snow-white pen, which you now view, behold, survey or see. But as for the place where: it lies north-north-east and by east from the west corner of your intricately laid-out garden. There I saw that low-spirited peasant, that lowly little fish of your amusement...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Me?"],"id":77,"m":"Me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] That unlettered small-knowing soul—"],"id":78,"m":"[Reads] 'that unlettered, ignorant soul...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Me?"],"id":79,"m":"Me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] That shallow vassal—"],"id":80,"m":"[Reads] 'that shallow servant...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Still me?"],"id":81,"m":"Still me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] Which, as I remember, hight Costard—"],"id":82,"m":"[Reads] 'who, as I recall, is called Costard...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["O me!"],"id":83,"m":"Oh, me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] Sorted and consorted, contrary to thy established","proclaimed edict and continent canon, which with, O, with—but with this","I passion to say wherewith—"],"id":84,"m":"[Reads] 'keep company and consort, against your announced decree and law of chastity, with... oh, with... but it pains me to say with what...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["With a wench."],"id":85,"m":"With a girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] With a child of our grandmother Eve, a female; or, for thy","more sweet understanding, a woman. Him, I, as my ever-esteemed duty","pricks me on, have sent to thee, to receive the meed of punishment, by","thy sweet Grace's officer, Antony Dull, a man of good repute, carriage,","bearing, and estimation."],"id":86,"m":"[Reads] 'with a child of our grandmother Eve, a female, or, for your sweeter understanding, a woman. As my ever-honoured duty urges me, I have sent him to you to receive his due punishment, in the charge of Your Grace's officer, Antony Dull, a man of good reputation, conduct, bearing and standing.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["Me, an't shall please you; I am Antony Dull."],"id":87,"m":"That's me, if you please; I am Antony Dull."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] For Jaquenetta, so is the weaker vessel called which I","apprehended with the aforesaid swain, I keep her as a vessel of thy","law's fury, and shall, at the least of thy sweet notice, bring her to","trial. Thine, in all compliments of devoted and heartburning heat of","duty,","Don Adriano de Armado."],"id":88,"m":"[Reads] 'As for Jaquenetta (that is what the weaker vessel is called whom I caught with the aforesaid peasant), I am keeping her as a vessel for your law's fury, and will bring her to trial at your least command. Yours, in all the courtesies of devoted and heart-burning heat of duty, Don Adriano de Armado.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This is not so well as I looked for, but the best that ever I heard."],"id":89,"m":"That's not as good as I hoped, but it's the best I ever heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay, the best for the worst. But, sirrah, what say you to this?"],"id":90,"m":"Yes, the best of the worst. But, fellow, what do you say to this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Sir, I confess the wench."],"id":91,"m":"Sir, I confess to the girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Did you hear the proclamation?"],"id":92,"m":"Did you hear the announcement?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I do confess much of the hearing it, but little of the marking of it."],"id":93,"m":"I confess I heard a lot of it, but paid little attention to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["It was proclaimed a year's imprisonment to be taken with a wench."],"id":94,"m":"It announced a year in prison for anyone caught with a wench."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I was taken with none, sir. I was taken with a damsel."],"id":95,"m":"I wasn't caught with a wench, sir. I was caught with a damsel."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Well, it was proclaimed \"damsel\"."],"id":96,"m":"Well, it said 'damsel' too."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["This was no damsel neither, sir; she was a virgin."],"id":97,"m":"She wasn't a damsel either, sir; she was a virgin."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["It is so varied too, for it was proclaimed \"virgin\"."],"id":98,"m":"That's covered too; it said 'virgin'."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["If it were, I deny her virginity. I was taken with a maid."],"id":99,"m":"If it did, I deny she's a virgin. I was caught with a maid."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["This maid will not serve your turn, sir."],"id":100,"m":"This 'maid' won't help you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["This maid will serve my turn, sir."],"id":101,"m":"This maid will help me fine, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Sir, I will pronounce your sentence: you shall fast a week with bran","and water."],"id":102,"m":"Sir, I'll pronounce your sentence: you will fast for a week on bran and water."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I had rather pray a month with mutton and porridge."],"id":103,"m":"I'd rather pray for a month on mutton and soup."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And Don Armado shall be your keeper.","My Lord Berowne, see him delivered o'er;","And go we, lords, to put in practice that","Which each to other hath so strongly sworn."],"id":104,"m":"And Don Armado will be your jailer. My Lord Berowne, see that he's handed over. And let's go, lords, and put into practice what we've each sworn to the others so firmly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King, Longaville and Dumaine.","id":105},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I'll lay my head to any good man's hat","These oaths and laws will prove an idle scorn.","Sirrah, come on."],"id":106,"m":"I'll bet my head against any good man's hat that these oaths and laws will turn out to be an empty joke. Come on, fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I suffer for the truth, sir; for true it is I was taken with","Jaquenetta, and Jaquenetta is a true girl. And therefore welcome the","sour cup of prosperity! Affliction may one day smile again, and till","then, sit thee down, sorrow."],"id":107,"m":"I suffer for the truth, sir; for it's true I was caught with Jaquenetta, and Jaquenetta is a true girl. So welcome, sour cup of prosperity! Affliction may smile on me again one day; and till then, sit down, sorrow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":108},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The park.","id":109},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Armado and Moth, his Page.","id":110},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Boy, what sign is it when a man of great spirit grows melancholy?"],"id":111,"m":"Boy, what does it mean when a man of great spirit grows melancholy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A great sign, sir, that he will look sad."],"id":112,"m":"It's a great sign, sir, that he'll look sad."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Why, sadness is one and the selfsame thing, dear imp."],"id":113,"m":"Why, sadness and melancholy are one and the same thing, dear imp."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["No, no, O Lord, sir, no."],"id":114,"m":"No, no, oh Lord, sir, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["How canst thou part sadness and melancholy, my tender juvenal?"],"id":115,"m":"How can you separate sadness from melancholy, my tender youth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["By a familiar demonstration of the working, my tough signior."],"id":116,"m":"By a simple demonstration of how they work, my tough sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Why tough signior? Why tough signior?"],"id":117,"m":"Why 'tough sir'? Why 'tough sir'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Why tender juvenal? Why tender juvenal?"],"id":118,"m":"Why 'tender youth'? Why 'tender youth'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I spoke it, tender juvenal, as a congruent epitheton appertaining to","thy young days, which we may nominate tender."],"id":119,"m":"I said it, tender youth, as a fitting description belonging to your young days, which we may call tender."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["And I, tough signior, as an appertinent title to your old time, which","we may name tough."],"id":120,"m":"And I said it, tough sir, as a suitable title for your old age, which we may call tough."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Pretty and apt."],"id":121,"m":"Pretty and apt."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["How mean you, sir? I pretty and my saying apt, or I apt, and my saying","pretty?"],"id":122,"m":"What do you mean, sir? That I'm pretty and my saying is apt, or that I'm apt and my saying is pretty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Thou pretty, because little."],"id":123,"m":"You're pretty, because you're little."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Little pretty, because little. Wherefore apt?"],"id":124,"m":"A little bit pretty, because little. And why apt?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["And therefore apt, because quick."],"id":125,"m":"And apt because you're quick."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Speak you this in my praise, master?"],"id":126,"m":"Do you mean this as praise, master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["In thy condign praise."],"id":127,"m":"As your well-deserved praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["I will praise an eel with the same praise."],"id":128,"m":"I could praise an eel the same way."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["What, that an eel is ingenious?"],"id":129,"m":"What, that an eel is clever?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["That an eel is quick."],"id":130,"m":"That an eel is quick."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I do say thou art quick in answers. Thou heat'st my blood."],"id":131,"m":"I'm saying you're quick with answers. You're making me angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["I am answered, sir."],"id":132,"m":"I have my answer, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I love not to be crossed."],"id":133,"m":"I don't like to be crossed."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["[Aside] He speaks the mere contrary; crosses love not him."],"id":134,"m":"[Aside] He's got it backwards: crosses don't like him. (Crosses were coins stamped with a cross; he's broke.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I have promised to study three years with the Duke."],"id":135,"m":"I've promised to study for three years with the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["You may do it in an hour, sir."],"id":136,"m":"You could do it in an hour, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Impossible."],"id":137,"m":"Impossible."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["How many is one thrice told?"],"id":138,"m":"How much is one three times over?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I am ill at reckoning. It fitteth the spirit of a tapster."],"id":139,"m":"I'm bad at arithmetic. It suits the mind of a barman."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["You are a gentleman and a gamester, sir."],"id":140,"m":"You're a gentleman and a gambler, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I confess both. They are both the varnish of a complete man."],"id":141,"m":"I admit to both. They are both the polish of a complete man."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Then I am sure you know how much the gross sum of deuce-ace amounts to."],"id":142,"m":"Then I'm sure you know how much a two and a one on the dice add up to."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["It doth amount to one more than two."],"id":143,"m":"They come to one more than two."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Which the base vulgar do call three."],"id":144,"m":"Which the common people call three."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["True."],"id":145,"m":"True."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Why, sir, is this such a piece of study? Now here's three studied ere","ye'll thrice wink. And how easy it is to put \"years\" to the word","\"three\", and study three years in two words, the dancing horse will","tell you."],"id":146,"m":"Well, sir, is that such a hard piece of study? There, you've studied 'three' before you can blink three times. And how easy it is to add 'years' to 'three', and study three years in two words, the performing horse could tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["A most fine figure!"],"id":147,"m":"A very fine figure of speech!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["[Aside] To prove you a cipher."],"id":148,"m":"[Aside] To prove you a zero."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I will hereupon confess I am in love; and as it is base for a soldier","to love, so am I in love with a base wench. If drawing my sword against","the humour of affection would deliver me from the reprobate thought of","it, I would take desire prisoner, and ransom him to any French courtier","for a new-devised curtsy. I think scorn to sigh; methinks I should","outswear Cupid. Comfort me, boy. What great men have been in love?"],"id":149,"m":"I'll confess, then, that I am in love; and since it is low for a soldier to love, I am in love with a low-born girl. If drawing my sword against the passion of love would free me from the sinful thought of it, I would take desire prisoner and sell him to any French courtier in exchange for a newly invented bow. I scorn to sigh; I think I could out-swear Cupid himself. Comfort me, boy. Which great men have been in love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Hercules, master."],"id":150,"m":"Hercules, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Most sweet Hercules! More authority, dear boy, name more; and, sweet my","child, let them be men of good repute and carriage."],"id":151,"m":"Most sweet Hercules! More examples, dear boy, name more; and, my sweet child, let them be men of good reputation and carriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Samson, master. He was a man of good carriage, great carriage, for he","carried the town gates on his back like a porter, and he was in love."],"id":152,"m":"Samson, master. He was a man of good carriage, great carriage, because he carried the town gates on his back like a porter; and he was in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["O well-knit Samson, strong-jointed Samson! I do excel thee in my rapier","as much as thou didst me in carrying gates. I am in love too. Who was","Samson's love, my dear Moth?"],"id":153,"m":"O well-built Samson, strong-limbed Samson! I beat you with my rapier as much as you beat me at carrying gates. I'm in love too. Who was Samson's love, my dear Moth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A woman, master."],"id":154,"m":"A woman, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Of what complexion?"],"id":155,"m":"What was her complexion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Of all the four, or the three, or the two, or one of the four."],"id":156,"m":"All four of them, or three, or two, or one of the four."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Tell me precisely of what complexion."],"id":157,"m":"Tell me exactly what her complexion was."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Of the sea-water green, sir."],"id":158,"m":"Sea-water green, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Is that one of the four complexions?"],"id":159,"m":"Is that one of the four complexions?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["As I have read, sir; and the best of them too."],"id":160,"m":"So I've read, sir; and the best of them, too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Green indeed is the colour of lovers. But to have a love of that","colour, methinks Samson had small reason for it. He surely affected her","for her wit."],"id":161,"m":"Green is certainly the colour of lovers. But I think Samson had little reason to love a woman that colour. Surely he loved her for her wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["It was so, sir, for she had a green wit."],"id":162,"m":"He did, sir, because she had a green wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["My love is most immaculate white and red."],"id":163,"m":"My love is the most spotless white and red."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Most maculate thoughts, master, are masked under such colours."],"id":164,"m":"The most spotted thoughts, master, hide behind those colours."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Define, define, well-educated infant."],"id":165,"m":"Explain, explain, well-educated infant."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["My father's wit and my mother's tongue assist me!"],"id":166,"m":"May my father's wit and my mother's tongue help me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sweet invocation of a child, most pretty, and pathetical!"],"id":167,"m":"A child's sweet prayer, very pretty and moving!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["If she be made of white and red,","Her faults will ne'er be known;","For blushing cheeks by faults are bred,","And fears by pale white shown.","Then if she fear, or be to blame,","By this you shall not know,","For still her cheeks possess the same","Which native she doth owe.","A dangerous rhyme, master, against the reason of white and red."],"id":168,"m":"If she is made of white and red, you'll never know her faults; because faults make cheeks blush red, and fear makes them pale white. So if she is afraid or guilty, you won't be able to tell from this, because her cheeks always keep the colours she was born with. A dangerous rhyme, master, against the case for white and red."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Is there not a ballad, boy, of the King and the Beggar?"],"id":169,"m":"Isn't there a ballad, boy, about the King and the Beggar girl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["The world was very guilty of such a ballad some three ages since, but I","think now 'tis not to be found; or if it were, it would neither serve","for the writing nor the tune."],"id":170,"m":"The world was guilty of a ballad like that about three generations ago, but I don't think it can be found now; or if it could, it would be no good for either the words or the tune."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I will have that subject newly writ o'er, that I may example my","digression by some mighty precedent. Boy, I do love that country girl","that I took in the park with the rational hind Costard. She deserves","well."],"id":171,"m":"I'll have that subject written again, so that I can excuse my lapse with a great example. Boy, I love that country girl I caught in the park with that reasoning animal Costard. She deserves well."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["[Aside] To be whipped: and yet a better love than my master."],"id":172,"m":"[Aside] She deserves to be whipped; and yet she's a better lover than my master deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sing, boy. My spirit grows heavy in love."],"id":173,"m":"Sing, boy. My spirit grows heavy with love."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["And that's great marvel, loving a light wench."],"id":174,"m":"And that's a great wonder, loving a light woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I say, sing."],"id":175,"m":"I said, sing."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Forbear till this company be past."],"id":176,"m":"Wait until these people have gone past."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Costard the Clown, Dull the Constable and Jaquenetta a Wench.","id":177},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["Sir, the Duke's pleasure is that you keep Costard safe; and you must","suffer him to take no delight, nor no penance, but he must fast three","days a week. For this damsel, I must keep her at the park. She is","allowed for the dey-woman. Fare you well."],"id":178,"m":"Sir, the Duke wants you to keep Costard securely; and you mustn't let him have any pleasure, nor any penance either, except that he must fast three days a week. As for this damsel, I have to keep her at the park. She's employed as the dairymaid. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I do betray myself with blushing.—Maid."],"id":179,"m":"My blushes are giving me away. Maid."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Man."],"id":180,"m":"Man."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I will visit thee at the lodge."],"id":181,"m":"I will visit you at the lodge."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["That's hereby."],"id":182,"m":"It's near here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I know where it is situate."],"id":183,"m":"I know where it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Lord, how wise you are!"],"id":184,"m":"Lord, how clever you are!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I will tell thee wonders."],"id":185,"m":"I will tell you wonders."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["With that face?"],"id":186,"m":"With that face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I love thee."],"id":187,"m":"I love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["So I heard you say."],"id":188,"m":"So I heard you say."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["And so, farewell."],"id":189,"m":"And so, farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Fair weather after you!"],"id":190,"m":"Good riddance to you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["Come, Jaquenetta, away."],"id":191,"m":"Come on, Jaquenetta, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Dull and Jaquenetta.","id":192},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Villain, thou shalt fast for thy offences ere thou be pardoned."],"id":193,"m":"Villain, you will fast for your crimes before you are pardoned."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Well, sir, I hope when I do it I shall do it on a full stomach."],"id":194,"m":"Well, sir, I hope when I fast I'll do it on a full stomach."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Thou shalt be heavily punished."],"id":195,"m":"You will be heavily punished."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I am more bound to you than your fellows, for they are but lightly","rewarded."],"id":196,"m":"Then I owe you more than your servants do, because they're only lightly rewarded."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Take away this villain. Shut him up."],"id":197,"m":"Take this villain away. Lock him up."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Come, you transgressing slave, away!"],"id":198,"m":"Come on, you lawbreaking slave, away with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Let me not be pent up, sir. I will fast being loose."],"id":199,"m":"Don't let me be shut up, sir. I'll fast while I'm loose."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["No, sir, that were fast and loose. Thou shalt to prison."],"id":200,"m":"No, sir, that would be 'fast and loose' (a cheat's trick). You're going to prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Well, if ever I do see the merry days of desolation that I have seen,","some shall see."],"id":201,"m":"Well, if I ever see the merry days of misery I've seen before, some people will see."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["What shall some see?"],"id":202,"m":"What will some people see?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Nay, nothing, Master Moth, but what they look upon. It is not for","prisoners to be too silent in their words, and therefore I will say","nothing. I thank God I have as little patience as another man, and","therefore I can be quiet."],"id":203,"m":"Nothing, Master Moth, except what they look at. It's not right for prisoners to be too silent in their words, so I'll say nothing. Thank God I have as little patience as the next man, so I can keep quiet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Moth and Costard.","id":204},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I do affect the very ground, which is base, where her shoe, which is","baser, guided by her foot, which is basest, doth tread. I shall be","forsworn, which is a great argument of falsehood, if I love. And how","can that be true love which is falsely attempted? Love is a familiar;","Love is a devil. There is no evil angel but Love. Yet was Samson so","tempted, and he had an excellent strength; yet was Solomon so seduced,","and he had a very good wit. Cupid's butt-shaft is too hard for","Hercules' club, and therefore too much odds for a Spaniard's rapier.","The first and second cause will not serve my turn; the passado he","respects not, the duello he regards not. His disgrace is to be called","boy, but his glory is to subdue men. Adieu, valour; rust, rapier; be","still, drum, for your manager is in love. Yea, he loveth. Assist me,","some extemporal god of rhyme, for I am sure I shall turn sonnet.","Devise, wit; write, pen; for I am for whole volumes in folio."],"id":205,"m":"I love the very ground, which is lowly, where her shoe, which is lowlier, guided by her foot, which is lowliest, steps. If I love, I'll break my oath, which is strong evidence of falseness. And how can a love be true that is begun falsely? Love is an evil spirit; Love is a devil. There is no bad angel except Love. Yet Samson was tempted like this, and he was extremely strong; Solomon was seduced like this, and he was very wise. Cupid's blunt arrow is too hard for Hercules' club, so it is far too much for a Spaniard's rapier. The rules of duelling won't help me: Cupid doesn't care for the forward thrust or the formal duel. It shames him to be called a boy, but his glory is to conquer men. Farewell, courage; rust, rapier; be quiet, drum, for your master is in love. Yes, he loves. Help me, some god of improvised rhyme, for I'm sure I'll turn into a sonnet. Invent, wit; write, pen; for I'm good for whole volumes in folio."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":206},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"The King of Navarre's park. A pavilion and tents at a distance.","id":207},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Princess of France, with three attending Ladies: Rosaline, Maria, Katharine and three Lords: Boyet, and two others.","id":208},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Now, madam, summon up your dearest spirits.","Consider who the King your father sends,","To whom he sends, and what's his embassy.","Yourself, held precious in the world's esteem,","To parley with the sole inheritor","Of all perfections that a man may owe,","Matchless Navarre; the plea of no less weight","Than Aquitaine, a dowry for a queen.","Be now as prodigal of all dear grace","As Nature was in making graces dear","When she did starve the general world beside","And prodigally gave them all to you."],"id":209,"m":"Now, madam, gather your finest powers. Think who the King your father is sending, to whom he is sending her, and what her mission is: you yourself, prized by the whole world, to negotiate with the sole heir of every perfection a man can have, the matchless King of Navarre; and the matter is nothing less than Aquitaine, a dowry fit for a queen. So be as lavish now with all your precious charm as Nature was when she made such charms precious by starving the rest of the world and giving them all to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Good Lord Boyet, my beauty, though but mean,","Needs not the painted flourish of your praise.","Beauty is bought by judgement of the eye,","Not uttered by base sale of chapmen's tongues.","I am less proud to hear you tell my worth","Than you much willing to be counted wise","In spending your wit in the praise of mine.","But now to task the tasker: good Boyet,","You are not ignorant, all-telling fame","Doth noise abroad Navarre hath made a vow,","Till painful study shall outwear three years,","No woman may approach his silent court.","Therefore to's seemeth it a needful course,","Before we enter his forbidden gates,","To know his pleasure; and in that behalf,","Bold of your worthiness, we single you","As our best-moving fair solicitor.","Tell him the daughter of the King of France,","On serious business craving quick dispatch,","Importunes personal conference with his Grace.","Haste, signify so much, while we attend,","Like humble-visaged suitors, his high will."],"id":210,"m":"Good Lord Boyet, my beauty, ordinary as it is, doesn't need the painted decoration of your praise. Beauty is judged by the eye that sees it, not sold off by the cheap patter of pedlars. I'm less proud to hear you tell my worth than you are eager to be thought clever for spending your wit on praising mine. But now to set the task-setter a task. Good Boyet, you know that rumour, which tells everything, is spreading the news that Navarre has made a vow: no woman may come near his silent court until three years of painful study are over. So it seems necessary to us, before we go through his forbidden gates, to find out what he wants; and for that, trusting your worth, we choose you as our most persuasive advocate. Tell him the daughter of the King of France, on serious business that needs a quick settlement, asks to speak with His Grace in person. Hurry and tell him, while we wait for his royal answer like humble petitioners."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Proud of employment, willingly I go."],"id":211,"m":"Proud to be employed, I go willingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["All pride is willing pride, and yours is so."],"id":212,"m":"All pride is willing pride, and yours is that kind."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Boyet.","id":213},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Who are the votaries, my loving lords,","That are vow-fellows with this virtuous Duke?"],"id":214,"m":"Who are the men who have taken the vow, my dear lords, along with this virtuous Duke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Lord Longaville is one."],"id":215,"m":"Lord Longaville is one."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Know you the man?"],"id":216,"m":"Do you know the man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["I know him, madam. At a marriage feast","Between Lord Perigort and the beauteous heir","Of Jaques Falconbridge, solemnized","In Normandy, saw I this Longaville.","A man of sovereign parts, he is esteemed,","Well fitted in arts, glorious in arms.","Nothing becomes him ill that he would well.","The only soil of his fair virtue's gloss,","If virtue's gloss will stain with any soil,","Is a sharp wit matched with too blunt a will,","Whose edge hath power to cut, whose will still wills","It should none spare that come within his power."],"id":217,"m":"I know him, madam. I saw this Longaville in Normandy, at the wedding feast of Lord Perigort and the beautiful heiress of Jaques Falconbridge. He is thought a man of outstanding gifts, well trained in learning, glorious in battle. Anything he wants to do well suits him. The only stain on the shine of his fine qualities, if such a shine can be stained at all, is a sharp wit joined to a will too blunt: his wit has the power to cut, and his will always wants it to spare no one who comes within its reach."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Some merry mocking lord, belike. Is't so?"],"id":218,"m":"Some merry, mocking lord, I suppose. Is that right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["They say so most that most his humours know."],"id":219,"m":"Those who know his moods best say so most."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Such short-lived wits do wither as they grow.","Who are the rest?"],"id":220,"m":"Wits like that, short-lived, wither as they grow. Who are the others?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["The young Dumaine, a well-accomplished youth,","Of all that virtue love for virtue loved;","Most power to do most harm, least knowing ill,","For he hath wit to make an ill shape good,","And shape to win grace though he had no wit.","I saw him at the Duke Alençon's once;","And much too little of that good I saw","Is my report to his great worthiness."],"id":221,"m":"The young Dumaine, a very accomplished young man, loved for his virtue by everyone who loves virtue. He has most power to do harm and knows least about evil, for he has the wit to make a bad shape look good, and the looks to win favour even if he had no wit. I saw him once at the Duke of Alençon's; and what I say about his great worth is much less than the good I saw."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Another of these students at that time","Was there with him, if I have heard a truth.","Berowne they call him, but a merrier man,","Within the limit of becoming mirth,","I never spent an hour's talk withal.","His eye begets occasion for his wit,","For every object that the one doth catch","The other turns to a mirth-moving jest,","Which his fair tongue, conceit's expositor,","Delivers in such apt and gracious words","That aged ears play truant at his tales,","And younger hearings are quite ravished,","So sweet and voluble is his discourse."],"id":222,"m":"Another of these students was there with him at the time, if what I've heard is true. They call him Berowne; but I never spent an hour talking with a merrier man, within the limits of decent fun. His eye finds the chances for his wit: everything the eye catches, the wit turns into a laughing joke, which his fine tongue, the interpreter of his ideas, delivers in such fitting and graceful words that old men play truant from their business to hear his stories, and young listeners are completely carried away, his talk is so sweet and fluent."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["God bless my ladies! Are they all in love,","That every one her own hath garnished","With such bedecking ornaments of praise?"],"id":223,"m":"God bless my ladies! Are they all in love, that each one has dressed up her own man with such decorations of praise?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Here comes Boyet."],"id":224,"m":"Here comes Boyet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Boyet.","id":225},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Now, what admittance, lord?"],"id":226,"m":"Now, what welcome, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Navarre had notice of your fair approach,","And he and his competitors in oath","Were all addressed to meet you, gentle lady,","Before I came. Marry, thus much I have learned:","He rather means to lodge you in the field,","Like one that comes here to besiege his court,","Than seek a dispensation for his oath,","To let you enter his unpeopled house."],"id":227,"m":"Navarre had heard of your arrival, and he and his partners in the oath were all ready to meet you, gentle lady, before I got there. But this much I've learned: he means to lodge you out in the open, like someone who has come to besiege his court, rather than seek release from his oath to let you into his empty house."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King of Navarre, Longaville, Dumaine, Berowne and Attendants.","id":228},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Here comes Navarre."],"id":229,"m":"Here comes Navarre."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Fair Princess, welcome to the court of Navarre."],"id":230,"m":"Fair Princess, welcome to the court of Navarre."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["\"Fair\" I give you back again, and \"welcome\" I have not yet. The roof of","this court is too high to be yours, and welcome to the wide fields too","base to be mine."],"id":231,"m":"I give you back 'fair'; and as for 'welcome', I haven't had any yet. The roof of this court is too high to be yours, and a welcome in the open fields is too lowly to be mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["You shall be welcome, madam, to my court."],"id":232,"m":"You'll be welcome to my court, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["I will be welcome then. Conduct me thither."],"id":233,"m":"Then I'll be welcome. Take me there."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Hear me, dear lady. I have sworn an oath."],"id":234,"m":"Listen to me, dear lady. I have sworn an oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Our Lady help my lord! He'll be forsworn."],"id":235,"m":"Our Lady help my lord! He's going to break it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Not for the world, fair madam, by my will."],"id":236,"m":"Not for the world, fair madam, if I can help it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Why, will shall break it; will, and nothing else."],"id":237,"m":"Why, desire will break it; desire, and nothing else."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Your ladyship is ignorant what it is."],"id":238,"m":"Your ladyship doesn't know what the oath is."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Were my lord so, his ignorance were wise,","Where now his knowledge must prove ignorance.","I hear your Grace hath sworn out housekeeping.","'Tis deadly sin to keep that oath, my lord,","And sin to break it.","But pardon me, I am too sudden bold.","To teach a teacher ill beseemeth me.","Vouchsafe to read the purpose of my coming,","And suddenly resolve me in my suit."],"id":239,"m":"If my lord didn't know either, his ignorance would be wise, whereas now his knowledge turns out to be ignorance. I hear Your Grace has sworn to give up hospitality. It's a deadly sin to keep that oath, my lord, and a sin to break it. But forgive me, I'm too suddenly bold; it isn't right for me to teach a teacher. Please read the reason for my visit, and answer my request quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"She gives him a paper.","id":240},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Madam, I will, if suddenly I may."],"id":241,"m":"Madam, I will, if I can do it quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["You will the sooner that I were away,","For you'll prove perjured if you make me stay."],"id":242,"m":"You'll be the quicker to get me gone, because you'll break your oath if you make me stay."},{"t":"sd","text":"The King reads the paper.","id":243},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["[To Rosaline] Did not I dance with you in Brabant once?"],"id":244,"m":"[To Rosaline] Didn't I dance with you in Brabant once?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Did not I dance with you in Brabant once?"],"id":245,"m":"Didn't I dance with you in Brabant once?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I know you did."],"id":246,"m":"I know you did."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["How needless was it then","To ask the question!"],"id":247,"m":"Then how pointless it was to ask the question!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["You must not be so quick."],"id":248,"m":"You mustn't be so sharp."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["'Tis long of you that spur me with such questions."],"id":249,"m":"It's your fault, for spurring me on with such questions."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Your wit's too hot, it speeds too fast, 'twill tire."],"id":250,"m":"Your wit is too hot; it gallops too fast; it will tire."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Not till it leave the rider in the mire."],"id":251,"m":"Not before it throws its rider in the mud."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["What time o' day?"],"id":252,"m":"What time of day is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["The hour that fools should ask."],"id":253,"m":"The hour when fools ask questions."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Now fair befall your mask."],"id":254,"m":"Good luck to your mask, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Fair fall the face it covers."],"id":255,"m":"And good luck to the face it covers."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["And send you many lovers!"],"id":256,"m":"And may it send you many lovers!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Amen, so you be none."],"id":257,"m":"Amen, as long as you're not one of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Nay, then will I be gone."],"id":258,"m":"Well then, I'll go."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Madam, your father here doth intimate","The payment of a hundred thousand crowns,","Being but the one half of an entire sum","Disbursed by my father in his wars.","But say that he or we, as neither have,","Received that sum, yet there remains unpaid","A hundred thousand more, in surety of the which","One part of Aquitaine is bound to us,","Although not valued to the money's worth.","If then the King your father will restore","But that one half which is unsatisfied,","We will give up our right in Aquitaine,","And hold fair friendship with his majesty.","But that, it seems, he little purposeth;","For here he doth demand to have repaid","A hundred thousand crowns, and not demands,","On payment of a hundred thousand crowns,","To have his title live in Aquitaine,","Which we much rather had depart withal,","And have the money by our father lent,","Than Aquitaine, so gelded as it is.","Dear Princess, were not his requests so far","From reason's yielding, your fair self should make","A yielding 'gainst some reason in my breast,","And go well satisfied to France again."],"id":259,"m":"Madam, your father mentions here the payment of a hundred thousand crowns, which is only half of the whole sum my father spent on his wars. But suppose he or we had received that sum, though neither of us has: there would still be another hundred thousand unpaid, and as security for that, part of Aquitaine is pledged to us, although it isn't worth the money. So if the King your father will repay just that one unpaid half, we'll give up our right to Aquitaine and keep good friendship with His Majesty. But it seems that is not what he intends: here he demands to be repaid a hundred thousand crowns, rather than offering to pay a hundred thousand crowns to get his claim to Aquitaine back. We would much rather part with Aquitaine, stripped of value as it is, and have the money our father lent. Dear Princess, if his demands weren't so far from reasonable, your lovely self would make something in my heart give way against reason, and you'd go back to France well satisfied."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["You do the King my father too much wrong,","And wrong the reputation of your name,","In so unseeming to confess receipt","Of that which hath so faithfully been paid."],"id":260,"m":"You do my father the King a great wrong, and damage the good name of your own, by pretending not to have received what has been so faithfully paid."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I do protest I never heard of it;","And, if you prove it, I'll repay it back","Or yield up Aquitaine."],"id":261,"m":"I swear I never heard of it; and if you prove it, I'll pay it back or give up Aquitaine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["We arrest your word.","Boyet, you can produce acquittances","For such a sum from special officers","Of Charles his father."],"id":262,"m":"We hold you to your word. Boyet, you can produce receipts for that sum from the officials of his father, Charles."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Satisfy me so."],"id":263,"m":"Then prove it to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["So please your Grace, the packet is not come","Where that and other specialties are bound.","Tomorrow you shall have a sight of them."],"id":264,"m":"If it please Your Grace, the package containing that and other documents hasn't arrived. Tomorrow you'll see them."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["It shall suffice me; at which interview","All liberal reason I will yield unto.","Meantime receive such welcome at my hand","As honour, without breach of honour, may","Make tender of to thy true worthiness.","You may not come, fair Princess, in my gates,","But here without you shall be so received","As you shall deem yourself lodged in my heart,","Though so denied fair harbour in my house.","Your own good thoughts excuse me, and farewell.","Tomorrow shall we visit you again."],"id":265,"m":"That will be good enough for me, and at that meeting I'll agree to any fair reasoning. In the meantime, accept such welcome from me as honour can offer to your true worth without breaking honour. You may not come inside my gates, fair Princess, but out here you'll be received so well that you'll feel you're lodged in my heart, though you're refused good lodging in my house. Your own kind thoughts will excuse me. Goodbye; we'll visit you again tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Sweet health and fair desires consort your Grace."],"id":266,"m":"Good health and happy wishes go with Your Grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thy own wish wish I thee in every place."],"id":267,"m":"I wish you the same everywhere you go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt the King, Longaville and Dumaine.","id":268},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Lady, I will commend you to mine own heart."],"id":269,"m":"Lady, I'll commend you to my own heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Pray you, do my commendations; I would be glad to see it."],"id":270,"m":"Please do give it my regards; I'd be glad to see it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I would you heard it groan."],"id":271,"m":"I wish you could hear it groan."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Is the fool sick?"],"id":272,"m":"Is the fool sick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Sick at the heart."],"id":273,"m":"Sick at heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Alack, let it blood."],"id":274,"m":"Oh dear, bleed it, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Would that do it good?"],"id":275,"m":"Would that do it good?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["My physic says \"ay\"."],"id":276,"m":"My medical knowledge says yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Will you prick't with your eye?"],"id":277,"m":"Will you pierce it with your eye?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Non point, with my knife."],"id":278,"m":"Not at all; with my knife."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Now, God save thy life."],"id":279,"m":"Well, God keep you alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["And yours from long living."],"id":280,"m":"And keep you from living long."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I cannot stay thanksgiving."],"id":281,"m":"I can't stay to thank you for that."},{"t":"sd","text":"He exits.","id":282},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dumaine.","id":283},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Sir, I pray you, a word. What lady is that same?"],"id":284,"m":"Sir, a word, please. Who is that lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["The heir of Alençon, Katharine her name."],"id":285,"m":"The heiress of Alençon; her name is Katharine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["A gallant lady. Monsieur, fare you well."],"id":286,"m":"A fine lady. Goodbye, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"He exits.","id":287},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Longaville.","id":288},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["I beseech you a word. What is she in the white?"],"id":289,"m":"A word, please. Who is she in white?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["A woman sometimes, an you saw her in the light."],"id":290,"m":"A woman, sometimes, if you saw her in the light."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Perchance light in the light. I desire her name."],"id":291,"m":"Perhaps light in the light (loose in her morals). I want her name."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["She hath but one for herself; to desire that were a shame."],"id":292,"m":"She has only one for herself; it would be shameful to want that."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Pray you, sir, whose daughter?"],"id":293,"m":"Please, sir, whose daughter is she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Her mother's, I have heard."],"id":294,"m":"Her mother's, I've heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["God's blessing on your beard!"],"id":295,"m":"God bless your beard! (that is, curse your cheek!)"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Good sir, be not offended.","She is an heir of Falconbridge."],"id":296,"m":"Good sir, don't be offended. She is an heiress of the Falconbridges."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Nay, my choler is ended.","She is a most sweet lady."],"id":297,"m":"Well, my anger is over. She is a very sweet lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Not unlike, sir; that may be."],"id":298,"m":"Quite likely, sir; that may be."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Longaville.","id":299},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Berowne.","id":300},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["What's her name in the cap?"],"id":301,"m":"What's the name of the one in the cap?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Rosaline, by good hap."],"id":302,"m":"Rosaline, as luck has it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Is she wedded or no?"],"id":303,"m":"Is she married or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["To her will, sir, or so."],"id":304,"m":"To her own will, sir, more or less."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["You are welcome, sir. Adieu."],"id":305,"m":"Thank you, sir. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Farewell to me, sir, and welcome to you."],"id":306,"m":"Goodbye to me, sir, and welcome to you (good riddance to you)."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Berowne.","id":307},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["That last is Berowne, the merry madcap lord.","Not a word with him but a jest."],"id":308,"m":"That last one is Berowne, the merry, madcap lord. Every word he says is a joke."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["And every jest but a word."],"id":309,"m":"And every joke is only a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["It was well done of you to take him at his word."],"id":310,"m":"You did well to take him up on his words."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["I was as willing to grapple as he was to board."],"id":311,"m":"I was as ready to grapple with him as he was to board me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Two hot sheeps, marry!"],"id":312,"m":"Two hot sheep, indeed!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["And wherefore not ships?","No sheep, sweet lamb, unless we feed on your lips."],"id":313,"m":"And why not ships? We're not sheep, sweet lamb, unless we feed on your lips."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["You sheep and I pasture. Shall that finish the jest?"],"id":314,"m":"You the sheep and I the pasture? Will that end the joke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["So you grant pasture for me."],"id":315,"m":"As long as you give me grazing."},{"t":"sd","text":"He tries to kiss her.","id":316},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Not so, gentle beast.","My lips are no common, though several they be."],"id":317,"m":"Not so, gentle beast. My lips are not common land, though they are two (and private)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Belonging to whom?"],"id":318,"m":"Belonging to whom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["To my fortunes and me."],"id":319,"m":"To my future and to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Good wits will be jangling; but, gentles, agree.","This civil war of wits were much better used","On Navarre and his bookmen, for here 'tis abused."],"id":320,"m":"Good wits will squabble; but, my friends, make peace. This civil war of wits would be much better used on Navarre and his bookworms; here it's wasted."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["If my observation, which very seldom lies,","By the heart's still rhetoric disclosed with eyes,","Deceive me not now, Navarre is infected."],"id":321,"m":"If my observation, which very rarely lies, isn't fooling me now about the silent language of the heart that shows through the eyes, Navarre is infected."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["With what?"],"id":322,"m":"With what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["With that which we lovers entitle \"affected\"."],"id":323,"m":"With what we lovers call 'being in love'."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Your reason."],"id":324,"m":"Your reasons?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Why, all his behaviours did make their retire","To the court of his eye, peeping thorough desire.","His heart, like an agate, with your print impressed,","Proud with his form, in his eye pride expressed.","His tongue, all impatient to speak and not see,","Did stumble with haste in his eyesight to be;","All senses to that sense did make their repair,","To feel only looking on fairest of fair.","Methought all his senses were locked in his eye,","As jewels in crystal for some prince to buy;","Who, tend'ring their own worth from where they were glassed,","Did point you to buy them, along as you passed.","His face's own margent did quote such amazes","That all eyes saw his eyes enchanted with gazes.","I'll give you Aquitaine, and all that is his,","An you give him for my sake but one loving kiss."],"id":325,"m":"Why, all his behaviour retreated into his eyes, peeping out with desire. His heart, like a seal-stone engraved with your picture, was proud of that image and showed its pride in his eyes. His tongue, impatient at being able to speak but not see, stumbled in its hurry to be in his eyes. All his senses ran to the sense of sight, just to feel themselves looking at the fairest of the fair. I thought all his senses were locked up in his eyes like jewels in a crystal case for a prince to buy, and from behind the glass they offered themselves and pointed you to buy them as you passed. His face was like the margin of a book, noting such wonder that every eye saw his eyes were bewitched by gazing. I'll give you Aquitaine and everything he owns if you'll give him just one loving kiss for my sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Come, to our pavilion. Boyet is disposed."],"id":326,"m":"Come, to our tent. Boyet is in a joking mood."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["But to speak that in words which his eye hath disclosed.","I only have made a mouth of his eye","By adding a tongue which I know will not lie."],"id":327,"m":"I'm only putting into words what his eyes revealed. I've just made his eye into a mouth by adding a tongue that I know won't lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Thou art an old love-monger, and speakest skilfully."],"id":328,"m":"You're an old love-dealer, and you talk cleverly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["He is Cupid's grandfather, and learns news of him."],"id":329,"m":"He is Cupid's grandfather, and gets his news from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Then was Venus like her mother; for her father is but grim."],"id":330,"m":"Then Venus took after her mother, because her father is very ugly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Do you hear, my mad wenches?"],"id":331,"m":"Are you listening, my mad girls?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["No."],"id":332,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["What, then, do you see?"],"id":333,"m":"What, then, do you see?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Ay, our way to be gone."],"id":334,"m":"Yes: our way out of here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["You are too hard for me."],"id":335,"m":"You're too much for me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":336},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"The King of Navarre's park.","id":337},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Armado the Braggart and Moth his Boy.","id":338},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Warble, child, make passionate my sense of hearing."],"id":339,"m":"Sing, child; fill my sense of hearing with passion."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["[Singing]","~Concolinel."],"id":340,"m":"[Singing] Concolinel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sweet air! Go, tenderness of years, take this key, give enlargement to","the swain, bring him festinately hither. I must employ him in a letter","to my love."],"id":341,"m":"A sweet tune! Go, tender youth, take this key, set the peasant free and bring him here quickly. I need him to carry a letter to my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Master, will you win your love with a French brawl?"],"id":342,"m":"Master, are you going to win your love with a French brawl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["How meanest thou? Brawling in French?"],"id":343,"m":"What do you mean? Quarrelling in French?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["No, my complete master; but to jig off a tune at the tongue's end,","canary to it with your feet, humour it with turning up your eyelids,","sigh a note and sing a note, sometime through the throat, as if you","swallowed love with singing love, sometime through the nose, as if you","snuffed up love by smelling love; with your hat penthouse-like o'er the","shop of your eyes, with your arms crossed on your thin-belly doublet","like a rabbit on a spit; or your hands in your pocket like a man after","the old painting; and keep not too long in one tune, but a snip and","away. These are compliments, these are humours; these betray nice","wenches that would be betrayed without these; and make them men of","note—do you note me?—that most are affected to these."],"id":344,"m":"No, my perfect master; I mean dance a jig off the tip of your tongue, dance a canary to it with your feet, add to the mood by rolling up your eyes, sigh a note and sing a note, sometimes through your throat as if you were swallowing love by singing about love, sometimes through your nose as if you were sniffing up love by smelling it; with your hat pulled over your eyes like a shop awning, your arms folded over your thin waistcoat like a rabbit on a spit, or your hands in your pockets like a man in an old painting; and don't stay too long on one tune, just a snatch and away. These are the fashions, these are the tricks. They seduce fine girls who would be seduced anyway; and they make the men who go in for them men of note. Do you note me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["How hast thou purchased this experience?"],"id":345,"m":"How did you buy this experience?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["By my penny of observation."],"id":346,"m":"With my penny's worth of observation."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["But O—but O—"],"id":347,"m":"But oh, but oh..."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["\"The hobby-horse is forgot.\""],"id":348,"m":"'...the hobby-horse is forgotten.' (A line from a popular song.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Call'st thou my love \"hobby-horse\"?"],"id":349,"m":"Are you calling my love a 'hobby-horse'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["No, master. The hobby-horse is but a colt, and your love perhaps a","hackney. But have you forgot your love?"],"id":350,"m":"No, master. The hobby-horse is only a colt, and your love is perhaps a hired hack. But have you forgotten your love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Almost I had."],"id":351,"m":"I nearly had."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Negligent student! Learn her by heart."],"id":352,"m":"Careless student! Learn her by heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["By heart and in heart, boy."],"id":353,"m":"By heart and in heart, boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["And out of heart, master. All those three I will prove."],"id":354,"m":"And out of heart, master. I'll prove all three."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["What wilt thou prove?"],"id":355,"m":"What will you prove?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A man, if I live; and this, \"by, in, and without,\" upon the instant:","\"by\" heart you love her, because your heart cannot come by her; \"in\"","heart you love her, because your heart is in love with her; and \"out\"","of heart you love her, being out of heart that you cannot enjoy her."],"id":356,"m":"A man, if I live; and this 'by, in and out of' straight away. You love her 'by' heart, because your heart can't come by her; 'in' heart, because your heart is in love with her; and 'out of' heart, because you're disheartened that you can't have her."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I am all these three."],"id":357,"m":"I am all three."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["And three times as much more, and yet nothing at all."],"id":358,"m":"And three times as much again, and yet nothing at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Fetch hither the swain. He must carry me a letter."],"id":359,"m":"Bring the peasant here. He must carry a letter for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A message well sympathized: a horse to be ambassador for an ass."],"id":360,"m":"A well-matched errand: a horse to be ambassador for an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Ha, ha, what sayest thou?"],"id":361,"m":"Ha, ha, what are you saying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Marry, sir, you must send the ass upon the horse, for he is very","slow-gaited. But I go."],"id":362,"m":"Why, sir, you'll have to send the ass on the horse, because he's very slow on his feet. But I'm going."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The way is but short. Away!"],"id":363,"m":"It's only a short way. Go!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["As swift as lead, sir."],"id":364,"m":"As fast as lead, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The meaning, pretty ingenious?","Is not lead a metal heavy, dull, and slow?"],"id":365,"m":"What do you mean, clever little one? Isn't lead a heavy, dull and slow metal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Minime, honest master; or rather, master, no."],"id":366,"m":"Not at all, honest master; or rather, master, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I say lead is slow."],"id":367,"m":"I say lead is slow."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["You are too swift, sir, to say so.","Is that lead slow which is fired from a gun?"],"id":368,"m":"You're too quick to say so, sir. Is lead slow when it's fired from a gun?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sweet smoke of rhetoric!","He reputes me a cannon; and the bullet, that's he.","I shoot thee at the swain."],"id":369,"m":"Sweet smoke of rhetoric! He takes me for a cannon, and he's the bullet. I fire you at the peasant."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Thump then, and I flee."],"id":370,"m":"Bang, then, and I fly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":371},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["A most acute juvenal, voluble and free of grace!","By thy favour, sweet welkin, I must sigh in thy face.","Most rude melancholy, valour gives thee place.","My herald is returned."],"id":372,"m":"A very sharp youth, quick-tongued and full of charm! By your leave, sweet sky, I must sigh in your face. Most rude melancholy, courage gives way to you. My messenger has come back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Moth and Costard.","id":373},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A wonder, master! Here's a costard broken in a shin."],"id":374,"m":"A wonder, master! Here's a Costard (an apple-head) with a broken shin."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Some enigma, some riddle. Come, thy l'envoi begin."],"id":375,"m":"Some enigma, some riddle. Come, begin your 'envoy', the poem's ending."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["No egma, no riddle, no l'envoi, no salve in the mail, sir. O, sir,","plantain, a plain plantain! No l'envoi, no l'envoi, no salve, sir,","but a plantain."],"id":376,"m":"No enigma, no riddle, no envoy, no ointment in the bag, sir. Oh, sir, a plantain leaf, a plain plantain! No envoy, no envoy, no ointment, sir, just a plantain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["By virtue, thou enforcest laughter; thy silly thought, my spleen; the","heaving of my lungs provokes me to ridiculous smiling. O, pardon me, my","stars! Doth the inconsiderate take salve for l'envoi, and the word","l'envoi for a salve?"],"id":377,"m":"Honestly, you force me to laugh; your silly idea forces my spleen; the heaving of my lungs makes me smile ridiculously. Oh, forgive me, my stars! Does this thoughtless fellow mistake 'salve' for 'envoy', and the word 'envoy' for a salve?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Do the wise think them other? Is not l'envoi a salve?"],"id":378,"m":"Do the wise think they're anything else? Isn't an envoy a salve (a farewell)?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["No, page; it is an epilogue or discourse to make plain","Some obscure precedence that hath tofore been sain.","I will example it:","The fox, the ape, and the humble-bee","Were still at odds, being but three.","There's the moral. Now the l'envoi."],"id":379,"m":"No, page; it's an epilogue or speech to explain something obscure that has been said before. I'll give an example: 'The fox, the ape and the bumble-bee were always at odds, being only three.' There's the moral. Now the envoy."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["I will add the l'envoi. Say the moral again."],"id":380,"m":"I'll add the envoy. Say the moral again."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The fox, the ape, and the humble-bee","Were still at odds, being but three."],"id":381,"m":"'The fox, the ape and the bumble-bee were always at odds, being only three.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Until the goose came out of door,","And stayed the odds by adding four.","Now will I begin your moral, and do you follow with my l'envoi.","The fox, the ape, and the humble-bee","Were still at odds, being but three."],"id":382,"m":"'Until the goose came out of the door and settled the odds by making four.' Now I'll begin your moral, and you follow with my envoy. 'The fox, the ape and the bumble-bee were always at odds, being only three.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Until the goose came out of door,","Staying the odds by adding four."],"id":383,"m":"'Until the goose came out of the door, settling the odds by making four.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A good l'envoi, ending in the goose. Would you desire more?"],"id":384,"m":"A good envoy, ending with the goose. What more could you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["The boy hath sold him a bargain, a goose, that's flat.","Sir, your pennyworth is good, an your goose be fat.","To sell a bargain well is as cunning as fast and loose.","Let me see: a fat l'envoi—ay, that's a fat goose."],"id":385,"m":"The boy has sold him a bargain, a goose (made a fool of him), that's for sure. Sir, you've got your money's worth, if your goose is fat. Selling a bargain well is as cunning a trick as 'fast and loose'. Let me see: a fat envoy; yes, that's a fat goose."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Come hither, come hither. How did this argument begin?"],"id":386,"m":"Come here, come here. How did this argument start?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["By saying that a costard was broken in a shin.","Then called you for the l'envoi."],"id":387,"m":"With my saying that a Costard had broken his shin. Then you called for the envoy."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["True, and I for a plantain. Thus came your argument in. Then the boy's","fat l'envoi, the goose that you bought; and he ended the market."],"id":388,"m":"True, and I called for a plantain. That's how your argument came in. Then the boy's fat envoy, the goose that you bought; and that closed the market."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["But tell me, how was there a costard broken in a shin?"],"id":389,"m":"But tell me, how did a Costard break his shin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["I will tell you sensibly."],"id":390,"m":"I'll tell you so you can feel it."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Thou hast no feeling of it, Moth. I will speak that l'envoi.","I, Costard, running out, that was safely within,","Fell over the threshold and broke my shin."],"id":391,"m":"You have no feeling of it, Moth. I'll speak that envoy. 'I, Costard, running out, when I was safely inside, fell over the doorstep and broke my shin.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["We will talk no more of this matter."],"id":392,"m":"We'll say no more about this matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Till there be more matter in the shin."],"id":393,"m":"Not till there's more matter (pus) in the shin."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sirrah Costard, I will enfranchise thee."],"id":394,"m":"Fellow Costard, I will enfranchise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["O, marry me to one Frances! I smell some l'envoi, some goose, in","this."],"id":395,"m":"Oh, marry me to some Frances! I smell some envoy, some goose (some trick), in this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["By my sweet soul, I mean setting thee at liberty, enfreedoming thy","person. Thou wert immured, restrained, captivated, bound."],"id":396,"m":"By my sweet soul, I mean setting you at liberty, making your person free. You were walled up, restrained, captured, bound."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["True, true; and now you will be my purgation, and let me loose."],"id":397,"m":"True, true; and now you'll be my purge (my release) and let me loose."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I give thee thy liberty, set thee from durance, and, in lieu thereof,","impose on thee nothing but this: [Giving him a letter] bear this","significant to the country maid Jaquenetta. [Giving money] There is","remuneration for the best ward of mine honour is rewarding my","dependents. Moth, follow."],"id":398,"m":"I give you your liberty, free you from imprisonment, and in return I ask nothing of you but this: [Giving him a letter] carry this message to the country girl Jaquenetta. [Giving money] There's a remuneration, for the best guard of my honour is rewarding my servants. Moth, follow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":399},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Like the sequel, I. Signior Costard, adieu."],"id":400,"m":"Like the sequel, I will. Signior Costard, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Moth.","id":401},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["My sweet ounce of man's flesh, my incony Jew!","Now will I look to his remuneration. \"Remuneration\"! O, that's the","Latin word for three farthings. Three farthings—remuneration. \"What's","the price of this inkle?\" \"One penny.\" \"No, I'll give you a","remuneration.\" Why, it carries it! Remuneration. Why, it is a fairer","name than French crown. I will never buy and sell out of this word."],"id":402,"m":"My sweet ounce of man's flesh, my dainty jewel! Now let me look at his 'remuneration'. 'Remuneration'! Oh, that's the Latin word for three farthings. Three farthings: remuneration. 'What's the price of this tape?' 'One penny.' 'No, I'll give you a remuneration.' Why, that does the trick! Remuneration. Why, it's a finer name than a French crown. I'll never buy or sell without this word."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Berowne.","id":403},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["My good knave Costard, exceedingly well met."],"id":404,"m":"My good fellow Costard, I'm very glad to meet you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Pray you, sir, how much carnation ribbon may a man buy for a","remuneration?"],"id":405,"m":"Please, sir, how much pink ribbon can a man buy for a remuneration?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["What is a remuneration?"],"id":406,"m":"What is a remuneration?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Marry, sir, halfpenny farthing."],"id":407,"m":"Why, sir, a halfpenny and a farthing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Why, then, three-farthing worth of silk."],"id":408,"m":"Why, then, three farthings' worth of silk."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I thank your worship. God be wi' you."],"id":409,"m":"Thank you, your worship. God be with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Stay, slave. I must employ thee.","As thou wilt win my favour, good my knave,","Do one thing for me that I shall entreat."],"id":410,"m":"Wait, fellow. I need you. If you want to win my favour, my good lad, do one thing I'll ask of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["When would you have it done, sir?"],"id":411,"m":"When do you want it done, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This afternoon."],"id":412,"m":"This afternoon."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Well, I will do it, sir. Fare you well."],"id":413,"m":"Well, I'll do it, sir. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Thou knowest not what it is."],"id":414,"m":"You don't know what it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I shall know, sir, when I have done it."],"id":415,"m":"I'll know, sir, when I've done it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Why, villain, thou must know first."],"id":416,"m":"Why, you rascal, you have to know first."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I will come to your worship tomorrow morning."],"id":417,"m":"I'll come to your worship tomorrow morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["It must be done this afternoon. Hark, slave, it is but this:","The Princess comes to hunt here in the park,","And in her train there is a gentle lady;","When tongues speak sweetly, then they name her name,","And Rosaline they call her. Ask for her","And to her white hand see thou do commend","This sealed-up counsel."],"id":418,"m":"It has to be done this afternoon. Listen, fellow, it's only this. The Princess is coming to hunt here in the park, and among her attendants there's a gentle lady. When tongues speak sweetly, they say her name: they call her Rosaline. Ask for her, and make sure you deliver this sealed letter into her white hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives him money.","id":419},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["There's thy guerdon. Go."],"id":420,"m":"There's your reward. Go."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Gardon, O sweet gardon! Better than remuneration, a 'levenpence","farthing better. Most sweet gardon! I will do it, sir, in print.","Gardon! Remuneration!"],"id":421,"m":"Reward, oh sweet reward! Better than remuneration: elevenpence-farthing better. Most sweet reward! I'll do it, sir, to the letter. Reward! Remuneration!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":422},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["And I, forsooth, in love! I, that have been love's whip,","A very beadle to a humorous sigh,","A critic, nay, a night-watch constable,","A domineering pedant o'er the boy,","Than whom no mortal so magnificent!","This wimpled, whining, purblind, wayward boy,","This Signior Junior, giant-dwarf, Dan Cupid,","Regent of love-rhymes, lord of folded arms,","Th' anointed sovereign of sighs and groans,","Liege of all loiterers and malcontents,","Dread prince of plackets, king of codpieces,","Sole imperator, and great general","Of trotting paritors—O my little heart!","And I to be a corporal of his field","And wear his colours like a tumbler's hoop!","What? I love, I sue, I seek a wife?","A woman, that is like a German clock,","Still a-repairing, ever out of frame,","And never going aright, being a watch,","But being watched that it may still go right!","Nay, to be perjured, which is worst of all;","And, among three, to love the worst of all,","A whitely wanton with a velvet brow,","With two pitch-balls stuck in her face for eyes;","Ay, and, by heaven, one that will do the deed","Though Argus were her eunuch and her guard.","And I to sigh for her, to watch for her,","To pray for her! Go to, it is a plague","That Cupid will impose for my neglect","Of his almighty dreadful little might.","Well, I will love, write, sigh, pray, sue, and groan.","Some men must love my lady, and some Joan."],"id":423,"m":"And I, of all people, in love! I, who have been love's whip, a real beadle punishing every fanciful sigh, a critic, no, a night-watchman, a bossy schoolmaster over the boy Cupid, than whom no mortal was more grand! This blindfolded, whining, half-blind, wilful boy, this Signior Junior, giant-dwarf, Lord Cupid, regent of love poems, lord of folded arms, the anointed king of sighs and groans, master of all idlers and malcontents, dread prince of petticoats, king of codpieces, sole emperor and great general of the officers who hunt down sexual sinners: oh my little heart! And I'm to be a corporal in his army and wear his colours like an acrobat's hoop! What? I love, I court, I look for a wife? A woman, who is like a German clock, always being repaired, always out of order, never running right however much it's a watch, unless it's watched to keep it going right! No, worse, to break my oath, which is worst of all; and, out of three, to love the worst of them: a pale, flirtatious girl with dark velvet brows and two balls of pitch stuck in her face for eyes; yes, and, by heaven, one who would do the deed even if hundred-eyed Argus were her eunuch and her guard. And I am to sigh for her, stay awake for her, pray for her! Come on, it's a punishment Cupid has given me for ignoring his almighty, dreadful little power. Well, I'll love, write, sigh, pray, plead and groan. Some men must love a lady, and some a Joan."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":424},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The King of Navarre's park.","id":425},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Princess, a Forester, Rosaline, Maria, Katharine, Boyet and other Lords.","id":426},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Was that the King that spurred his horse so hard","Against the steep uprising of the hill?"],"id":427,"m":"Was that the King who spurred his horse so hard up the steep slope of the hill?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["I know not, but I think it was not he."],"id":428,"m":"I don't know, but I don't think it was him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Whoe'er he was, he showed a mounting mind.","Well, lords, today we shall have our dispatch;","On Saturday we will return to France.","Then, forester, my friend, where is the bush","That we must stand and play the murderer in?"],"id":429,"m":"Whoever he was, he showed an aspiring mind. Well, lords, today we'll have our business settled, and on Saturday we'll go back to France. Now, forester, my friend, where is the thicket where we must stand and play the murderer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORESTER","lines":["Hereby, upon the edge of yonder coppice,","A stand where you may make \"the fairest shoot\"."],"id":430,"m":"Close by, at the edge of that copse over there: a hiding place where you can make the fairest shot."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["I thank my beauty, I am fair that shoot,","And thereupon thou speak'st the fairest shoot."],"id":431,"m":"Thanks to my beauty, I am the fair one who shoots, and so you call it the 'fairest shoot'."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORESTER","lines":["Pardon me, madam, for I meant not so."],"id":432,"m":"Forgive me, madam, I didn't mean that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["What, what? First praise me, and again say no?","O short-lived pride! Not fair? Alack for woe!"],"id":433,"m":"What, what? First you praise me, and then say no? Oh, short-lived pride! Not fair? Alas, how sad!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORESTER","lines":["Yes, madam, fair."],"id":434,"m":"Yes, madam, fair."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Nay, never paint me now.","Where fair is not, praise cannot mend the brow.","Here, good my glass, take this for telling true:"],"id":435,"m":"No, don't paint me now. Where there's no beauty, praise can't improve a face. Here, my good mirror, take this for telling the truth:"},{"t":"sd","text":"She gives him money.","id":436},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Fair payment for foul words is more than due."],"id":437,"m":"Fair payment for foul words is more than they deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORESTER","lines":["Nothing but fair is that which you inherit."],"id":438,"m":"Everything you have is nothing but fair."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["See, see, my beauty will be saved by merit.","O heresy in fair, fit for these days!","A giving hand, though foul, shall have fair praise.","But come, the bow. Now mercy goes to kill,","And shooting well is then accounted ill.","Thus will I save my credit in the shoot:","Not wounding, pity would not let me do't;","If wounding, then it was to show my skill,","That more for praise than purpose meant to kill.","And out of question so it is sometimes,","Glory grows guilty of detested crimes,","When, for fame's sake, for praise, an outward part,","We bend to that the working of the heart;","As I for praise alone now seek to spill","The poor deer's blood, that my heart means no ill."],"id":439,"m":"Look, look, my beauty is saved by my good deeds! Oh, heresy about beauty, just right for these times! A hand that gives, however ugly, will get fair praise. But come, the bow. Now mercy goes out to kill, and shooting well counts as a bad thing. So I'll save my reputation in the hunt like this: if I don't wound the deer, it was pity that stopped me; if I do, it was to show my skill, and I aimed to kill more for praise than for any real purpose. And no doubt it is sometimes so: the love of glory becomes guilty of hateful crimes, when for fame's sake, for praise, which is only an outward thing, we bend the workings of our heart to it; as I now, only for praise, try to spill the poor deer's blood, though my heart means it no harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Do not curst wives hold that self-sovereignty","Only for praise' sake, when they strive to be","Lords o'er their lords?"],"id":440,"m":"Don't shrewish wives hold on to their self-rule just for praise, when they try to be lords over their lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Only for praise; and praise we may afford","To any lady that subdues a lord."],"id":441,"m":"Only for praise; and we can give praise to any lady who conquers a lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Costard.","id":442},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Here comes a member of the commonwealth."],"id":443,"m":"Here comes a member of the community."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["God dig-you-den all! Pray you, which is the head lady?"],"id":444,"m":"God give you good evening, all! Please, which is the head lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Thou shalt know her, fellow, by the rest that have no heads."],"id":445,"m":"You'll know her, fellow, because the rest have no heads."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Which is the greatest lady, the highest?"],"id":446,"m":"Which is the greatest lady, the highest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["The thickest and the tallest."],"id":447,"m":"The thickest and the tallest."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["The thickest and the tallest. It is so, truth is truth.","An your waist, mistress, were as slender as my wit,","One o' these maids' girdles for your waist should be fit.","Are not you the chief woman? You are the thickest here."],"id":448,"m":"The thickest and the tallest. That's right; truth is truth. If your waist, mistress, were as slender as my wit, one of these girls' belts would fit you. Aren't you the chief woman? You're the thickest here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["What's your will, sir? What's your will?"],"id":449,"m":"What do you want, sir? What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I have a letter from Monsieur Berowne to one Lady Rosaline."],"id":450,"m":"I have a letter from Monsieur Berowne to a Lady Rosaline."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["O, thy letter, thy letter! He's a good friend of mine.","Stand aside, good bearer. Boyet, you can carve.","Break up this capon."],"id":451,"m":"Oh, your letter, your letter! He's a good friend of mine. Stand aside, good messenger. Boyet, you can carve; cut up this chicken (open this letter)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["I am bound to serve.","This letter is mistook; it importeth none here.","It is writ to Jaquenetta."],"id":452,"m":"I'm bound to serve. This letter has been mixed up; it's for no one here. It's written to Jaquenetta."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["We will read it, I swear.","Break the neck of the wax, and everyone give ear."],"id":453,"m":"We'll read it, I swear. Break the seal, and everyone listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["[Reads] By heaven, that thou art fair is most infallible; true that","thou art beauteous; truth itself that thou art lovely. More fairer than","fair, beautiful than beauteous, truer than truth itself, have","commiseration on thy heroical vassal. The magnanimous and most","illustrate King Cophetua set eye upon the pernicious and indubitate","beggar Zenelophon, and he it was that might rightly say, \"Veni, vidi,","vici,\" which to annothanize in the vulgar—O base and obscure","vulgar!—videlicet, He came, see, and overcame. He came, one; see,","two; overcame, three. Who came? The King. Why did he come? To see. Why","did he see? To overcome. To whom came he? To the beggar. What saw he?","The beggar. Who overcame he? The beggar. The conclusion is victory. On","whose side? The King's. The captive is enriched. On whose side? The","beggar's. The catastrophe is a nuptial. On whose side? The King's? No,","on both in one, or one in both. I am the King, for so stands the","comparison; thou the beggar, for so witnesseth thy lowliness. Shall I","command thy love? I may. Shall I enforce thy love? I could. Shall I","entreat thy love? I will. What shalt thou exchange for rags? Robes. For","tittles? Titles. For thyself? Me. Thus expecting thy reply, I profane","my lips on thy foot, my eyes on thy picture, and my heart on thy every","part.","Thine in the dearest design of industry,","Don Adriano de Armado.","Thus dost thou hear the Nemean lion roar","'Gainst thee, thou lamb, that standest as his prey.","Submissive fall his princely feet before,","And he from forage will incline to play.","But if thou strive, poor soul, what are thou then?","Food for his rage, repasture for his den."],"id":454,"m":"[Reads] 'By heaven, it is utterly certain that you are fair; true that you are beautiful; the truth itself that you are lovely. More fairer than fair, more beautiful than beautiful, truer than truth itself, take pity on your heroic servant. The noble and most famous King Cophetua set eyes on the wicked and undoubted beggar Zenelophon, and he was the one who could rightly say, \"Veni, vidi, vici\", which to translate into the common tongue (oh, low and obscure common tongue!) is: he came, saw and conquered. He came, one; saw, two; conquered, three. Who came? The King. Why did he come? To see. Why did he see? To conquer. To whom did he come? To the beggar. What did he see? The beggar. Whom did he conquer? The beggar. The conclusion is victory: on whose side? The King's. The captive is made rich: on whose side? The beggar's. The final outcome is a wedding: on whose side? The King's? No, on both in one, or one in both. I am the King, for that is how the comparison goes; you are the beggar, as your lowliness shows. Shall I command your love? I may. Shall I force your love? I could. Shall I beg for your love? I will. What will you get in exchange for rags? Robes. For trifles? Titles. For yourself? Me. Expecting your reply, I kiss your foot with my lips, your picture with my eyes and every part of you with my heart. Yours in the most devoted plan of effort, Don Adriano de Armado.' 'So you hear the Nemean lion roar at you, little lamb, standing there as his prey. Fall humbly at his royal feet and he will turn from hunting to play. But if you struggle, poor soul, what are you then? Food for his rage and a meal for his den.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["What plume of feathers is he that indited this letter?","What vane? What weathercock? Did you ever hear better?"],"id":455,"m":"What feather-brained fop wrote this letter? What weather-vane? What weathercock? Did you ever hear anything better?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["I am much deceived but I remember the style."],"id":456,"m":"I'm much mistaken if I don't recognise the style."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Else your memory is bad, going o'er it erewhile."],"id":457,"m":"Your memory must be bad otherwise, having just read through it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["This Armado is a Spaniard that keeps here in court,","A phantasime, a Monarcho, and one that makes sport","To the Prince and his book-mates."],"id":458,"m":"This Armado is a Spaniard who lives here at court, a fantastic, a Monarcho (a famous mad braggart), who entertains the Prince and his fellow bookworms."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Thou, fellow, a word.","Who gave thee this letter?"],"id":459,"m":"You, fellow, a word. Who gave you this letter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I told you: my lord."],"id":460,"m":"I told you: my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["To whom shouldst thou give it?"],"id":461,"m":"Who were you supposed to give it to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["From my lord to my lady."],"id":462,"m":"From my lord to my lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["From which lord to which lady?"],"id":463,"m":"From which lord to which lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["From my Lord Berowne, a good master of mine,","To a lady of France that he called Rosaline."],"id":464,"m":"From my Lord Berowne, a good master of mine, to a French lady he called Rosaline."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Thou hast mistaken his letter. Come, lords, away.","Here, sweet, put up this: 'twill be thine another day."],"id":465,"m":"You've delivered the wrong letter. Come, lords, let's go. Here, sweet, keep this; it'll be yours another day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Boyet, Rosaline, Maria and Costard.","id":466},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Who is the shooter? Who is the shooter?"],"id":467,"m":"Who is the shooter? Who is the shooter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Shall I teach you to know?"],"id":468,"m":"Shall I teach you how to tell?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Ay, my continent of beauty."],"id":469,"m":"Yes, my container of beauty."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Why, she that bears the bow.","Finely put off!"],"id":470,"m":"Why, the one who carries the bow. Nicely dodged!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["My lady goes to kill horns, but if thou marry,","Hang me by the neck if horns that year miscarry.","Finely put on!"],"id":471,"m":"My lady goes to kill horned deer; but if you marry, hang me if horns don't flourish that year (your husband will be a cuckold). Nicely hit!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Well, then, I am the shooter."],"id":472,"m":"Well then, I am the shooter."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["And who is your deer?"],"id":473,"m":"And who is your deer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["If we choose by the horns, yourself come not near.","Finely put on indeed!"],"id":474,"m":"If we choose by the horns, it's you; don't come near. A nice hit indeed!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["You still wrangle with her, Boyet, and she strikes at the brow."],"id":475,"m":"You keep squabbling with her, Boyet, and she aims at your forehead."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["But she herself is hit lower. Have I hit her now?"],"id":476,"m":"But she herself is hit lower down. Have I hit her now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Shall I come upon thee with an old saying, that was a man when King","Pepin of France was a little boy, as touching the hit it?"],"id":477,"m":"Shall I come at you with an old saying, which was already grown up when King Pepin of France was a little boy, about 'hitting it'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["So I may answer thee with one as old, that was a woman when Queen","Guinevere of Britain was a little wench, as touching the hit it."],"id":478,"m":"Then I can answer with one just as old, which was a woman when Queen Guinevere of Britain was a little girl, about 'hitting it'."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["~Thou canst not hit it, hit it, hit it,","~Thou canst not hit it, my good man."],"id":479,"m":"(Singing) You can't hit it, hit it, hit it, you can't hit it, my good man."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["~An I cannot, cannot, cannot,","~An I cannot, another can."],"id":480,"m":"(Singing) And if I can't, can't, can't, if I can't, another man can."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Rosaline.","id":481},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["By my troth, most pleasant. How both did fit it!"],"id":482,"m":"Honestly, very pleasant. How well they both matched it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["A mark marvellous well shot, for they both did hit it."],"id":483,"m":"A target wonderfully well shot, because they both hit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["A mark! O, mark but that mark! A mark, says my lady!","Let the mark have a prick in't, to mete at, if it may be."],"id":484,"m":"A target! Just look at that target! A target, says my lady! Let the target have a bullseye in it to aim at, if possible."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Wide o' the bow hand! I' faith, your hand is out."],"id":485,"m":"Wide on the left! Really, you're out of practice."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Indeed, a' must shoot nearer, or he'll ne'er hit the clout."],"id":486,"m":"Yes, he must shoot closer, or he'll never hit the middle."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["An if my hand be out, then belike your hand is in."],"id":487,"m":"And if I'm out of practice, then perhaps you're in."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Then will she get the upshoot by cleaving the pin."],"id":488,"m":"Then she'll win the match by splitting the peg in the centre."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Come, come, you talk greasily, your lips grow foul."],"id":489,"m":"Come on, you're talking dirty; your lips are getting foul."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["She's too hard for you at pricks, sir. Challenge her to bowl."],"id":490,"m":"She's too good for you at archery, sir. Challenge her to bowls."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["I fear too much rubbing. Good night, my good owl."],"id":491,"m":"I'm afraid of too much rubbing. Good night, my good owl."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Boyet and Maria.","id":492},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["By my soul, a swain, a most simple clown!","Lord, Lord, how the ladies and I have put him down!","O' my troth, most sweet jests, most incony vulgar wit,","When it comes so smoothly off, so obscenely, as it were, so fit.","Armado, o' the one side, O, a most dainty man!","To see him walk before a lady and to bear her fan!","To see him kiss his hand and how most sweetly he will swear!","And his page o' t'other side, that handful of wit!","Ah, heavens, it is a most pathetical nit."],"id":493,"m":"By my soul, a peasant, a most simple clown! Lord, Lord, how the ladies and I put him down! Honestly, very sweet jokes, very fine, common wit, when it comes off so smoothly, so obscenely, as it were, so neatly. Armado on one side: oh, a very dainty man! To see him walk in front of a lady and carry her fan! To see him kiss his hand, and how sweetly he swears! And his page on the other side, that handful of wit! Ah, heavens, he is a most moving little flea."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shout within.","id":494},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Sola, sola!"],"id":495,"m":"Hallo, hallo! (a hunting cry)"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":496},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"The same.","id":497},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dull, Holofernes, the Pedant and Nathaniel.","id":498},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Very reverend sport, truly, and done in the testimony of a good","conscience."],"id":499,"m":"Very respectable sport, truly, and done with a clear conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["The deer was, as you know, sanguis, in blood, ripe as the pomewater,","who now hangeth like a jewel in the ear of caelo, the sky, the","welkin, the heaven, and anon falleth like a crab on the face of","terra, the soil, the land, the earth."],"id":500,"m":"The deer was, as you know, 'sanguis', in prime condition, ripe as an apple, which hangs now like a jewel in the ear of 'caelo', the sky, the heavens, the firmament, and in a moment falls like a crab-apple on the face of 'terra', the soil, the land, the earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Truly, Master Holofernes, the epithets are sweetly varied, like a","scholar at the least. But, sir, I assure ye it was a buck of the first","head."],"id":501,"m":"Truly, Master Holofernes, your descriptive words are sweetly varied, like a scholar's at the very least. But, sir, I assure you it was a buck of the first head (a five-year-old deer with its first full antlers)."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Sir Nathaniel, haud credo."],"id":502,"m":"Sir Nathaniel, 'haud credo': I don't believe it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["'Twas not a \"auld grey doe\", 'twas a pricket."],"id":503,"m":"It wasn't an 'old grey doe'; it was a pricket (a two-year-old buck)."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Most barbarous intimation! Yet a kind of insinuation, as it were, in","via, in way, of explication; facere, as it were, replication, or","rather, ostentare, to show, as it were, his inclination, after his","undressed, unpolished, uneducated, unpruned, untrained, or rather,","unlettered, or ratherest, unconfirmed fashion, to insert again my haud","credo for a deer."],"id":504,"m":"A most barbarous suggestion! Yet a kind of hint, as it were, 'in via', on the way to explaining; 'facere', as it were, a reply; or rather 'ostentare', to show, as it were, his inclination, in his undressed, unpolished, uneducated, unpruned, untrained, or rather unlettered, or rather-est, unconfirmed way, to put in again my 'haud credo' for a deer."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["I said the deer was not a \"auld grey doe\", 'twas a pricket."],"id":505,"m":"I said the deer wasn't an 'old grey doe'; it was a pricket."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Twice-sod simplicity, bis coctus!","O, thou monster Ignorance, how deformed dost thou look!"],"id":506,"m":"Twice-boiled stupidity, 'bis coctus'! Oh, you monster Ignorance, how deformed you look!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Sir, he hath never fed of the dainties that are bred of a book.","He hath not eat paper, as it were; he hath not drunk ink.","His intellect is not replenished; he is only an animal, only sensible","in the duller parts.","And such barren plants are set before us that we thankful should be—","Which we of taste and feeling are—for those parts that do fructify in","us more than he.","For as it would ill become me to be vain, indiscreet, or a fool,","So, were there a patch set on learning, to see him in a school.","But, omne bene, say I, being of an old father's mind;","Many can brook the weather that love not the wind."],"id":507,"m":"Sir, he has never fed on the delicacies that grow in books. He hasn't eaten paper, so to speak; he hasn't drunk ink. His mind isn't nourished; he's only an animal, with feeling only in his duller parts. And such barren plants are set before us so that we, who have taste and feeling, should be thankful for the parts in us that bear more fruit than his. For just as it would be wrong for me to be vain, foolish or a fool, it would be like patching a fool onto learning to see him in a school. But 'omne bene', all's well, I say, agreeing with an old Church Father: many can stand the weather who don't like the wind."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["You two are bookmen. Can you tell me by your wit","What was a month old at Cain's birth, that's not five weeks old as yet?"],"id":508,"m":"You two are bookmen. Can you tell me, with your cleverness, what was a month old when Cain was born, and isn't five weeks old yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Dictynna, goodman Dull. Dictynna, goodman Dull."],"id":509,"m":"Dictynna, goodman Dull. Dictynna, goodman Dull."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["What is Dictynna?"],"id":510,"m":"What is Dictynna?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["A title to Phoebe, to Luna, to the moon."],"id":511,"m":"A name for Phoebe, for Luna, for the moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["The moon was a month old when Adam was no more,","And raught not to five weeks when he came to five-score.","Th' allusion holds in the exchange."],"id":512,"m":"The moon was a month old when Adam was no older, and hadn't reached five weeks when he reached a hundred. The allusion holds good when you swap the names."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["'Tis true, indeed. The collusion holds in the exchange."],"id":513,"m":"That's true, indeed. The collusion holds good in the exchange."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["God comfort thy capacity! I say, th' allusion holds in the exchange."],"id":514,"m":"God help your brain! I said, the allusion holds good in the exchange."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["And I say the pollution holds in the exchange, for the moon is never","but a month old; and I say beside that 'twas a pricket that the","Princess killed."],"id":515,"m":"And I say the pollution holds good in the exchange, because the moon is never more than a month old; and what's more, I say it was a pricket that the Princess killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Sir Nathaniel, will you hear an extemporal epitaph on the death of the","deer? And, to humour the ignorant, call I the deer the Princess killed","a pricket."],"id":516,"m":"Sir Nathaniel, will you hear an improvised epitaph on the death of the deer? And, to humour the ignorant, I'll call the deer the Princess killed a pricket."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Perge, good Master Holofernes, perge, so it shall please you to","abrogate scurrility."],"id":517,"m":"'Perge', go on, good Master Holofernes, go on, as long as you leave out anything rude."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I will something affect the letter; for it argues facility.","The preyful Princess pierced and pricked a pretty pleasing pricket;","Some say a sore; but not a sore till now made sore with shooting.","The dogs did yell, put \"l\" to sore, then sorel jumps from thicket;","Or pricket sore, or else sorel, the people fall a-hooting.","If sore be sore, then \"L\" to \"sore\" makes fifty sores o' sorel.","Of one sore I an hundred make, by adding but one more \"L\"."],"id":518,"m":"I'll indulge in a little alliteration, for it shows fluency. 'The predatory Princess pierced and pricked a pretty, pleasing pricket. Some say a sore (a four-year-old buck), but it wasn't sore until shooting made it sore. The dogs yelled; put \"L\" on \"sore\" and a sorel (a three-year-old buck) jumps out of the thicket; whether pricket, sore or sorel, the people start shouting. If sore is sore, then L (fifty in Roman numerals) added to sore makes fifty sores of sorel. From one sore I make a hundred, by adding just one more L.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["A rare talent!"],"id":519,"m":"A rare talent!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["[Aside] If a talent be a claw, look how he claws him with a talent."],"id":520,"m":"[Aside] If a talent is a talon (a claw), see how he claws at him with flattery."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["This is a gift that I have, simple, simple; a foolish extravagant","spirit, full of forms, figures, shapes, objects, ideas, apprehensions,","motions, revolutions. These are begot in the ventricle of memory,","nourished in the womb of pia mater, and delivered upon the mellowing","of occasion. But the gift is good in those in whom it is acute, and I","am thankful for it."],"id":521,"m":"This is a gift I have, simple, simple: a foolish, wandering spirit, full of forms, figures, shapes, objects, ideas, notions, impulses, changes. They are conceived in the chamber of memory, nourished in the womb of the brain and born when the moment is ripe. But the gift is good in those in whom it is sharp, and I am thankful for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Sir, I praise the Lord for you, and so may my parishioners, for their","sons are well tutored by you, and their daughters profit very greatly","under you. You are a good member of the commonwealth."],"id":522,"m":"Sir, I praise the Lord for you, and so may my parishioners, because their sons are well taught by you, and their daughters do very well under you. You're a fine member of the community."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Mehercle! If their sons be ingenious, they shall want no instruction;","if their daughters be capable, I will put it to them. But, vir sapit","qui pauca loquitur. A soul feminine saluteth us."],"id":523,"m":"By Hercules! If their sons are clever, they'll lack no instruction; if their daughters are able, I'll put it to them. But 'vir sapit qui pauca loquitur': the wise man speaks little. A female soul greets us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Jaquenetta and Costard.","id":524},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["God give you good morrow, Master Person."],"id":525,"m":"God give you good morning, Master Parson."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Master Person, quasi pierce one. And if one should be pierced, which","is the one?"],"id":526,"m":"Master 'Person', as if 'pierce-one'. And if one were to be pierced, which is the one?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Marry, Master schoolmaster, he that is likest to a hogshead."],"id":527,"m":"Why, Master Schoolmaster, the one most like a barrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Of piercing a hogshead! A good lustre or conceit in a turf of earth;","fire enough for a flint, pearl enough for a swine. 'Tis pretty; it is","well."],"id":528,"m":"Of piercing a barrel! A good spark of wit in a lump of earth; enough fire for a flint, a pearl good enough for a pig. It's pretty; it's well done."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Good Master Parson, be so good as read me this letter. It was given me","by Costard, and sent me from Don Armado. I beseech you read it."],"id":529,"m":"Good Master Parson, please be so kind as to read me this letter. Costard gave it to me, and Don Armado sent it. Please read it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving a letter to Nathaniel.","id":530},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Fauste precor, gelida quando pecus omne sub umbra Ruminat—","and so forth. Ah, good old Mantuan, I may speak of thee as the","traveller doth of Venice:","Venetia, Venetia,","Chi non ti vede, non ti pretia.","Old Mantuan, old Mantuan! Who understandeth thee not, loves thee not.","[He sings]","~Ut, re, sol, la, mi, fa.","Under pardon, sir, what are the contents? Or rather as Horace says in","his—What, my soul, verses?"],"id":531,"m":"'Fauste, precor, gelida quando pecus omne sub umbra ruminat' (a line from the Latin poet Mantuan), and so on. Ah, good old Mantuan, I can say of you what the traveller says of Venice: 'Venice, Venice, whoever does not see you does not prize you.' Old Mantuan, old Mantuan! Whoever doesn't understand you doesn't love you. [He sings] Do, re, so, la, mi, fa. With your pardon, sir, what does it say? Or rather, as Horace says in his... What, my soul, verses?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Ay, sir, and very learned."],"id":532,"m":"Yes, sir, and very learned ones."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Let me hear a staff, a stanze, a verse,","Lege, domine."],"id":533,"m":"Let me hear a stanza, a stanza, a verse. 'Lege, domine': read, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["[Reads]","If love make me forsworn, how shall I swear to love?","Ah, never faith could hold, if not to beauty vowed.","Though to myself forsworn, to thee I'll faithful prove.","Those thoughts to me were oaks, to thee like osiers bowed.","Study his bias leaves, and makes his book thine eyes,","Where all those pleasures live that art would comprehend.","If knowledge be the mark, to know thee shall suffice.","Well learned is that tongue that well can thee commend,","All ignorant that soul that sees thee without wonder;","Which is to me some praise, that I thy parts admire.","Thy eye Jove's lightning bears, thy voice his dreadful thunder,","Which, not to anger bent, is music and sweet fire.","Celestial as thou art, O, pardon love this wrong,","That sings heaven's praise with such an earthly tongue."],"id":534,"m":"[Reads] 'If love makes me break my oath, how can I swear to love? Ah, no promise could ever hold unless it was made to beauty. Though I break faith with myself, I'll be faithful to you. Those vows that were like oaks to me bend like willows before you. The student leaves his course and makes your eyes his book, where all the pleasures live that learning wants to grasp. If knowledge is the target, knowing you will be enough. Well-educated is the tongue that can praise you well; utterly ignorant the soul that sees you without wonder; and it is some credit to me that I admire your qualities. Your eye carries Jove's lightning, your voice his dreadful thunder, which, when not meant in anger, is music and sweet fire. Heavenly as you are, oh, forgive love this wrong, that sings heaven's praise with such an earthly tongue.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["You find not the apostrophus, and so miss the accent. Let me supervise","the canzonet. [He takes the letter] Here are only numbers ratified,","but, for the elegancy, facility, and golden cadence of poesy, caret.","Ovidius Naso was the man. And why indeed \"Naso,\" but for smelling out","the odoriferous flowers of fancy, the jerks of invention? Imitari is","nothing: so doth the hound his master, the ape his keeper, the tired","horse his rider. But, damosella virgin, was this directed to you?"],"id":535,"m":"You don't observe the apostrophes, and so you miss the stresses. Let me look over the little song. [He takes the letter] There's nothing here but metre in order; as for the elegance, fluency and golden rhythm of poetry, 'caret', it's missing. Ovidius Naso was the man. And why indeed 'Naso' (nose), except for smelling out the sweet-scented flowers of fancy, the flashes of invention? 'Imitari', imitating, is nothing: the dog does that to his master, the ape to his keeper, the harnessed horse to his rider. But, 'damosella', virgin, was this addressed to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Ay, sir, from one Monsieur Berowne, one of the strange queen's lords."],"id":536,"m":"Yes, sir, from a Monsieur Berowne, one of the foreign queen's lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I will overglance the superscript: To the snow-white hand of the most","beauteous Lady Rosaline. I will look again on the intellect of the","letter, for the nomination of the party writing to the person written","unto: Your Ladyship's in all desired employment, Berowne. Sir","Nathaniel, this Berowne is one of the votaries with the King, and here","he hath framed a letter to a sequent of the stranger queen's, which","accidentally, or by the way of progression, hath miscarried. Trip and","go, my sweet, deliver this paper into the royal hand of the King. It","may concern much. Stay not thy compliment. I forgive thy duty. Adieu."],"id":537,"m":"I'll glance at the address: 'To the snow-white hand of the most beautiful Lady Rosaline.' I'll look again at the content of the letter for the name of the person writing to the person written to: 'Your Ladyship's in all desired service, Berowne.' Sir Nathaniel, this Berowne is one of the men under oath with the King, and here he has written a letter to a follower of the foreign queen, which accidentally, or on its way, has gone astray. Off you go, my sweet; deliver this paper into the King's royal hand. It may be very important. Don't stay to curtsy. I excuse you from your duty. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Good Costard, go with me. Sir, God save your life."],"id":538,"m":"Good Costard, come with me. Sir, God save your life."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Have with thee, my girl."],"id":539,"m":"I'm with you, my girl."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Costard and Jaquenetta.","id":540},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Sir, you have done this in the fear of God, very religiously; and, as a","certain Father saith—"],"id":541,"m":"Sir, you've done this in the fear of God, very religiously; and, as a certain Church Father says..."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Sir, tell not me of the Father, I do fear colourable colours. But to","return to the verses: did they please you, Sir Nathaniel?"],"id":542,"m":"Sir, don't tell me about the Fathers; I'm wary of plausible excuses. But to come back to the verses: did you like them, Sir Nathaniel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Marvellous well for the pen."],"id":543,"m":"Marvellously well, for the handwriting."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I do dine today at the father's of a certain pupil of mine, where if,","before repast, it shall please you to gratify the table with a grace, I","will, on my privilege I have with the parents of the foresaid child or","pupil, undertake your ben venuto; where I will prove those verses to","be very unlearned, neither savouring of poetry, wit, nor invention. I","beseech your society."],"id":544,"m":"I'm dining today at the house of the father of a certain pupil of mine, where, if you would like to grace the table by saying grace before the meal, I will, on the strength of my standing with the parents of that child or pupil, guarantee your welcome. There I'll prove those verses to be very unlearned, tasting of neither poetry, wit nor invention. I'd be glad of your company."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["And thank you too; for society, saith the text, is the happiness of","life."],"id":545,"m":"And thank you too; for company, the text says, is the happiness of life."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["And certes, the text most infallibly concludes it. [To Dull] Sir, I","do invite you too. You shall not say me nay. Pauca verba. Away! The","gentles are at their game, and we will to our recreation."],"id":546,"m":"And truly, the text concludes it beyond any doubt. [To Dull] Sir, I invite you too. You shan't say no to me. 'Pauca verba': few words. Let's go! The gentry are at their sport, and we'll go to our entertainment."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":547},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"The same.","id":548},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Berowne with a paper in his hand, alone.","id":549},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["The King, he is hunting the deer; I am coursing myself. They have","pitched a toil; I am toiling in a pitch, pitch that defiles. Defile! A","foul word! Well, set thee down, sorrow, for so they say the fool said,","and so say I, and I the fool. Well proved, wit! By the Lord, this love","is as mad as Ajax. It kills sheep, it kills me, I a sheep. Well proved","again, o' my side! I will not love; if I do, hang me! I' faith, I will","not. O, but her eye! By this light, but for her eye, I would not love","her; yes, for her two eyes. Well, I do nothing in the world but lie,","and lie in my throat. By heaven, I do love, and it hath taught me to","rhyme, and to be melancholy. And here is part of my rhyme, and here my","melancholy. Well, she hath one o' my sonnets already. The clown bore","it, the fool sent it, and the lady hath it. Sweet clown, sweeter fool,","sweetest lady! By the world, I would not care a pin if the other three","were in. Here comes one with a paper. God give him grace to groan!"],"id":550,"m":"The King is hunting the deer; I'm hunting myself. They have set up a net; I'm struggling in pitch, pitch that soils. Soil! A dirty word! Well, 'sit down, sorrow', as they say the fool said; and so say I, and I'm the fool. Well argued, wit! By the Lord, this love is as mad as Ajax: it kills sheep; it kills me, and I'm a sheep. Well argued again, on my side! I won't love; if I do, hang me! Honestly, I won't. Oh, but her eye! By this light, if it weren't for her eye, I wouldn't love her; yes, for her two eyes. Well, I do nothing in the world but lie, and lie through my teeth. By heaven, I do love, and it has taught me to rhyme and to be melancholy; and here is part of my rhyme, and here is my melancholy. Well, she has one of my sonnets already. The clown carried it, the fool sent it and the lady has it. Sweet clown, sweeter fool, sweetest lady! By the world, I wouldn't care a pin if the other three were in love too. Here comes one with a paper. God give him the grace to groan!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He stands aside.","id":551},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King with a paper.","id":552},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay me!"],"id":553,"m":"Oh dear!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["[Aside] Shot, by heaven! Proceed, sweet Cupid, thou hast thumped him","with thy birdbolt under the left pap. In faith, secrets!"],"id":554,"m":"[Aside] Hit, by heaven! Carry on, sweet Cupid; you've thumped him with your blunt arrow under the left nipple. Honestly, secrets!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads]","So sweet a kiss the golden sun gives not","To those fresh morning drops upon the rose,","As thy eye-beams, when their fresh rays have smote","The night of dew that on my cheeks down flows.","Nor shines the silver moon one half so bright","Through the transparent bosom of the deep","As doth thy face, through tears of mine give light.","Thou shin'st in every tear that I do weep.","No drop but as a coach doth carry thee;","So ridest thou triumphing in my woe.","Do but behold the tears that swell in me,","And they thy glory through my grief will show.","But do not love thyself; then thou wilt keep","My tears for glasses, and still make me weep.","O queen of queens, how far dost thou excel","No thought can think, nor tongue of mortal tell.","How shall she know my griefs? I'll drop the paper.","Sweet leaves, shade folly. Who is he comes here?"],"id":555,"m":"[Reads] 'The golden sun doesn't give as sweet a kiss to the fresh morning dewdrops on the rose as your eyes' rays give when their fresh beams strike the night's dew of tears that runs down my cheeks. And the silver moon doesn't shine half as brightly through the clear depths of the sea as your face, shining through my tears. You shine in every tear I weep. Every drop carries you like a coach, so you ride in triumph through my sorrow. Just look at the tears that well up in me, and they'll show your glory through my grief. But don't fall in love with yourself; then you'd keep my tears as mirrors and make me weep for ever. Oh queen of queens, no thought can think and no mortal tongue can tell how far you surpass all others.' How will she know my griefs? I'll drop the paper. Sweet leaves, hide my foolishness. Who's this coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Steps aside.","id":556},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What, Longaville, and reading! Listen, ear."],"id":557,"m":"What, Longaville, and reading! Listen, ear."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Longaville with a paper.","id":558},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["[Aside] Now, in thy likeness, one more fool appear!"],"id":559,"m":"[Aside] Now, dressed just like you, here comes one more fool!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Ay me! I am forsworn."],"id":560,"m":"Oh dear! I have broken my oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Why, he comes in like a perjure, wearing papers."],"id":561,"m":"Why, he comes in like a perjurer, wearing papers (as perjurers were made to wear, listing their crime)."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["In love, I hope. Sweet fellowship in shame."],"id":562,"m":"In love, I hope. Sweet companionship in shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["One drunkard loves another of the name."],"id":563,"m":"One drunkard loves another of the same kind."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Am I the first that have been perjured so?"],"id":564,"m":"Am I the first to have broken his oath like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I could put thee in comfort: not by two that I know.","Thou makest the triumviry, the corner-cap of society,","The shape of love's Tyburn, that hangs up simplicity."],"id":565,"m":"I could comfort you: not by two that I know of. You make up the threesome, the three-cornered cap of our company, the shape of love's Tyburn gallows, that hangs up simple-mindedness."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["I fear these stubborn lines lack power to move.","O sweet Maria, empress of my love,","These numbers will I tear, and write in prose."],"id":566,"m":"I'm afraid these stiff lines aren't moving enough. Oh sweet Maria, empress of my love, I'll tear up these verses and write in prose."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["O, rhymes are guards on wanton Cupid's hose.","Disfigure not his shop."],"id":567,"m":"Oh, rhymes are the trimmings on wanton Cupid's trousers. Don't spoil his merchandise."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["This same shall go."],"id":568,"m":"This one will do."},{"t":"sd","text":"He reads the sonnet.","id":569},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Did not the heavenly rhetoric of thine eye,","'Gainst whom the world cannot hold argument,","Persuade my heart to this false perjury?","Vows for thee broke deserve not punishment.","A woman I forswore, but I will prove,","Thou being a goddess, I forswore not thee.","My vow was earthly, thou a heavenly love;","Thy grace being gained, cures all disgrace in me.","Vows are but breath, and breath a vapour is.","Then thou, fair sun, which on my earth dost shine,","Exhal'st this vapour-vow; in thee it is.","If broken then, it is no fault of mine;","If by me broke, what fool is not so wise","To lose an oath to win a paradise?"],"id":570,"m":"'Didn't the heavenly eloquence of your eyes, which the whole world can't argue against, persuade my heart to this false perjury? Vows broken for you don't deserve punishment. I gave up women, but I'll prove that since you're a goddess, I didn't give up you. My vow was earthly; you are a heavenly love. Winning your grace cures all my disgrace. Vows are only breath, and breath is only vapour. So you, fair sun, shining on my earth, draw up this vapour-vow; it lives in you. If it's broken, then, it isn't my fault; and if I did break it, what fool isn't wise enough to lose an oath to win paradise?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This is the liver vein, which makes flesh a deity,","A green goose a goddess. Pure, pure idolatry.","God amend us, God amend! We are much out o' th' way."],"id":571,"m":"This is the passionate vein (the liver was thought the seat of love), which makes flesh into a god and a silly goose into a goddess. Pure, pure idolatry. God help us, God help us! We've gone badly astray."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["By whom shall I send this?—Company! Stay."],"id":572,"m":"Who can I send this with? Someone's coming! Wait."},{"t":"sd","text":"He steps aside.","id":573},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dumaine with a paper.","id":574},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["All hid, all hid, an old infant play.","Like a demigod here sit I in the sky,","And wretched fools' secrets heedfully o'er-eye.","More sacks to the mill. O heavens, I have my wish.","Dumaine transformed! Four woodcocks in a dish!"],"id":575,"m":"All hide, all hide: an old children's game. Like a demigod I sit up here in the sky and carefully watch wretched fools' secrets. More sacks to the mill! Oh heavens, I have my wish: Dumaine transformed! Four fools in one dish!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["O most divine Kate!"],"id":576,"m":"Oh most divine Kate!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["O most profane coxcomb!"],"id":577,"m":"Oh most unholy fool!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["By heaven, the wonder in a mortal eye!"],"id":578,"m":"By heaven, the wonder in a mortal eye!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["By earth, she is but corporal. There you lie."],"id":579,"m":"By earth, she's only flesh and blood. There you lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Her amber hairs for foul hath amber quoted."],"id":580,"m":"Her amber hair has shown that amber is ugly by comparison."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["An amber-coloured raven was well noted."],"id":581,"m":"An amber-coloured raven: well observed."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["As upright as the cedar."],"id":582,"m":"As straight as the cedar."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Stoop, I say.","Her shoulder is with child."],"id":583,"m":"Bending, I'd say. Her shoulder is pregnant (she's hunched)."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["As fair as day."],"id":584,"m":"As fair as day."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Ay, as some days, but then no sun must shine."],"id":585,"m":"Yes, as some days, but ones when the sun doesn't shine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["O, that I had my wish!"],"id":586,"m":"Oh, if only I had my wish!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["And I had mine!"],"id":587,"m":"And I had mine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And I mine too, good Lord!"],"id":588,"m":"And I mine too, good Lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Amen, so I had mine. Is not that a good word?"],"id":589,"m":"Amen, if I had mine. Isn't that a good word?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["I would forget her; but a fever she","Reigns in my blood, and will remembered be."],"id":590,"m":"I'd like to forget her; but she rules in my blood like a fever and won't be forgotten."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A fever in your blood? Why, then incision","Would let her out in saucers. Sweet misprision!"],"id":591,"m":"A fever in your blood? Why, then, cutting a vein would let her out into bowls. A sweet mistake!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Once more I'll read the ode that I have writ."],"id":592,"m":"I'll read the poem I've written once more."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Once more I'll mark how love can vary wit."],"id":593,"m":"I'll watch once more how love can vary a man's wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["[Dumaine reads his sonnet]","On a day—alack the day!—","Love, whose month is ever May,","Spied a blossom passing fair","Playing in the wanton air.","Through the velvet leaves the wind,","All unseen, can passage find;","That the lover, sick to death,","Wished himself the heaven's breath.","\"Air,\" quoth he, \"thy cheeks may blow;","Air, would I might triumph so!\"","But, alack, my hand is sworn","Ne'er to pluck thee from thy thorn.","Vow, alack, for youth unmeet,","Youth so apt to pluck a sweet.","Do not call it sin in me,","That I am forsworn for thee;","Thou for whom Jove would swear","Juno but an Ethiope were,","And deny himself for Jove,","Turning mortal for thy love.","This will I send, and something else more plain,","That shall express my true love's fasting pain.","O, would the King, Berowne and Longaville","Were lovers too! Ill, to example ill,","Would from my forehead wipe a perjured note,","For none offend where all alike do dote."],"id":594,"m":"[Dumaine reads his sonnet] 'One day (alas, that day!) Love, whose month is always May, saw a beautiful blossom playing in the wanton air. Through the velvet leaves, unseen, the wind found a way in, so that the lover, sick to death, wished he were the breath of heaven. \"Air,\" he said, \"you may blow on her cheeks; Air, I wish I could triumph like you!\" But, alas, my hand has sworn never to pluck you from your thorn: a vow, alas, unfit for youth, which is so ready to pluck sweetness. Don't call it a sin in me that I break my oath for you, you for whom Jove would swear that Juno is ugly as an Ethiopian, and would deny he was Jove, turning mortal for your love.' I'll send this, and something else plainer that will express the pain of my true love's starving. Oh, if only the King, Berowne and Longaville were in love too! Others' bad example would wipe the mark of a perjurer off my forehead, for no one is guilty where everyone dotes alike."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["[Comes forward] Dumaine, thy love is far from charity,","That in love's grief desir'st society.","You may look pale, but I should blush, I know,","To be o'erheard and taken napping so."],"id":595,"m":"[Comes forward] Dumaine, your love is far from charity, wanting company in love's grief. You may look pale, but I know I'd blush to be overheard and caught out like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Comes forward] Come, sir, you blush. As his, your case is such.","You chide at him, offending twice as much.","You do not love Maria? Longaville","Did never sonnet for her sake compile,","Nor never lay his wreathed arms athwart","His loving bosom to keep down his heart.","I have been closely shrouded in this bush,","And marked you both, and for you both did blush.","I heard your guilty rhymes, observed your fashion,","Saw sighs reek from you, noted well your passion.","\"Ay, me!\" says one. \"O Jove!\" the other cries.","One, her hairs were gold; crystal the other's eyes.","[To Longaville] You would for paradise break faith and troth;","[To Dumaine] And Jove, for your love would infringe an oath.","What will Berowne say when that he shall hear","Faith infringed which such zeal did swear?","How will he scorn, how will he spend his wit!","How will he triumph, leap, and laugh at it!","For all the wealth that ever I did see,","I would not have him know so much by me."],"id":596,"m":"[Comes forward] Come, sir, you blush. Your case is just like his. You scold him, while you're twice as guilty. You don't love Maria? Longaville never wrote a sonnet for her sake, never folded his arms across his loving chest to hold down his heart? I've been hidden closely in this bush and watched you both, and blushed for you both. I heard your guilty rhymes, watched how you behaved, saw sighs steam out of you, noted your passion well. 'Oh dear!' says one. 'Oh Jove!' cries the other. One says her hair is gold; the other that her eyes are crystal. [To Longaville] You'd break your faith and word for paradise; [To Dumaine] and Jove would break an oath for your love. What will Berowne say when he hears about a promise broken that was sworn so passionately? How he'll mock, how he'll spend his wit on it! How he'll triumph, jump about and laugh at it! For all the riches I've ever seen, I wouldn't want him to know as much about me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["[Comes forward]","Now step I forth to whip hypocrisy.","Ah, good my liege, I pray thee pardon me.","Good heart, what grace hast thou thus to reprove","These worms for loving, that art most in love?","Your eyes do make no coaches; in your tears","There is no certain princess that appears.","You'll not be perjured, 'tis a hateful thing:","Tush, none but minstrels like of sonneting!","But are you not ashamed? Nay, are you not,","All three of you, to be thus much o'ershot?","You found his mote, the King your mote did see;","But I a beam do find in each of three.","O, what a scene of foolery have I seen,","Of sighs, of groans, of sorrow, and of teen!","O me, with what strict patience have I sat,","To see a king transformed to a gnat!","To see great Hercules whipping a gig,","And profound Solomon to tune a jig,","And Nestor play at push-pin with the boys,","And critic Timon laugh at idle toys.","Where lies thy grief, O, tell me, good Dumaine?","And, gentle Longaville, where lies thy pain?","And where my liege's? All about the breast?","A caudle, ho!"],"id":597,"m":"[Comes forward] Now I step out to whip hypocrisy. Ah, my good lord, forgive me, please. Good heart, what right have you to scold these worms for loving, when you're the most in love of all? Your eyes don't make coaches; no particular princess appears in your tears. You won't break your oath; it's a hateful thing. Pah, only minstrels like writing sonnets! But aren't you ashamed? No, aren't you all three ashamed to be so caught out? You found the speck in his eye, the King saw the speck in yours; but I find a plank in each of the three. Oh, what a scene of foolishness I've watched, of sighs, groans, sorrow and suffering! Oh my, with what strict patience I have sat to see a king turned into a gnat, to see great Hercules spinning a top, and wise Solomon singing a jig, and old Nestor playing children's games with the boys, and the scornful Timon laughing at silly toys! Where is your grief? Oh, tell me, good Dumaine. And, gentle Longaville, where is your pain? And where is my lord's? All around the heart? A hot drink for the sick, quickly!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Too bitter is thy jest.","Are we betrayed thus to thy over-view?"],"id":598,"m":"Your joke is too bitter. Have we been given away like this to your spying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Not you to me, but I betrayed by you.","I that am honest, I that hold it sin","To break the vow I am engaged in.","I am betrayed by keeping company","With men like you, men of inconstancy.","When shall you see me write a thing in rhyme?","Or groan for Joan? Or spend a minute's time","In pruning me? When shall you hear that I","Will praise a hand, a foot, a face, an eye,","A gait, a state, a brow, a breast, a waist,","A leg, a limb—"],"id":599,"m":"Not you to me, but I betrayed by you. I, who am honest, who think it a sin to break the vow I'm bound by, am betrayed by keeping company with men like you, inconstant men. When will you ever see me write anything in rhyme? Or groan for some Joan? Or spend a minute prettying myself up? When will you hear me praise a hand, a foot, a face, an eye, a walk, a bearing, a brow, a breast, a waist, a leg, a limb..."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Soft! Whither away so fast?","A true man, or a thief, that gallops so?"],"id":600,"m":"Wait! Where are you off to so fast? Is it an honest man or a thief who gallops like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I post from love. Good lover, let me go."],"id":601,"m":"I'm running away from love. Good lover, let me go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Jaquenetta, with a letter, and Costard.","id":602},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["God bless the King!"],"id":603,"m":"God bless the King!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What present hast thou there?"],"id":604,"m":"What gift have you got there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Some certain treason."],"id":605,"m":"Some sort of treason."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What makes treason here?"],"id":606,"m":"What's treason doing here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Nay, it makes nothing, sir."],"id":607,"m":"It's not doing anything, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["If it mar nothing neither,","The treason and you go in peace away together."],"id":608,"m":"If it isn't undoing anything either, then the treason and you can go away in peace together."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["I beseech your Grace, let this letter be read.","Our person misdoubts it; 'twas treason, he said."],"id":609,"m":"Please, Your Grace, have this letter read. Our parson suspects it; he said it was treason."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Berowne, read it over."],"id":610,"m":"Berowne, read it through."},{"t":"sd","text":"Berowne reads the letter.","id":611},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Where hadst thou it?"],"id":612,"m":"Where did you get it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JAQUENETTA","lines":["Of Costard."],"id":613,"m":"From Costard."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Where hadst thou it?"],"id":614,"m":"Where did you get it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Of Dun Adramadio, Dun Adramadio."],"id":615,"m":"From Dun Adramadio, Dun Adramadio."},{"t":"sd","text":"Berowne tears the letter.","id":616},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How now, what is in you? Why dost thou tear it?"],"id":617,"m":"What's the matter with you? Why are you tearing it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A toy, my liege, a toy. Your Grace needs not fear it."],"id":618,"m":"It's a trifle, my lord, a trifle. Your Grace needn't worry about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["It did move him to passion, and therefore let's hear it."],"id":619,"m":"It made him very upset, so let's hear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["[Picking up the pieces]","It is Berowne's writing, and here is his name."],"id":620,"m":"[Picking up the pieces] It's Berowne's handwriting, and here's his name."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["[To Costard] Ah, you whoreson loggerhead, you were born to do me","shame.","Guilty, my lord, guilty. I confess, I confess."],"id":621,"m":"[To Costard] Ah, you bastard blockhead, you were born to disgrace me. Guilty, my lord, guilty. I confess, I confess."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What?"],"id":622,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["That you three fools lacked me fool to make up the mess.","He, he, and you—and you, my liege—and I","Are pick-purses in love, and we deserve to die.","O, dismiss this audience, and I shall tell you more."],"id":623,"m":"That you three fools needed me, a fool, to make up the set of four. He, he and you (and you, my lord) and I are pickpockets in love, and we deserve to die. Oh, send these people away and I'll tell you more."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Now the number is even."],"id":624,"m":"Now the number is even."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["True, true, we are four.","Will these turtles be gone?"],"id":625,"m":"True, true, we are four. Will these lovebirds go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Hence, sirs, away!"],"id":626,"m":"Off with you, go!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Walk aside the true folk, and let the traitors stay."],"id":627,"m":"Let the honest folk walk away, and let the traitors stay."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Costard and Jaquenetta.","id":628},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Sweet lords, sweet lovers, O, let us embrace!","As true we are as flesh and blood can be.","The sea will ebb and flow, heaven show his face;","Young blood doth not obey an old decree.","We cannot cross the cause why we were born;","Therefore of all hands must we be forsworn."],"id":629,"m":"Sweet lords, sweet lovers, oh, let's embrace! We're as true as flesh and blood can be. The sea will ebb and flow, the sky show its face; young blood doesn't obey an old decree. We can't go against the reason we were born; so, whichever way we turn, we must break our oaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What, did these rent lines show some love of thine?"],"id":630,"m":"What, did these torn lines show some love of yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["\"Did they?\" quoth you? Who sees the heavenly Rosaline","That, like a rude and savage man of Ind,","At the first op'ning of the gorgeous east,","Bows not his vassal head and, strucken blind,","Kisses the base ground with obedient breast?","What peremptory eagle-sighted eye","Dares look upon the heaven of her brow","That is not blinded by her majesty?"],"id":631,"m":"'Did they?' you ask? Who can see the heavenly Rosaline and not, like a rough savage from India at the first opening of the gorgeous dawn, bow his humble head and, struck blind, kiss the lowly ground with an obedient chest? What bold, eagle-eyed eye dares to look at the heaven of her brow and not be blinded by her majesty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What zeal, what fury hath inspired thee now?","My love, her mistress, is a gracious moon;","She, an attending star, scarce seen a light."],"id":632,"m":"What passion, what frenzy has inspired you now? My love, her mistress, is a gracious moon; she's an attending star, barely seen as a light."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["My eyes are then no eyes, nor I Berowne.","O, but for my love, day would turn to night!","Of all complexions the culled sovereignty","Do meet as at a fair in her fair cheek,","Where several worthies make one dignity,","Where nothing wants that want itself doth seek.","Lend me the flourish of all gentle tongues—","Fie, painted rhetoric! O, she needs it not.","To things of sale a seller's praise belongs.","She passes praise; then praise too short doth blot.","A withered hermit, five-score winters worn,","Might shake off fifty, looking in her eye.","Beauty doth varnish age, as if new born,","And gives the crutch the cradle's infancy.","O, 'tis the sun that maketh all things shine!"],"id":633,"m":"Then my eyes aren't eyes, and I'm not Berowne. Oh, if it weren't for my love, day would turn to night! The pick of all the best complexions meet in her fair cheek as if at a fair, where many good qualities make one perfection, where nothing is lacking that desire itself could want. Lend me the decoration of every fine tongue... No, away with painted rhetoric! Oh, she doesn't need it. A seller's praise belongs to things for sale. She is beyond praise; so praise that falls short only stains her. A withered hermit, worn by a hundred winters, could shake off fifty of them by looking in her eyes. Beauty varnishes old age as if it were newborn, and gives the man on crutches the childhood of the cradle. Oh, she is the sun that makes everything shine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["By heaven, thy love is black as ebony."],"id":634,"m":"By heaven, your love is as black as ebony."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Is ebony like her? O word divine!","A wife of such wood were felicity.","O, who can give an oath? Where is a book?","That I may swear beauty doth beauty lack","If that she learn not of her eye to look.","No face is fair that is not full so black."],"id":635,"m":"Is ebony like her? Oh, divine word! A wife made of such wood would be bliss. Oh, who can give an oath? Where's a Bible? So that I can swear that beauty lacks beauty unless it learns how to look from her eyes. No face is fair that isn't just as black."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O paradox! Black is the badge of hell,","The hue of dungeons and the school of night;","And beauty's crest becomes the heavens well."],"id":636,"m":"Oh, what a paradox! Black is the badge of hell, the colour of dungeons and the gown of night; and beauty's true emblem is the bright sky."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Devils soonest tempt, resembling spirits of light.","O, if in black my lady's brows be decked,","It mourns that painting and usurping hair","Should ravish doters with a false aspect;","And therefore is she born to make black fair.","Her favour turns the fashion of the days,","For native blood is counted painting now;","And therefore red, that would avoid dispraise,","Paints itself black, to imitate her brow."],"id":637,"m":"Devils tempt soonest when they look like spirits of light. Oh, if my lady's brows are dressed in black, it's because they mourn that make-up and false hair should seduce fools with a fake appearance; and so she was born to make black beautiful. Her looks change the fashion of the times, for natural colour is now thought to be paint; and so red cheeks, to avoid criticism, paint themselves black to imitate her brow."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["To look like her are chimney-sweepers black."],"id":638,"m":"Chimney sweeps are black to look like her."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["And since her time are colliers counted bright."],"id":639,"m":"And since she came along, coal-miners are counted handsome."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And Ethiopes of their sweet complexion crack."],"id":640,"m":"And Ethiopians boast of their lovely complexion."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Dark needs no candles now, for dark is light."],"id":641,"m":"Darkness doesn't need candles now, because dark is light."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Your mistresses dare never come in rain,","For fear their colours should be washed away."],"id":642,"m":"Your mistresses never dare go out in the rain, for fear their colours would be washed off."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["'Twere good yours did; for, sir, to tell you plain,","I'll find a fairer face not washed today."],"id":643,"m":"It would be good if yours did; for, sir, to be honest, I'll find a fairer face that hasn't been washed today."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I'll prove her fair, or talk till doomsday here."],"id":644,"m":"I'll prove she's fair, or talk here until doomsday."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["No devil will fright thee then so much as she."],"id":645,"m":"Then no devil will frighten you as much as she does."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["I never knew man hold vile stuff so dear."],"id":646,"m":"I never knew a man to value such poor stuff so highly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["[Showing his shoe]","Look, here's thy love, my foot and her face see."],"id":647,"m":"[Showing his shoe] Look, here's your love: see my foot and her face (both black)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["O, if the streets were paved with thine eyes,","Her feet were much too dainty for such tread."],"id":648,"m":"Oh, if the streets were paved with your eyes, her feet would be too dainty to tread on them."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["O vile! Then, as she goes, what upward lies","The street should see as she walked over head."],"id":649,"m":"Oh, how vile! Then, as she walked, the street would see what's up under her skirts."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["But what of this? Are we not all in love?"],"id":650,"m":"But what of all this? Aren't we all in love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Nothing so sure, and thereby all forsworn."],"id":651,"m":"Nothing is surer, and so we've all broken our oaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then leave this chat, and, good Berowne, now prove","Our loving lawful, and our faith not torn."],"id":652,"m":"Then leave this chatter, and, good Berowne, prove now that our loving is lawful and our faith is not broken."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Ay, marry, there; some flattery for this evil."],"id":653,"m":"Yes indeed, that's it; some flattering excuse for this evil."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["O, some authority how to proceed.","Some tricks, some quillets, how to cheat the devil."],"id":654,"m":"Oh, some authority to tell us how to go on; some tricks, some quibbles, to cheat the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Some salve for perjury."],"id":655,"m":"Some ointment for perjury."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["O, 'tis more than need.","Have at you, then, affection's men-at-arms.","Consider what you first did swear unto:","To fast, to study, and to see no woman—","Flat treason 'gainst the kingly state of youth.","Say, can you fast? Your stomachs are too young,","And abstinence engenders maladies.","O, we have made a vow to study, lords,","And in that vow we have forsworn our books;","For when would you, my liege, or you, or you,","In leaden contemplation have found out","Such fiery numbers as the prompting eyes","Of beauty's tutors have enriched you with?","Other slow arts entirely keep the brain,","And therefore, finding barren practisers,","Scarce show a harvest of their heavy toil;","But love, first learned in a lady's eyes,","Lives not alone immured in the brain,","But with the motion of all elements","Courses as swift as thought in every power,","And gives to every power a double power,","Above their functions and their offices.","It adds a precious seeing to the eye.","A lover's eyes will gaze an eagle blind.","A lover's ear will hear the lowest sound,","When the suspicious head of theft is stopped.","Love's feeling is more soft and sensible","Than are the tender horns of cockled snails.","Love's tongue proves dainty Bacchus gross in taste.","For valour, is not Love a Hercules,","Still climbing trees in the Hesperides?","Subtle as Sphinx, as sweet and musical","As bright Apollo's lute, strung with his hair.","And when Love speaks, the voice of all the gods","Make heaven drowsy with the harmony.","Never durst poet touch a pen to write","Until his ink were tempered with Love's sighs.","O, then his lines would ravish savage ears","And plant in tyrants mild humility.","From women's eyes this doctrine I derive.","They sparkle still the right Promethean fire;","They are the books, the arts, the academes,","That show, contain, and nourish, all the world;","Else none at all in aught proves excellent.","Then fools you were these women to forswear,","Or, keeping what is sworn, you will prove fools.","For wisdom's sake, a word that all men love,","Or for love's sake, a word that loves all men,","Or for men's sake, the authors of these women,","Or women's sake, by whom we men are men,","Let us once lose our oaths to find ourselves,","Or else we lose ourselves to keep our oaths.","It is religion to be thus forsworn,","For charity itself fulfils the law,","And who can sever love from charity?"],"id":656,"m":"Oh, there's more than enough. Here goes, then, love's soldiers. Think what you first swore to do: to fast, to study and to see no woman. That's outright treason against the royal state of youth. Tell me, can you fast? Your stomachs are too young, and going without breeds diseases. Oh, we made a vow to study, lords, and in that vow we gave up our real books. For when would you, my lord, or you, or you, in dull, leaden thinking, ever have come up with such fiery verses as the inspiring eyes of beauty's tutors have given you? Other slow kinds of learning stay entirely in the brain, and so, finding barren students, hardly show a harvest for their heavy work. But love, first learned in a lady's eyes, doesn't live shut up in the brain alone. It moves as fast as thought through every faculty, like the elements, and gives each faculty double power, beyond its normal work. It adds precious sight to the eye: a lover's eyes can outstare an eagle. A lover's ear can hear the faintest sound, when even a suspicious thief hears nothing. Love's touch is softer and more sensitive than the tender horns of snails. Love's tongue makes dainty Bacchus seem crude in his taste. For courage, isn't Love a Hercules, still climbing trees in the garden of the Hesperides? It is as subtle as the Sphinx, as sweet and musical as bright Apollo's lute strung with his golden hair. And when Love speaks, the voices of all the gods make heaven drowsy with their harmony. No poet ever dared to touch a pen to write until his ink was mixed with Love's sighs; oh, then his lines would enchant savage ears and plant gentle humility in tyrants. This is the lesson I draw from women's eyes. They always sparkle with the true fire of Prometheus; they are the books, the arts, the academies that show, contain and nourish the whole world. Without them, no one is excellent at anything. So you were fools to give up these women, and if you keep your oath you'll prove yourselves fools. For wisdom's sake, a word all men love; or for love's sake, a word that loves all men; or for men's sake, who made these women; or for women's sake, who make us men: let's lose our oaths once to find ourselves, or else we lose ourselves to keep our oaths. It is a religious act to break our oaths like this; for love itself fulfils the law, and who can separate love from charity?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Saint Cupid, then, and, soldiers, to the field!"],"id":657,"m":"Then Saint Cupid, and to the battlefield, soldiers!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Advance your standards, and upon them, lords!","Pell-mell, down with them! But be first advised","In conflict that you get the sun of them."],"id":658,"m":"Raise your banners, and at them, lords! Charge, down with them! But make sure first that in the fight you get the sun behind you (in their eyes)."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Now to plain dealing. Lay these glozes by.","Shall we resolve to woo these girls of France?"],"id":659,"m":"Now let's talk plainly. Put these fine glosses aside. Shall we resolve to woo these girls from France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And win them too. Therefore let us devise","Some entertainment for them in their tents."],"id":660,"m":"And win them too. So let's plan some entertainment for them in their tents."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["First, from the park let us conduct them thither.","Then homeward every man attach the hand","Of his fair mistress. In the afternoon","We will with some strange pastime solace them,","Such as the shortness of the time can shape;","For revels, dances, masques, and merry hours","Forerun fair Love, strewing her way with flowers."],"id":661,"m":"First let's escort them there from the park. Then on the way home let each man take the hand of his fair mistress. In the afternoon we'll entertain them with some unusual amusement, as much as the short time allows; for revels, dances, masques and merry hours go in front of fair Love, scattering flowers in her path."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Away, away! No time shall be omitted","That will betime and may by us be fitted."],"id":662,"m":"Away, away! We won't waste a moment of the time we have that can be used."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Allons! allons! Sowed cockle reaped no corn,","And justice always whirls in equal measure.","Light wenches may prove plagues to men forsworn;","If so, our copper buys no better treasure."],"id":663,"m":"Let's go! Let's go! Sow weeds and you don't reap corn, and justice always turns around fairly. Loose girls may turn out to be a plague to men who break their oaths; if so, our copper coins buy no better treasure."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":664},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"The King of Navarre's park.","id":665},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Holofernes, Sir Nathaniel and Dull.","id":666},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Satis quod sufficit."],"id":667,"m":"'Satis quod sufficit': enough is as good as a feast."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["I praise God for you, sir. Your reasons at dinner have been sharp and","sententious, pleasant without scurrility, witty without affection,","audacious without impudency, learned without opinion, and strange","without heresy. I did converse this quondam day with a companion of","the King's, who is intituled, nominated, or called, Don Adriano de","Armado."],"id":668,"m":"I praise God for you, sir. Your conversation at dinner has been sharp and full of wise sayings, pleasant without being rude, witty without being affected, bold without being impudent, learned without being opinionated, and original without being heretical. The other day I was talking with a companion of the King's, who is entitled, named or called Don Adriano de Armado."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Novi hominem tanquam te. His humour is lofty, his discourse","peremptory, his tongue filed, his eye ambitious, his gait majestical","and his general behaviour vain, ridiculous, and thrasonical. He is too","picked, too spruce, too affected, too odd, as it were, too peregrinate,","as I may call it."],"id":669,"m":"'Novi hominem tanquam te': I know the man as well as I know you. His manner is lofty, his speech overbearing, his tongue polished, his eye ambitious, his walk majestic and his general behaviour vain, ridiculous and boastful. He is too refined, too spruce, too affected, too odd, as it were, too much the traveller, as I might call it."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["A most singular and choice epithet."],"id":670,"m":"A most remarkable and choice description."},{"t":"sd","text":"Draws out his table-book.","id":671},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["He draweth out the thread of his verbosity finer than the staple of his","argument. I abhor such fanatical phantasimes, such insociable and","point-devise companions, such rackers of orthography, as to speak","\"dout\" sine \"b\", when he should say \"doubt\", \"det\" when he should","pronounce \"debt\"—d, e, b, t, not d, e, t. He clepeth a calf \"cauf\",","half \"hauf\"; neighbour vocatur \"nebour\", neigh abbreviated \"ne\". This","is abhominable, which he would call \"abominable\". It insinuateth me of","insanie. Ne intelligis, domine? To make frantic, lunatic."],"id":672,"m":"He draws out the thread of his wordiness finer than the substance of his argument. I loathe such fantastic weirdos, such unsociable and over-precise fellows, such torturers of spelling, who say 'dout' without the 'b' when he should say 'doubt', and 'det' when he should pronounce 'debt': d, e, b, t, not d, e, t. He calls a calf a 'cauf', half 'hauf'; 'neighbour' he calls 'nebour', and 'neigh' is shortened to 'ne'. This is abhominable (which he would call 'abominable'). It drives me to insanie. 'Ne intelligis, domine?' Do you understand, sir? To make frantic, lunatic."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Laus Deo, bone intelligo."],"id":673,"m":"'Laus Deo, bone intelligo': praise God, I understand well."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Bone? Bone for bene? Priscian a little scratched; 'twill serve."],"id":674,"m":"'Bone'? 'Bone' for 'bene'? Priscian's grammar is a little scratched; it'll do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Armado, Moth and Costard.","id":675},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Videsne quis venit?"],"id":676,"m":"'Videsne quis venit?' Do you see who's coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Video, et gaudeo."],"id":677,"m":"'Video, et gaudeo': I see, and rejoice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Chirrah!"],"id":678,"m":"Chirrah! (He means the Greek greeting 'chaire', or mispronounces 'sirrah'.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Quare \"chirrah\", not \"sirrah\"?"],"id":679,"m":"'Quare', why, 'chirrah' and not 'sirrah'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Men of peace, well encountered."],"id":680,"m":"Men of peace, well met."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Most military sir, salutation."],"id":681,"m":"Most military sir, greetings."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["[Aside to Costard] They have been at a great feast of languages and","stolen the scraps."],"id":682,"m":"[Aside to Costard] They've been at a great feast of languages and stolen the leftovers."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["O, they have lived long on the almsbasket of words. I marvel thy master","hath not eaten thee for a word, for thou art not so long by the head as","honorificabilitudinitatibus. Thou art easier swallowed than a","flap-dragon."],"id":683,"m":"Oh, they've lived for a long time on the scrap basket of words. I'm amazed your master hasn't eaten you up for a word, because you're not as long from head to foot as 'honorificabilitudinitatibus'. You'd be easier to swallow than a flaming raisin in a drinking game."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Peace! The peal begins."],"id":684,"m":"Quiet! The bells are starting to ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["[To Holofernes] Monsieur, are you not lettered?"],"id":685,"m":"[To Holofernes] Monsieur, aren't you educated?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Yes, yes, he teaches boys the hornbook. What is a, b, spelt backward","with the horn on his head?"],"id":686,"m":"Yes, yes, he teaches boys their alphabet. What is 'a, b' spelt backwards, with a horn on its head?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Ba, pueritia, with a horn added."],"id":687,"m":"'Ba', 'pueritia' (child), with a horn added."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Ba, most silly sheep with a horn. You hear his learning."],"id":688,"m":"'Baa', you very silly sheep with a horn. You hear how learned he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Quis, quis, thou consonant?"],"id":689,"m":"'Quis, quis', who, who, you consonant (you nobody)?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["The third of the five vowels, if you repeat them; or the fifth, if I."],"id":690,"m":"The third of the five vowels, if you say them; or the fifth, if I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I will repeat them: a, e, i—"],"id":691,"m":"I'll say them: a, e, I..."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["The sheep. The other two concludes it: o, u."],"id":692,"m":"The sheep (you're the ewe, the 'I'). The other two finish it: o, u (oh, you)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Now, by the salt wave of the Mediterraneum, a sweet touch, a quick","venue of wit! Snip, snap, quick and home! It rejoiceth my intellect.","True wit!"],"id":693,"m":"Now, by the salty waves of the Mediterranean, a sweet touch, a quick fencing hit of wit! Snip, snap, quick and right on target! It delights my intellect. True wit!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Offered by a child to an old man—which is wit-old."],"id":694,"m":"Offered by a child to an old man, which is 'wit-old' (wittol: a willing cuckold)."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["What is the figure? What is the figure?"],"id":695,"m":"What's the figure of speech? What's the figure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Horns."],"id":696,"m":"Horns."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Thou disputes like an infant. Go whip thy gig."],"id":697,"m":"You argue like a baby. Go and spin your top."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Lend me your horn to make one, and I will whip about your infamy unum","cita. A gig of a cuckold's horn."],"id":698,"m":"Lend me your horn to make one, and I'll whip your shame around 'unum cita' (he means 'circum circa', round and round). A top made of a cuckold's horn!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["An I had but one penny in the world, thou shouldst have it to buy","gingerbread. Hold, there is the very remuneration I had of thy master,","thou halfpenny purse of wit, thou pigeon-egg of discretion. O, an the","heavens were so pleased that thou wert but my bastard, what a joyful","father wouldst thou make me! Go to, thou hast it ad dunghill, at the","fingers' ends, as they say."],"id":699,"m":"If I had only one penny in the world, you'd have it to buy gingerbread. Here, there's the very remuneration I got from your master, you halfpenny purse of wit, you pigeon's egg of good sense. Oh, if heaven had made you my bastard, what a happy father you'd make me! Go on, you have it 'ad dunghill', at your fingertips, as they say."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["O, I smell false Latin! Dunghill for unguem."],"id":700,"m":"Oh, I smell bad Latin! 'Dunghill' for 'unguem' (ad unguem: to a fingernail, perfectly)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Arts-man, preambulate. We will be singuled from the barbarous. Do you","not educate youth at the charge-house on the top of the mountain?"],"id":701,"m":"Man of learning, walk ahead. We will separate ourselves from the barbarians. Don't you teach young people at the school on the top of the mountain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Or mons, the hill."],"id":702,"m":"Or 'mons', the hill."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["At your sweet pleasure, for the mountain."],"id":703,"m":"As you like, for 'mountain'."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I do, sans question."],"id":704,"m":"I do, 'sans', without, question."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sir, it is the King's most sweet pleasure and affection to congratulate","the Princess at her pavilion in the posteriors of this day, which the","rude multitude call the afternoon."],"id":705,"m":"Sir, it is the King's sweet pleasure and wish to pay his respects to the Princess at her pavilion in the posteriors of this day, which the rough masses call the afternoon."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["The posterior of the day, most generous sir, is liable, congruent, and","measurable for the afternoon. The word is well culled, chose, sweet,","and apt, I do assure you, sir, I do assure."],"id":706,"m":"The posterior of the day, most noble sir, is suitable, fitting and appropriate for the afternoon. The word is well picked, chosen, sweet and apt, I assure you, sir, I assure you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sir, the King is a noble gentleman, and my familiar, I do assure ye,","very good friend. For what is inward between us, let it pass. I do","beseech thee, remember thy courtesy; I beseech thee, apparel thy head.","And among other importunate and most serious designs, and of great","import indeed, too—but let that pass. For I must tell thee it will","please his Grace, by the world, sometime to lean upon my poor shoulder","and with his royal finger thus dally with my excrement, with my","mustachio. But, sweet heart, let that pass. By the world, I recount no","fable! Some certain special honours it pleaseth his greatness to impart","to Armado, a soldier, a man of travel, that hath seen the world. But","let that pass. The very all of all is—but, sweet heart, I do implore","secrecy—that the King would have me present the Princess, sweet chuck,","with some delightful ostentation, or show, or pageant, or antic, or","firework. Now, understanding that the curate and your sweet self are","good at such eruptions and sudden breaking-out of mirth, as it were, I","have acquainted you withal, to the end to crave your assistance."],"id":707,"m":"Sir, the King is a noble gentleman and an intimate friend of mine, I assure you, a very good friend. As for what is private between us, let it pass. I beg you, remember your courtesy; I beg you, put your hat back on. And among other pressing and very serious plans, and of great importance indeed... but let that pass. For I must tell you, it pleases His Grace, by the world, sometimes to lean on my poor shoulder and play with my excrement, that is, my moustache, with his royal finger, like this. But, sweetheart, let that pass. By the world, I am not telling tales! It pleases His Greatness to grant some special honours to Armado, a soldier, a traveller, who has seen the world. But let that pass. The be-all and end-all is (but, sweetheart, I beg you to keep it secret) that the King wants me to present the Princess, sweet chick, with some delightful display, or show, or pageant, or comic dance, or fireworks. Now, knowing that the curate and your sweet self are good at such eruptions and sudden outbursts of fun, as it were, I have told you about it in order to ask your help."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Sir, you shall present before her the Nine Worthies. Sir Nathaniel, as","concerning some entertainment of time, some show in the posterior of","this day, to be rendered by our assistance, the King's command, and","this most gallant, illustrate, and learned gentleman, before the","Princess, I say, none so fit as to present the Nine Worthies."],"id":708,"m":"Sir, you shall present the Nine Worthies before her. Sir Nathaniel, as for some entertainment, some show in the posterior of this day, to be performed with our help, at the King's command and by this most gallant, famous and learned gentleman, before the Princess: I say nothing is as fitting as to present the Nine Worthies."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Where will you find men worthy enough to present them?"],"id":709,"m":"Where will you find men worthy enough to play them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Joshua, yourself; myself; and this gallant gentleman, Judas Maccabaeus.","This swain, because of his great limb or joint, shall pass Pompey the","Great; the page, Hercules."],"id":710,"m":"Joshua, yourself; myself, and this gallant gentleman, Judas Maccabaeus; this peasant, because of his big limbs and joints, will pass for Pompey the Great; the page, Hercules."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Pardon, sir; error. He is not quantity enough for that Worthy's thumb;","he is not so big as the end of his club."],"id":711,"m":"Excuse me, sir: error. He isn't big enough for that Worthy's thumb; he isn't as big as the end of his club."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Shall I have audience? He shall present Hercules in minority. His enter","and exit shall be strangling a snake; and I will have an apology for","that purpose."],"id":712,"m":"May I be heard? He will play Hercules as a child. His entrance and exit will show him strangling a snake, and I'll have an explanatory speech for the purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["An excellent device! So, if any of the audience hiss, you may cry \"Well","done, Hercules, now thou crushest the snake!\" That is the way to make","an offence gracious, though few have the grace to do it."],"id":713,"m":"An excellent plan! Then if anyone in the audience hisses, you can shout, 'Well done, Hercules, now you're crushing the snake!' That's the way to make an offence look graceful, though few have the grace to do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["For the rest of the Worthies?"],"id":714,"m":"And the rest of the Worthies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I will play three myself."],"id":715,"m":"I'll play three myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Thrice-worthy gentleman!"],"id":716,"m":"Thrice-worthy gentleman!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Shall I tell you a thing?"],"id":717,"m":"Shall I tell you something?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["We attend."],"id":718,"m":"We're listening."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["We will have, if this fadge not, an antic. I beseech you, follow."],"id":719,"m":"If this doesn't work, we'll have a comic dance. Please, follow me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Via, goodman Dull! Thou has spoken no word all this while."],"id":720,"m":"Come on, goodman Dull! You haven't said a word all this time."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["Nor understood none neither, sir."],"id":721,"m":"Nor understood one either, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Allons! we will employ thee."],"id":722,"m":"Let's go! We'll give you a part."},{"t":"sp","who":"DULL","lines":["I'll make one in a dance, or so; or I will play on the tabor to the","Worthies, and let them dance the hay."],"id":723,"m":"I'll join in a dance, or something; or I'll play the drum for the Worthies and let them dance a country dance."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Most dull, honest Dull! To our sport, away."],"id":724,"m":"Most dull, honest Dull! To our entertainment, away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":725},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. Before the Princess's pavilion.","id":726},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Princess, Rosaline, Katharine and Maria.","id":727},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Sweet hearts, we shall be rich ere we depart,","If fairings come thus plentifully in.","A lady walled about with diamonds!","Look you what I have from the loving King."],"id":728,"m":"Sweethearts, we'll be rich before we leave if presents keep coming in like this. A lady walled in with diamonds! Look what I've had from the loving King."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Madam, came nothing else along with that?"],"id":729,"m":"Madam, didn't anything else come with it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Nothing but this? Yes, as much love in rhyme","As would be crammed up in a sheet of paper","Writ o' both sides the leaf, margent and all,","That he was fain to seal on Cupid's name."],"id":730,"m":"Nothing but this? Yes, as much love in rhyme as could be crammed onto a sheet of paper, written on both sides, margins and all, so that he had to seal it on Cupid's name."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["That was the way to make his godhead wax,","For he hath been five thousand years a boy."],"id":731,"m":"That was the way to make the god grow bigger (as wax), because he has been a boy for five thousand years."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Ay, and a shrewd unhappy gallows too."],"id":732,"m":"Yes, and a nasty, mischievous rascal too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["You'll ne'er be friends with him. He killed your sister."],"id":733,"m":"You'll never be friends with him. He killed your sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["He made her melancholy, sad, and heavy;","And so she died. Had she been light, like you,","Of such a merry, nimble, stirring spirit,","She might ha' been a grandam ere she died.","And so may you, for a light heart lives long."],"id":734,"m":"He made her melancholy, sad and heavy-hearted, and so she died. If she had been light like you, with such a merry, nimble, lively spirit, she might have been a grandmother before she died. And so may you, because a light heart lives long."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["What's your dark meaning, mouse, of this light word?"],"id":735,"m":"What's your dark meaning, little mouse, in this light word?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["A light condition in a beauty dark."],"id":736,"m":"A light (wanton) character in a dark beauty."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["We need more light to find your meaning out."],"id":737,"m":"We need more light to work out your meaning."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["You'll mar the light by taking it in snuff;","Therefore I'll darkly end the argument."],"id":738,"m":"You'll spoil the light by taking it in snuff (taking offence, like a smoking candle-wick); so I'll end the argument in the dark."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Look what you do, you do it still i' th' dark."],"id":739,"m":"Whatever you do, you always do it in the dark."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["So do not you, for you are a light wench."],"id":740,"m":"You don't, because you're a light girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Indeed, I weigh not you, and therefore light."],"id":741,"m":"It's true, I don't weigh you, and so I'm light."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["You weigh me not? O, that's you care not for me."],"id":742,"m":"You don't weigh me? Oh, that means you don't care about me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Great reason, for past cure is still past care."],"id":743,"m":"With good reason, because what's past cure is past care."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Well bandied both; a set of wit well played.","But, Rosaline, you have a favour too.","Who sent it? And what is it?"],"id":744,"m":"Well volleyed, both of you; a set of wit well played. But Rosaline, you have a present too. Who sent it? And what is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["I would you knew.","An if my face were but as fair as yours,","My favour were as great. Be witness this.","Nay, I have verses too, I thank Berowne;","The numbers true, and, were the numbering too,","I were the fairest goddess on the ground.","I am compared to twenty thousand fairs.","O, he hath drawn my picture in his letter."],"id":745,"m":"I wish you knew. If my face were as fair as yours, my present would be as great. Look at this. No, I have verses too, thanks to Berowne. The metre is correct, and if the measuring of me were correct too, I'd be the fairest goddess on earth. I'm compared to twenty thousand beauties. Oh, he's drawn my portrait in his letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Anything like?"],"id":746,"m":"Anything like you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Much in the letters, nothing in the praise."],"id":747,"m":"Very like in the letters (black ink), nothing like in the praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Beauteous as ink: a good conclusion."],"id":748,"m":"As beautiful as ink: a good conclusion."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Fair as a text B in a copy-book."],"id":749,"m":"As fair as a black capital B in a copybook."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["'Ware pencils, how! Let me not die your debtor,","My red dominical, my golden letter.","O, that your face were not so full of O's!"],"id":750,"m":"Watch out for paintbrushes, there! I won't die in your debt, my red Sunday letter in the calendar, my gilded letter. Oh, if only your face weren't so full of O's (pockmarks)!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["A pox of that jest! And beshrew all shrews.","But, Katharine, what was sent to you from fair Dumaine?"],"id":751,"m":"A plague on that joke! And curse all shrews. But, Katharine, what did fair Dumaine send you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Madam, this glove."],"id":752,"m":"This glove, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Did he not send you twain?"],"id":753,"m":"Didn't he send you two?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Yes, madam, and moreover,","Some thousand verses of a faithful lover.","A huge translation of hypocrisy,","Vilely compiled, profound simplicity."],"id":754,"m":"Yes, madam, and on top of that, some thousand verses of a faithful lover: a huge translation of hypocrisy, wretchedly put together, deeply simple-minded."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["This, and these pearls, to me sent Longaville.","The letter is too long by half a mile."],"id":755,"m":"Longaville sent this to me, and these pearls. The letter is too long by half a mile."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["I think no less. Dost thou not wish in heart","The chain were longer and the letter short?"],"id":756,"m":"I quite believe it. Don't you wish in your heart that the chain were longer and the letter short?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Ay, or I would these hands might never part."],"id":757,"m":"Yes, or I wish these hands might never part."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["We are wise girls to mock our lovers so."],"id":758,"m":"We're clever girls, to mock our lovers like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["They are worse fools to purchase mocking so.","That same Berowne I'll torture ere I go.","O that I knew he were but in by th' week!","How I would make him fawn, and beg, and seek,","And wait the season, and observe the times,","And spend his prodigal wits in bootless rhymes,","And shape his service wholly to my hests,","And make him proud to make me proud that jests!","So pair-taunt-like would I o'ersway his state,","That he should be my fool, and I his fate."],"id":759,"m":"They're worse fools, to buy mockery at such a price. That Berowne, I'll torture him before I leave. Oh, if only I knew he was trapped for good! How I would make him fawn and beg and seek, and wait for the right season and keep to the right times, and waste his extravagant wits on useless rhymes, and shape all his service to my orders, and make him proud to make me proud, who only jest! I'd rule over his life so like a fateful planet that he would be my fool, and I his fate."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["None are so surely caught, when they are catched,","As wit turned fool. Folly, in wisdom hatched,","Hath wisdom's warrant and the help of school","And wit's own grace to grace a learned fool."],"id":760,"m":"No one is so firmly caught, once caught, as wit turned fool. Folly hatched in a wise man has wisdom's authority, the help of education and wit's own grace to grace a learned fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["The blood of youth burns not with such excess","As gravity's revolt to wantonness."],"id":761,"m":"The blood of youth doesn't burn as fiercely as seriousness when it rebels into wantonness."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Folly in fools bears not so strong a note","As fool'ry in the wise when wit doth dote,","Since all the power thereof it doth apply","To prove, by wit, worth in simplicity."],"id":762,"m":"Folly in fools is not as striking as foolishness in the wise when their wit dotes, since it uses all its power to prove, by wit, that silliness is worthwhile."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Boyet.","id":763},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Here comes Boyet, and mirth is in his face."],"id":764,"m":"Here comes Boyet, with laughter in his face."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["O, I am stabbed with laughter! Where's her Grace?"],"id":765,"m":"Oh, I'm stabbed with laughter! Where's Her Grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Thy news, Boyet?"],"id":766,"m":"Your news, Boyet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Prepare, madam, prepare!","Arm, wenches, arm! Encounters mounted are","Against your peace. Love doth approach disguised,","Armed in arguments. You'll be surprised.","Muster your wits, stand in your own defence,","Or hide your heads like cowards, and fly hence."],"id":767,"m":"Get ready, madam, get ready! To arms, girls, to arms! An attack is being mounted against your peace. Love is coming in disguise, armed with arguments. You'll be ambushed. Gather your wits, stand and defend yourselves, or hide your heads like cowards and run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Saint Denis to Saint Cupid! What are they","That charge their breath against us? Say, scout, say."],"id":768,"m":"Saint Denis against Saint Cupid! Who are they who aim their breath at us? Speak, scout, speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Under the cool shade of a sycamore","I thought to close mine eyes some half an hour,","When, lo, to interrupt my purposed rest,","Toward that shade I might behold addressed","The King and his companions. Warily","I stole into a neighbour thicket by,","And overheard what you shall overhear:","That, by and by, disguised they will be here.","Their herald is a pretty knavish page","That well by heart hath conned his embassage.","Action and accent did they teach him there:","\"Thus must thou speak,\" and \"thus thy body bear.\"","And ever and anon they made a doubt","Presence majestical would put him out;","\"For,\" quoth the King, \"an angel shalt thou see;","Yet fear not thou, but speak audaciously.\"","The boy replied \"An angel is not evil;","I should have feared her had she been a devil.\"","With that all laughed and clapped him on the shoulder,","Making the bold wag by their praises bolder.","One rubbed his elbow thus, and fleered, and swore","A better speech was never spoke before.","Another with his finger and his thumb","Cried \"Via, we will do 't, come what will come.\"","The third he capered, and cried \"All goes well!\"","The fourth turned on the toe, and down he fell.","With that they all did tumble on the ground,","With such a zealous laughter, so profound,","That in this spleen ridiculous appears,","To check their folly, passion's solemn tears."],"id":769,"m":"I was thinking of closing my eyes for half an hour in the cool shade of a sycamore, when, look, to interrupt my rest, I saw the King and his companions heading towards that shade. Carefully I crept into a thicket nearby and overheard what you're about to hear: that very soon they'll come here in disguise. Their messenger is a pretty, rascally page who has learned his message by heart. They were teaching him gestures and delivery there: 'This is how you must speak', and 'this is how you hold yourself'. And every so often they worried that a majestic presence would make him forget his lines. 'For,' said the King, 'you'll see an angel; but don't be afraid, speak out boldly.' The boy answered, 'An angel isn't evil; I'd have been afraid of her if she'd been a devil.' At that they all laughed and clapped him on the shoulder, making the cheeky boy bolder with their praise. One rubbed his elbow like this, grinned and swore a better speech had never been spoken. Another snapped his finger and thumb and cried, 'Come on, we'll do it, come what may.' The third danced about and cried, 'All's going well!' The fourth spun on his toe and fell over. At that they all tumbled on the ground with such hearty, deep laughter that in this fit of silliness solemn tears of emotion appeared, to check their folly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["But what, but what, come they to visit us?"],"id":770,"m":"But what, but what? Are they coming to visit us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["They do, they do, and are apparelled thus,","Like Muscovites, or Russians, as I guess.","Their purpose is to parley, court, and dance,","And every one his love-feat will advance","Unto his several mistress, which they'll know","By favours several which they did bestow."],"id":771,"m":"They are, they are, and they're dressed like this: as Muscovites, or Russians, I'd guess. They plan to talk, court and dance, and each one will press his love-suit on his own mistress, whom they'll recognise by the different presents they sent."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["And will they so? The gallants shall be tasked;","For, ladies, we will every one be masked,","And not a man of them shall have the grace,","Despite of suit, to see a lady's face.","Hold, Rosaline, this favour thou shalt wear,","And then the King will court thee for his dear.","Hold, take thou this, my sweet, and give me thine,","So shall Berowne take me for Rosaline.","And change you favours too; so shall your loves","Woo contrary, deceived by these removes."],"id":772,"m":"Will they really? These gallants shall be put to the test. For, ladies, we'll all be masked, and not one of them will be allowed, however much he begs, to see a lady's face. Here, Rosaline, you wear this present, and then the King will court you as his darling. Here, my sweet, take this, and give me yours; then Berowne will take me for Rosaline. And you two swap presents too; then your lovers, deceived by these exchanges, will woo the wrong ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Come on, then, wear the favours most in sight."],"id":773,"m":"Come on, then, wear the presents where they can be seen."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["But in this changing, what is your intent?"],"id":774,"m":"But what do you mean by this swapping?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["The effect of my intent is to cross theirs.","They do it but in mocking merriment,","And mock for mock is only my intent.","Their several counsels they unbosom shall","To loves mistook, and so be mocked withal","Upon the next occasion that we meet,","With visages displayed to talk and greet."],"id":775,"m":"What I mean by it is to cross their plan. They're only doing it as a mocking joke, and all I intend is mockery for mockery. They'll pour out their secret feelings to the wrong ladies, and so be mocked for it the next time we meet them, with our faces showing, to talk and greet."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["But shall we dance, if they desire us to't?"],"id":776,"m":"But shall we dance if they want us to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["No, to the death we will not move a foot,","Nor to their penned speech render we no grace,","But while 'tis spoke each turn away her face."],"id":777,"m":"No, not for our lives will we move a foot, and we won't honour their prepared speech either; while it's spoken, each of us will turn her face away."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Why, that contempt will kill the speaker's heart,","And quite divorce his memory from his part."],"id":778,"m":"Why, that contempt will break the speaker's heart and make him forget his part completely."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Therefore I do it, and I make no doubt","The rest will ne'er come in, if he be out.","There's no such sport as sport by sport o'erthrown,","To make theirs ours and ours none but our own.","So shall we stay, mocking intended game,","And they, well mocked, depart away with shame."],"id":779,"m":"That's why I'm doing it; and I've no doubt the rest will never come in if he dries up. There's no sport like sport overthrown by sport: making their game ours and keeping ours to ourselves. So we'll stay, mocking the game they planned, and they, well mocked, will leave in shame."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpet, within.","id":780},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["The trumpet sounds. Be masked; the maskers come."],"id":781,"m":"The trumpet sounds. Put on your masks; the masquers are coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"The Ladies mask.","id":782},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Blackamoors with music, Moth, with a speech, the King, Berowne, Longaville and Dumaine disguised.","id":783},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["All hail, the richest beauties on the earth!"],"id":784,"m":"All hail, the richest beauties on the earth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Beauties no richer than rich taffeta."],"id":785,"m":"Beauties no richer than rich silk (their masks)."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["A holy parcel of the fairest dames"],"id":786,"m":"A holy group of the fairest ladies"},{"t":"sd","text":"The Ladies turn their backs to him.","id":787},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["That ever turned their—backs—to mortal views!"],"id":788,"m":"that ever turned their... backs... to mortal eyes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Their eyes, villain, their eyes."],"id":789,"m":"Their eyes, you rogue, their eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["That ever turned their eyes to mortal views.","Out—"],"id":790,"m":"that ever turned their eyes to mortal eyes. Out..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["True; out indeed."],"id":791,"m":"True; he's out (forgotten his lines) indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Out of your favours, heavenly spirits, vouchsafe","Not to behold—"],"id":792,"m":"Out of your kindness, heavenly spirits, please do not look..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Once to behold, rogue!"],"id":793,"m":"Please do look, you rogue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Once to behold with your sun-beamed eyes—","With your sun-beamed eyes—"],"id":794,"m":"please do look with your sun-bright eyes... with your sun-bright eyes..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["They will not answer to that epithet.","You were best call it \"daughter-beamed eyes\"."],"id":795,"m":"They won't answer to that description. You'd better call them 'daughter-bright eyes'."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["They do not mark me, and that brings me out."],"id":796,"m":"They're not paying attention to me, and that's putting me off."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Is this your perfectness? Be gone, you rogue!"],"id":797,"m":"Is this how well you know it? Get out, you rogue!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Moth.","id":798},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["What would these strangers? Know their minds, Boyet.","If they do speak our language, 'tis our will","That some plain man recount their purposes.","Know what they would."],"id":799,"m":"What do these strangers want? Find out what they mean, Boyet. If they speak our language, we wish some plain man to tell us their purpose. Find out what they want."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["What would you with the Princess?"],"id":800,"m":"What do you want with the Princess?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Nothing but peace and gentle visitation."],"id":801,"m":"Nothing but peace and a polite visit."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["What would they, say they?"],"id":802,"m":"What do they say they want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Nothing but peace and gentle visitation."],"id":803,"m":"Nothing but peace and a polite visit."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Why, that they have, and bid them so be gone."],"id":804,"m":"Why, they've had that, so tell them to go."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["She says you have it, and you may be gone."],"id":805,"m":"She says you've had it, and you may go."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Say to her we have measured many miles","To tread a measure with her on this grass."],"id":806,"m":"Tell her we have measured many miles to tread a measure (dance) with her on this grass."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["They say that they have measured many a mile","To tread a measure with you on this grass."],"id":807,"m":"They say they have measured many miles to tread a measure with you on this grass."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["It is not so. Ask them how many inches","Is in one mile? If they have measured many,","The measure then of one is easily told."],"id":808,"m":"That's not true. Ask them how many inches there are in one mile. If they've measured many, they can easily tell the measure of one."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["If to come hither you have measured miles,","And many miles, the Princess bids you tell","How many inches doth fill up one mile."],"id":809,"m":"If you have measured miles to come here, and many miles, the Princess asks you to say how many inches make up one mile."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Tell her we measure them by weary steps."],"id":810,"m":"Tell her we measure them in weary steps."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["She hears herself."],"id":811,"m":"She can hear for herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["How many weary steps","Of many weary miles you have o'ergone","Are numbered in the travel of one mile?"],"id":812,"m":"How many weary steps of the many weary miles you've travelled are counted in the journey of one mile?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["We number nothing that we spend for you.","Our duty is so rich, so infinite,","That we may do it still without account.","Vouchsafe to show the sunshine of your face,","That we, like savages, may worship it."],"id":813,"m":"We count nothing we spend for you. Our devotion is so rich, so infinite, that we can always give it without counting. Please show us the sunshine of your face, so that we can worship it like savages."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["My face is but a moon, and clouded too."],"id":814,"m":"My face is only a moon, and a clouded one at that."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Blessed are clouds, to do as such clouds do!","Vouchsafe, bright moon, and these thy stars, to shine,","Those clouds removed, upon our watery eyne."],"id":815,"m":"Blessed are the clouds that do what those clouds do! Bright moon, you and your stars, please remove those clouds and shine on our watering eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["O vain petitioner! Beg a greater matter!","Thou now requests but moonshine in the water."],"id":816,"m":"Oh, foolish petitioner! Ask for something bigger! What you're asking for now is just moonshine in the water."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then in our measure do but vouchsafe one change.","Thou bidd'st me beg; this begging is not strange."],"id":817,"m":"Then grant us just one turn in our dance. You told me to beg; this begging isn't strange."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Play, music, then! Nay, you must do it soon."],"id":818,"m":"Play, music, then! No, you must do it quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music plays.","id":819},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Not yet? No dance! Thus change I like the moon."],"id":820,"m":"Not yet? No dance! I change like the moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Will you not dance? How come you thus estranged?"],"id":821,"m":"Won't you dance? Why have you become so distant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["You took the moon at full, but now she's changed."],"id":822,"m":"You caught the moon when it was full, but now it's changed."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Yet still she is the moon, and I the man.","The music plays, vouchsafe some motion to it."],"id":823,"m":"But it's still the moon, and I'm the man in it. The music's playing; please move a little to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Our ears vouchsafe it."],"id":824,"m":"Our ears do."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["But your legs should do it."],"id":825,"m":"But your legs should."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Since you are strangers and come here by chance,","We'll not be nice. Take hands. We will not dance."],"id":826,"m":"Since you're strangers and have come here by chance, we won't be fussy. Take hands. We won't dance."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why take we hands then?"],"id":827,"m":"Why take hands, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Only to part friends.","Curtsy, sweet hearts, and so the measure ends."],"id":828,"m":"Only to part as friends. Curtsy, sweethearts, and so the dance ends."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["More measure of this measure! Be not nice."],"id":829,"m":"More of this measure! Don't be coy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["We can afford no more at such a price."],"id":830,"m":"We can't give any more at such a price."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Price you yourselves? What buys your company?"],"id":831,"m":"You set a price on yourselves? What would buy your company?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Your absence only."],"id":832,"m":"Only your absence."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["That can never be."],"id":833,"m":"That can never be."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Then cannot we be bought. And so adieu—","Twice to your visor, and half once to you!"],"id":834,"m":"Then we can't be bought. And so goodbye: twice to your mask, and half a goodbye to you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["If you deny to dance, let's hold more chat."],"id":835,"m":"If you refuse to dance, let's talk some more."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["In private then."],"id":836,"m":"In private, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I am best pleased with that."],"id":837,"m":"I'm very happy with that."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":838},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["White-handed mistress, one sweet word with thee."],"id":839,"m":"White-handed mistress, one sweet word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Honey, and milk, and sugar: there is three."],"id":840,"m":"Honey, milk and sugar: there's three."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Nay, then, two treys, an if you grow so nice,","Metheglin, wort, and malmsey. Well run, dice!","There's half a dozen sweets."],"id":841,"m":"Well then, two threes, since you're so clever: mead, malt and malmsey wine. Good throw, dice! There's half a dozen sweets."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Seventh sweet, adieu.","Since you can cog, I'll play no more with you."],"id":842,"m":"Seventh sweet, goodbye. Since you cheat at dice, I won't play with you any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["One word in secret."],"id":843,"m":"One word in secret."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Let it not be sweet."],"id":844,"m":"Don't let it be sweet."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Thou griev'st my gall."],"id":845,"m":"You vex my gall."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Gall! Bitter."],"id":846,"m":"Gall! That's bitter."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Therefore meet."],"id":847,"m":"So it's fitting."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":848},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Will you vouchsafe with me to change a word?"],"id":849,"m":"Will you be so kind as to exchange a word with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Name it."],"id":850,"m":"Say it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Fair lady—"],"id":851,"m":"Fair lady..."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Say you so? Fair lord!","Take that for your \"fair lady\"."],"id":852,"m":"Is that so? Fair lord! Take that in return for your 'fair lady'."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Please it you,","As much in private, and I'll bid adieu."],"id":853,"m":"If you please, as much again in private, and then I'll say goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":854},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["What, was your visor made without a tongue?"],"id":855,"m":"What, was your mask made without a tongue?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["I know the reason, lady, why you ask."],"id":856,"m":"I know why you ask, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["O, for your reason! Quickly, sir, I long."],"id":857,"m":"Oh, let's have your reason! Quickly, sir, I'm longing to hear."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["You have a double tongue within your mask,","And would afford my speechless visor half."],"id":858,"m":"You have two tongues inside your mask, and would like to give half to my speechless mask."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["\"Veal\", quoth the Dutchman. Is not veal a calf?"],"id":859,"m":"'Veal', as the Dutchman says (meaning 'well', and 'veil'). Isn't veal a calf?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["A calf, fair lady?"],"id":860,"m":"A calf, fair lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["No, a fair lord calf."],"id":861,"m":"No, a fair lord calf."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Let's part the word."],"id":862,"m":"Let's share the word."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["No, I'll not be your half.","Take all and wean it; it may prove an ox."],"id":863,"m":"No, I won't be your other half. Take it all and wean it; it may grow into an ox."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Look how you butt yourself in these sharp mocks.","Will you give horns, chaste lady? Do not so."],"id":864,"m":"Look how you butt yourself with these sharp jokes. Will you give your husband horns, chaste lady? Don't."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Then die a calf before your horns do grow."],"id":865,"m":"Then die a calf before your horns grow."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["One word in private with you ere I die."],"id":866,"m":"One word in private with you before I die."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Bleat softly, then; the butcher hears you cry."],"id":867,"m":"Bleat quietly, then; the butcher can hear you crying."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":868},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["The tongues of mocking wenches are as keen","As is the razor's edge invisible,","Cutting a smaller hair than may be seen;","Above the sense of sense, so sensible","Seemeth their conference. Their conceits have wings","Fleeter than arrows, bullets, wind, thought, swifter things."],"id":869,"m":"The tongues of mocking girls are as sharp as the edge of a razor too fine to see, cutting a hair smaller than can be seen. Their talk seems so subtle it is beyond the reach of the senses. Their ideas have wings swifter than arrows, bullets, wind, thought, or anything swifter."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Not one word more, my maids; break off, break off."],"id":870,"m":"Not one word more, my girls; break off, break off."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["By heaven, all dry-beaten with pure scoff!"],"id":871,"m":"By heaven, thoroughly beaten with nothing but scorn!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Farewell, mad wenches. You have simple wits."],"id":872,"m":"Farewell, mad girls. You have simple wits."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King, Lords and Blackamoors.","id":873},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Twenty adieus, my frozen Muscovites.","Are these the breed of wits so wondered at?"],"id":874,"m":"Twenty goodbyes, my frozen Muscovites. Are these the kind of wits people marvel at?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Tapers they are, with your sweet breaths puffed out."],"id":875,"m":"They're candles, puffed out by your sweet breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Well-liking wits they have; gross, gross; fat, fat."],"id":876,"m":"They have well-fed wits: gross, gross; fat, fat."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["O poverty in wit, kingly-poor flout!","Will they not, think you, hang themselves tonight?","Or ever but in vizors show their faces?","This pert Berowne was out of countenance quite."],"id":877,"m":"Oh, poverty of wit, a king's poor joke! Don't you think they'll hang themselves tonight? Or never show their faces again except in masks? This cocky Berowne was completely put out of countenance."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["They were all in lamentable cases.","The King was weeping-ripe for a good word."],"id":878,"m":"They were all in a pitiful state. The King was ready to cry for one kind word."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Berowne did swear himself out of all suit."],"id":879,"m":"Berowne swore so much he was out of the game altogether."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Dumaine was at my service, and his sword.","\"Non point,\" quoth I; my servant straight was mute."],"id":880,"m":"Dumaine offered me his service and his sword. 'Not at all,' I said, and my servant went silent straight away."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Lord Longaville said I came o'er his heart;","And trow you what he called me?"],"id":881,"m":"Lord Longaville said I had come over his heart; and do you know what he called me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Qualm, perhaps."],"id":882,"m":"His 'qualm' (sickness), perhaps."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Yes, in good faith."],"id":883,"m":"Yes, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Go, sickness as thou art!"],"id":884,"m":"Away with you, you sickness!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Well, better wits have worn plain statute-caps.","But will you hear? The King is my love sworn."],"id":885,"m":"Well, better wits have worn plain woollen caps (as citizens did). But listen: the King has sworn to be my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["And quick Berowne hath plighted faith to me."],"id":886,"m":"And quick Berowne has pledged his faith to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["And Longaville was for my service born."],"id":887,"m":"And Longaville was born to serve me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Dumaine is mine as sure as bark on tree."],"id":888,"m":"Dumaine is mine, as sure as bark is on a tree."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Madam, and pretty mistresses, give ear.","Immediately they will again be here","In their own shapes, for it can never be","They will digest this harsh indignity."],"id":889,"m":"Madam, and pretty mistresses, listen. They'll be back here at once as themselves, because they'll never be able to swallow this harsh humiliation."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Will they return?"],"id":890,"m":"Will they come back?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["They will, they will, God knows,","And leap for joy, though they are lame with blows.","Therefore, change favours and, when they repair,","Blow like sweet roses in this summer air."],"id":891,"m":"They will, they will, God knows, and jump for joy, though they're lame from beatings. So swap your presents back, and when they come, bloom like sweet roses in this summer air."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["How \"blow\"? How \"blow\"? Speak to be understood."],"id":892,"m":"What do you mean, 'bloom'? Speak so you can be understood."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Fair ladies masked are roses in their bud.","Dismasked, their damask sweet commixture shown,","Are angels vailing clouds, or roses blown."],"id":893,"m":"Fair ladies in masks are roses in the bud. Unmasked, showing their sweet mixture of red and white, they are angels coming out from behind clouds, or roses in full bloom."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Avaunt, perplexity! What shall we do","If they return in their own shapes to woo?"],"id":894,"m":"Enough riddles! What shall we do if they come back as themselves to woo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Good madam, if by me you'll be advised,","Let's mock them still, as well known as disguised.","Let us complain to them what fools were here,","Disguised like Muscovites in shapeless gear;","And wonder what they were, and to what end","Their shallow shows and prologue vilely penned,","And their rough carriage so ridiculous,","Should be presented at our tent to us."],"id":895,"m":"Good madam, if you'll take my advice, let's keep mocking them, as much when we know them as when they were disguised. Let's complain to them about what fools were here, disguised as Muscovites in shapeless clothes, and wonder who they were, and why their shallow show, their badly written prologue and their rough, ridiculous behaviour should be put on at our tent for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Ladies, withdraw. The gallants are at hand."],"id":896,"m":"Ladies, go inside. The gallants are close by."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Whip to our tents, as roes run o'er the land."],"id":897,"m":"Dash to our tents, as deer run across the land."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Princess, Rosaline, Katharine and Maria.","id":898},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King, Berowne, Longaville and Dumaine as themselves.","id":899},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Fair sir, God save you. Where's the Princess?"],"id":900,"m":"Good sir, God save you. Where is the Princess?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Gone to her tent. Please it your Majesty","Command me any service to her thither?"],"id":901,"m":"Gone to her tent. Would Your Majesty like me to take any message to her there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["That she vouchsafe me audience for one word."],"id":902,"m":"That she will grant me a hearing for one word."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["I will; and so will she, I know, my lord."],"id":903,"m":"I will; and so will she, I know, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":904},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This fellow pecks up wit as pigeons peas","And utters it again when God doth please.","He is wit's pedlar, and retails his wares","At wakes and wassails, meetings, markets, fairs;","And we that sell by gross, the Lord doth know,","Have not the grace to grace it with such show.","This gallant pins the wenches on his sleeve.","Had he been Adam, he had tempted Eve.","He can carve too, and lisp. Why, this is he","That kissed his hand away in courtesy.","This is the ape of form, Monsieur the Nice,","That, when he plays at tables, chides the dice","In honourable terms. Nay, he can sing","A mean most meanly; and in ushering","Mend him who can. The ladies call him sweet.","The stairs, as he treads on them, kiss his feet.","This is the flower that smiles on everyone,","To show his teeth as white as whale's bone;","And consciences that will not die in debt","Pay him the due of \"honey-tongued Boyet\"."],"id":905,"m":"This fellow pecks up wit the way pigeons peck up peas, and spits it out again whenever God pleases. He's a pedlar of wit, and sells his goods retail at festivals and parties, meetings, markets and fairs; and we who sell wholesale, the Lord knows, haven't the grace to show it off like that. This gallant has the girls pinned to his sleeve. If he'd been Adam, he'd have tempted Eve. He can carve at table, too, and lisp. Why, this is the man who kissed his hand away being polite. This is the ape of good manners, Monsieur Fastidious, who, when he plays backgammon, scolds the dice in respectful language. Why, he can sing a middle part most moderately; and as a gentleman usher, let anyone beat him if they can. The ladies call him sweet. The stairs kiss his feet as he walks on them. This is the flower that smiles at everyone, to show his teeth as white as whalebone; and people who don't want to die in debt pay him his due by calling him 'honey-tongued Boyet'."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["A blister on his sweet tongue, with my heart,","That put Armado's page out of his part!"],"id":906,"m":"A blister on his sweet tongue, with all my heart, for putting Armado's page off his lines!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Princess, Rosaline, Maria, Katharine with Boyet.","id":907},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["See where it comes! Behaviour, what wert thou","Till this man showed thee, and what art thou now?"],"id":908,"m":"Look, here he comes! Good manners, what were you before this man displayed you, and what are you now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["All hail, sweet madam, and fair time of day."],"id":909,"m":"All hail, sweet madam, and good day to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["\"Fair\" in \"all hail\" is foul, as I conceive."],"id":910,"m":"'Fair' and 'hail' don't go together, as I see it (hail is foul weather)."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Construe my speeches better, if you may."],"id":911,"m":"Interpret my words better, if you can."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Then wish me better. I will give you leave."],"id":912,"m":"Then wish me better. I'll allow you to."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["We came to visit you, and purpose now","To lead you to our court. Vouchsafe it then."],"id":913,"m":"We came to visit you, and we mean now to take you to our court. Please agree to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["This field shall hold me, and so hold your vow.","Nor God nor I delights in perjured men."],"id":914,"m":"This field will hold me, and so you can hold to your vow. Neither God nor I takes pleasure in men who break their oaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Rebuke me not for that which you provoke.","The virtue of your eye must break my oath."],"id":915,"m":"Don't scold me for what you cause. The power of your eyes must break my oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["You nickname virtue: \"vice\" you should have spoke;","For virtue's office never breaks men's troth.","Now by my maiden honour, yet as pure","As the unsullied lily, I protest,","A world of torments though I should endure,","I would not yield to be your house's guest,","So much I hate a breaking cause to be","Of heavenly oaths, vowed with integrity."],"id":916,"m":"You call it by the wrong name: you should have said 'vice', not 'virtue', because virtue never makes men break their word. Now, by my maiden honour, which is still as pure as the spotless lily, I swear that even if I had to suffer a world of torments I would not agree to be a guest in your house; I so hate to be the cause of breaking holy oaths, sworn in good faith."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O, you have lived in desolation here,","Unseen, unvisited, much to our shame."],"id":917,"m":"Oh, you've lived out here in loneliness, unseen and unvisited, much to our shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Not so, my lord. It is not so, I swear.","We have had pastimes here and pleasant game.","A mess of Russians left us but of late."],"id":918,"m":"Not so, my lord. It isn't so, I swear. We've had amusements here and pleasant games. A group of Russians left us only recently."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How, madam? Russians?"],"id":919,"m":"What, madam? Russians?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Ay, in truth, my lord.","Trim gallants, full of courtship and of state."],"id":920,"m":"Yes, truly, my lord. Smart gallants, full of courtly manners and dignity."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Madam, speak true. It is not so, my lord.","My lady, to the manner of the days,","In courtesy gives undeserving praise.","We four indeed confronted were with four","In Russian habit. Here they stayed an hour","And talked apace; and in that hour, my lord,","They did not bless us with one happy word.","I dare not call them fools; but this I think,","When they are thirsty, fools would fain have drink."],"id":921,"m":"Madam, tell the truth. It isn't so, my lord. My lady, following the fashion of the times, politely gives undeserved praise. We four were indeed confronted by four men in Russian dress. They stayed here an hour and talked a lot; and in that hour, my lord, they didn't bless us with one good word. I daren't call them fools; but this I do think: when they're thirsty, fools would like a drink."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This jest is dry to me. My gentle sweet,","Your wit makes wise things foolish. When we greet,","With eyes' best seeing, heaven's fiery eye,","By light we lose light. Your capacity","Is of that nature that to your huge store","Wise things seem foolish and rich things but poor."],"id":922,"m":"This joke seems dry to me. My gentle sweet, your wit makes wise things look foolish. When we stare at the sun, heaven's fiery eye, with our best eyesight, we lose our sight through too much light. Your mind is such that, beside its huge store, wise things look foolish and rich things look poor."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["This proves you wise and rich, for in my eye—"],"id":923,"m":"That proves you are wise and rich, because in my eyes..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I am a fool, and full of poverty."],"id":924,"m":"I am a fool, and full of poverty."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["But that you take what doth to you belong,","It were a fault to snatch words from my tongue."],"id":925,"m":"If you weren't taking what belongs to you, it would be wrong to snatch words out of my mouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["O, I am yours, and all that I possess."],"id":926,"m":"Oh, I am yours, and everything I own."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["All the fool mine?"],"id":927,"m":"The whole fool is mine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["I cannot give you less."],"id":928,"m":"I can't give you less."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Which of the visors was it that you wore?"],"id":929,"m":"Which of the masks was it you wore?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Where, when, what visor? Why demand you this?"],"id":930,"m":"Where, when, what mask? Why do you ask that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["There, then, that visor; that superfluous case","That hid the worse and showed the better face."],"id":931,"m":"There, then, that mask; that unnecessary cover that hid the worse face and showed the better one."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["We are descried. They'll mock us now downright."],"id":932,"m":"We've been found out. They'll mock us openly now."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Let us confess and turn it to a jest."],"id":933,"m":"Let's confess and turn it into a joke."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Amazed, my lord? Why looks your Highness sad?"],"id":934,"m":"Bewildered, my lord? Why does Your Highness look sad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Help! Hold his brows! He'll swoon. Why look you pale?","Seasick, I think, coming from Muscovy."],"id":935,"m":"Help! Hold his head! He'll faint. Why do you look pale? Seasick, I think, from the voyage from Muscovy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Thus pour the stars down plagues for perjury.","Can any face of brass hold longer out?","Here stand I, lady; dart thy skill at me.","Bruise me with scorn, confound me with a flout,","Thrust thy sharp wit quite through my ignorance,","Cut me to pieces with thy keen conceit,","And I will wish thee never more to dance,","Nor never more in Russian habit wait.","O, never will I trust to speeches penned,","Nor to the motion of a school-boy's tongue,","Nor never come in visor to my friend,","Nor woo in rhyme like a blind harper's song.","Taffeta phrases, silken terms precise,","Three-piled hyperboles, spruce affectation,","Figures pedantical: these summer flies","Have blown me full of maggot ostentation.","I do forswear them, and I here protest,","By this white glove—how white the hand, God knows!—","Henceforth my wooing mind shall be expressed","In russet yeas and honest kersey noes.","And, to begin: wench, so God help me, law,","My love to thee is sound, sans crack or flaw."],"id":936,"m":"This is how the stars rain down plagues for breaking oaths. Can any brazen face hold out any longer? Here I stand, lady; throw your skill at me. Bruise me with scorn, crush me with a jeer, thrust your sharp wit right through my ignorance, cut me to pieces with your keen ideas, and I'll never ask you to dance again, or wait on you in Russian dress again. Oh, I'll never again trust prepared speeches, or a schoolboy's tongue reciting them, or come in a mask to my love, or woo in rhyme like a blind harper's song. Silky phrases, precise, elegant terms, velvet-thick exaggerations, spruce affectation, pedantic figures of speech: these summer flies have filled me with the maggots of showing off. I renounce them, and I swear here, by this white glove (how white the hand is, God knows!), that from now on my wooing will be expressed in plain homespun yeses and honest woollen noes. And to begin: girl, so help me God, la, my love for you is sound, sans crack or flaw."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Sans \"sans,\" I pray you."],"id":937,"m":"Without 'sans', please."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Yet I have a trick","Of the old rage. Bear with me, I am sick;","I'll leave it by degrees. Soft, let us see:","Write \"Lord have mercy on us\" on those three.","They are infected; in their hearts it lies;","They have the plague, and caught it of your eyes.","These lords are visited. You are not free,","For the Lord's tokens on you do I see."],"id":938,"m":"I still have a touch of the old disease. Be patient with me, I'm sick; I'll give it up gradually. Wait, let's see. Write 'Lord have mercy on us' on those three (as on plague houses). They are infected; it's in their hearts. They have the plague, and caught it from your eyes. These lords are stricken. And you're not free of it, because I can see the Lord's tokens (the plague spots, and their love-gifts) on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["No, they are free that gave these tokens to us."],"id":939,"m":"No, the ones who gave us these tokens are free."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Our states are forfeit. Seek not to undo us."],"id":940,"m":"Our estates are forfeit. Don't try to ruin us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["It is not so. For how can this be true,","That you stand forfeit, being those that sue?"],"id":941,"m":"That's not so. How can it be true that you're forfeit, when you are the ones bringing the lawsuit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Peace! for I will not have to do with you."],"id":942,"m":"Quiet! I won't have anything to do with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Nor shall not, if I do as I intend."],"id":943,"m":"And you won't, if I do what I intend."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Speak for yourselves. My wit is at an end."],"id":944,"m":"Speak for yourselves. My wit has run out."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Teach us, sweet madam, for our rude transgression","Some fair excuse."],"id":945,"m":"Teach us, sweet madam, some good excuse for our rude offence."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["The fairest is confession.","Were not you here but even now, disguised?"],"id":946,"m":"The best is confession. Weren't you here just now, in disguise?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Madam, I was."],"id":947,"m":"Madam, I was."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["And were you well advised?"],"id":948,"m":"And did you know what you were doing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I was, fair madam."],"id":949,"m":"I did, fair madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["When you then were here,","What did you whisper in your lady's ear?"],"id":950,"m":"When you were here, then, what did you whisper in your lady's ear?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["That more than all the world I did respect her."],"id":951,"m":"That I respected her more than all the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["When she shall challenge this, you will reject her."],"id":952,"m":"When she holds you to that, you'll reject her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Upon mine honour, no."],"id":953,"m":"No, upon my honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Peace, peace, forbear!","Your oath once broke, you force not to forswear."],"id":954,"m":"Quiet, quiet, stop! Once you've broken one oath, you don't mind breaking another."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Despise me when I break this oath of mine."],"id":955,"m":"Despise me if I break this oath of mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["I will; and therefore keep it. Rosaline,","What did the Russian whisper in your ear?"],"id":956,"m":"I will; so keep it. Rosaline, what did the Russian whisper in your ear?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Madam, he swore that he did hold me dear","As precious eyesight, and did value me","Above this world; adding thereto, moreover,","That he would wed me, or else die my lover."],"id":957,"m":"Madam, he swore that he held me as dear as his precious eyesight and valued me above the whole world; and he added, besides, that he would marry me or else die loving me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["God give thee joy of him! The noble lord","Most honourably doth uphold his word."],"id":958,"m":"God give you joy of him! The noble lord keeps his word most honourably."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What mean you, madam? By my life, my troth,","I never swore this lady such an oath."],"id":959,"m":"What do you mean, madam? By my life and my faith, I never swore such an oath to this lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["By heaven, you did! And to confirm it plain,","You gave me this. But take it, sir, again."],"id":960,"m":"By heaven, you did! And to prove it clearly, you gave me this. But take it back, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My faith and this the Princess I did give.","I knew her by this jewel on her sleeve."],"id":961,"m":"I gave my promise and this to the Princess. I recognised her by this jewel on her sleeve."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Pardon me, sir, this jewel did she wear,","And Lord Berowne, I thank him, is my dear.","What, will you have me, or your pearl again?"],"id":962,"m":"Excuse me, sir, she was wearing this jewel; and Lord Berowne, I thank him, is my darling. What, will you have me, or your pearl back?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Neither of either; I remit both twain.","I see the trick on't. Here was a consent,","Knowing aforehand of our merriment,","To dash it like a Christmas comedy.","Some carry-tale, some please-man, some slight zany,","Some mumble-news, some trencher-knight, some Dick,","That smiles his cheek in years and knows the trick","To make my lady laugh when she's disposed,","Told our intents before; which once disclosed,","The ladies did change favours, and then we,","Following the signs, wooed but the sign of she.","Now, to our perjury to add more terror,","We are again forsworn in will and error.","Much upon this 'tis. [To Boyet] And might not you","Forestall our sport, to make us thus untrue?","Do not you know my lady's foot by th' squier,","And laugh upon the apple of her eye?","And stand between her back, sir, and the fire,","Holding a trencher, jesting merrily?","You put our page out. Go, you are allowed;","Die when you will, a smock shall be your shroud.","You leer upon me, do you? There's an eye","Wounds like a leaden sword."],"id":963,"m":"Neither one nor the other; I give up both. I see the trick of it. There was a plot here: knowing about our fun beforehand, they set out to spoil it like a Christmas comedy. Some tell-tale, some flatterer, some lowly clown, some gossip, some hanger-on at the dinner table, some fellow who smiles his face into wrinkles and knows the trick of making my lady laugh when she's in the mood, told them our plans in advance. Once that was out, the ladies swapped favours, and we, following the signs, wooed only the sign of our lady. Now, to add more terror to our perjury, we've broken our oaths again, in intention and by mistake. That's about it. [To Boyet] And wasn't it you who spoiled our fun ahead of time, making us false like this? Don't you know my lady's foot by the ruler, and laugh in the apple of her eye, and stand between her back and the fire holding a plate, joking merrily? You put our page off his lines. Go on, you're the licensed fool; die when you like, a woman's slip will be your shroud. You leer at me, do you? There's an eye that wounds like a lead sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Full merrily","Hath this brave manage, this career, been run."],"id":964,"m":"Very merrily this fine gallop, this charge, has been run."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Lo, he is tilting straight! Peace! I have done."],"id":965,"m":"Look, he's charging straight at me with his lance! Quiet! I've finished."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Costard.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Welcome, pure wit! Thou part'st a fair fray."],"id":967,"m":"Welcome, pure wit! You've broken up a good fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["O Lord, sir, they would know","Whether the three Worthies shall come in or no."],"id":968,"m":"Oh Lord, sir, they want to know whether the three Worthies should come in or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["What, are there but three?"],"id":969,"m":"What, are there only three?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["No, sir; but it is vara fine,","For every one pursents three."],"id":970,"m":"No, sir; but it's very fine, because each one presents three."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["And three times thrice is nine."],"id":971,"m":"And three times three is nine."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Not so, sir, under correction, sir, I hope it is not so.","You cannot beg us, sir, I can assure you, sir; we know what we know.","I hope, sir, three times thrice, sir—"],"id":972,"m":"Not so, sir, if you'll allow me to correct you, sir; I hope it isn't so. You can't make fools of us, sir, I assure you, sir; we know what we know. I hope, sir, three times three, sir..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Is not nine?"],"id":973,"m":"Isn't nine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Under correction, sir, we know whereuntil it doth amount."],"id":974,"m":"With respect, sir, we know what it comes to."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["By Jove, I always took three threes for nine."],"id":975,"m":"By Jove, I always thought three threes made nine."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["O Lord, sir, it were pity you should get your living by reckoning, sir."],"id":976,"m":"Oh Lord, sir, it would be a pity if you had to earn your living by arithmetic, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["How much is it?"],"id":977,"m":"How much is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["O Lord, sir, the parties themselves, the actors, sir, will show","whereuntil it doth amount. For mine own part, I am, as they say, but to","parfect one man in one poor man—Pompion the Great, sir."],"id":978,"m":"Oh Lord, sir, the parties themselves, the actors, sir, will show what it comes to. For my own part, I am, as they say, only to perform one man in one poor man: Pompion (pumpkin) the Great, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Art thou one of the Worthies?"],"id":979,"m":"Are you one of the Worthies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["It pleased them to think me worthy of Pompey the Great. For mine own","part, I know not the degree of the Worthy, but I am to stand for him."],"id":980,"m":"They were good enough to think me worthy of Pompey the Great. For my own part, I don't know how high up the Worthy is, but I'm to stand in for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Go bid them prepare."],"id":981,"m":"Go and tell them to get ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["We will turn it finely off, sir; we will take some care."],"id":982,"m":"We'll carry it off nicely, sir; we'll take some care."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Costard.","id":983},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Berowne, they will shame us. Let them not approach."],"id":984,"m":"Berowne, they'll shame us. Don't let them come."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["We are shame-proof, my lord, and 'tis some policy","To have one show worse than the King's and his company."],"id":985,"m":"We're shame-proof, my lord; and it's good policy to have one show that's worse than the King's and his company's."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I say they shall not come."],"id":986,"m":"I say they shan't come."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Nay, my good lord, let me o'errule you now.","That sport best pleases that doth least know how,","Where zeal strives to content, and the contents","Die in the zeal of that which it presents;","Their form confounded makes most form in mirth,","When great things labouring perish in their birth."],"id":987,"m":"No, my good lord, let me overrule you now. The entertainment that pleases most is the one that knows least how to do it: where eagerness tries to please, and the content dies in the eagerness of those presenting it. Their efforts going wrong makes the best kind of fun, when great things, struggling to be born, die in the attempt."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A right description of our sport, my lord."],"id":988,"m":"An exact description of our own entertainment, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Armado, the Braggart.","id":989},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Anointed, I implore so much expense of thy royal sweet breath as will","utter a brace of words."],"id":990,"m":"Anointed one, I beg as much of your royal sweet breath as will speak a couple of words."},{"t":"sd","text":"Armado and King talk apart.","id":991},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Doth this man serve God?"],"id":992,"m":"Does this man serve God?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Why ask you?"],"id":993,"m":"Why do you ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["He speaks not like a man of God his making."],"id":994,"m":"He doesn't speak like a man God made."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["That is all one, my fair, sweet, honey monarch; for, I protest, the","schoolmaster is exceeding fantastical; too, too vain, too, too vain.","But we will put it, as they say, to fortuna de la guerra. I wish you","the peace of mind, most royal couplement!"],"id":995,"m":"It's all the same, my fair, sweet, honey monarch; for, I declare, the schoolmaster is extremely fantastical; too, too vain, too, too vain. But we'll leave it, as they say, to 'fortuna de la guerra', the fortune of war. I wish you peace of mind, most royal couple!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":996},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Here is like to be a good presence of Worthies. He presents Hector of","Troy; the swain, Pompey the Great; the parish curate, Alexander;","Armado's page, Hercules; the pedant, Judas Maccabaeus.","And if these four Worthies in their first show thrive,","These four will change habits and present the other five."],"id":997,"m":"It looks like there'll be a good array of Worthies. He plays Hector of Troy; the peasant, Pompey the Great; the parish curate, Alexander; Armado's page, Hercules; the schoolmaster, Judas Maccabaeus. And if these four Worthies do well in their first show, these four will change costumes and present the other five."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["There is five in the first show."],"id":998,"m":"There are five in the first show."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["You are deceived. 'Tis not so."],"id":999,"m":"You're wrong. That's not so."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["The pedant, the braggart, the hedge-priest, the fool, and the boy.","Abate throw at novum, and the whole world again","Cannot pick out five such, take each one in his vein."],"id":1000,"m":"The schoolmaster, the braggart, the hedge-priest, the fool and the boy. Except for a lucky throw at dice, the whole world couldn't pick out another five like them, each in his own way."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The ship is under sail, and here she comes amain."],"id":1001,"m":"The ship is under sail, and here she comes at full speed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Costard as Pompey.","id":1002},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I Pompey am—"],"id":1003,"m":"I Pompey am..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["You lie, you are not he."],"id":1004,"m":"You're lying; you're not him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I Pompey am—"],"id":1005,"m":"I Pompey am..."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["With leopard's head on knee."],"id":1006,"m":"with a leopard's head on his knee (as on a coat of arms)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Well said, old mocker. I must needs be friends with thee."],"id":1007,"m":"Well said, old mocker. I'll have to be friends with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I Pompey am, Pompey surnamed the Big."],"id":1008,"m":"I Pompey am, Pompey surnamed the Big..."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["The \"Great\"."],"id":1009,"m":"The 'Great'."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["It is \"Great\", sir; Pompey surnamed the Great,","That oft in field, with targe and shield, did make my foe to sweat.","And travelling along this coast, I here am come by chance,","And lay my arms before the legs of this sweet lass of France.","If your ladyship would say, \"Thanks, Pompey\", I had done."],"id":1010,"m":"It is 'Great', sir. 'Pompey surnamed the Great, who often in the field, with shield and buckler, made my enemy sweat; and travelling along this coast, I have come here by chance, and lay my arms before the legs of this sweet lass of France.' If your ladyship would say 'Thanks, Pompey', I'd be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Great thanks, great Pompey."],"id":1011,"m":"Great thanks, great Pompey."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["'Tis not so much worth; but I hope I was perfect. I made a little fault","in \"Great\"."],"id":1012,"m":"It isn't worth that much; but I hope I was word-perfect. I made a little mistake on 'Great'."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["My hat to a halfpenny, Pompey proves the best Worthy."],"id":1013,"m":"I'll bet my hat against a halfpenny that Pompey turns out the best Worthy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Nathaniel, the Curate, for Alexander.","id":1014},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["When in the world I lived, I was the world's commander;","By east, west, north, and south, I spread my conquering might.","My scutcheon plain declares that I am Alisander."],"id":1015,"m":"'When I lived in the world, I was the world's commander; east, west, north and south, I spread my conquering might. My coat of arms plainly declares that I am Alisander.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Your nose says, no, you are not; for it stands to right."],"id":1016,"m":"Your nose says no, you are not, because it stands too straight (Alexander's head was said to tilt)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Your nose smells \"no\" in this, most tender-smelling knight."],"id":1017,"m":"Your nose smells 'no' in this, most delicate-smelling knight (Alexander's body was said to smell sweet)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["The conqueror is dismayed. Proceed, good Alexander."],"id":1018,"m":"The conqueror is upset. Carry on, good Alexander."},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["When in the world I lived, I was the world's commander—"],"id":1019,"m":"'When I lived in the world, I was the world's commander...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Most true; 'tis right. You were so, Alisander."],"id":1020,"m":"Very true; that's right. You were, Alisander."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Pompey the Great—"],"id":1021,"m":"Pompey the Great..."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Your servant, and Costard."],"id":1022,"m":"Your servant, and Costard."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Take away the conqueror, take away Alisander."],"id":1023,"m":"Take away the conqueror, take away Alisander."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["[To Sir Nathaniel] O sir, you have overthrown Alisander the","Conqueror. You will be scraped out of the painted cloth for this. Your","lion, that holds his pole-axe sitting on a close-stool, will be given","to Ajax. He will be the ninth Worthy. A conqueror, and afeard to speak?","Run away for shame, Alisander. [Nathaniel retires] There, an't shall","please you, a foolish mild man; an honest man, look you, and soon","dashed. He is a marvellous good neighbour, faith, and a very good","bowler; but for Alisander, alas you see how 'tis—a little o'erparted.","But there are Worthies a-coming will speak their mind in some other","sort."],"id":1024,"m":"[To Sir Nathaniel] Oh, sir, you've overthrown Alisander the Conqueror! You'll be scraped off the painted wall-hanging for this. Your lion holding his battle-axe sitting on a toilet will be given to Ajax: he'll be the ninth Worthy. A conqueror, and afraid to speak? Run away for shame, Alisander. [Nathaniel retires] There, if you please, a foolish, mild man; an honest man, you see, and easily flustered. He's a marvellously good neighbour, honestly, and a very good bowler; but as for Alisander, alas, you see how it is: a little out of his depth. But there are Worthies coming who'll speak their minds in a different way."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Stand aside, good Pompey."],"id":1025,"m":"Stand aside, good Pompey."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Holofernes, the Pedant, as Judas, and Moth, the Boy, as Hercules.","id":1026},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Great Hercules is presented by this imp,","Whose club killed Cerberus, that three-headed canus,","And when he was a babe, a child, a shrimp,","Thus did he strangle serpents in his manus.","Quoniam he seemeth in minority,","Ergo I come with this apology.","Keep some state in thy exit, and vanish."],"id":1027,"m":"'Great Hercules is played by this imp, whose club killed Cerberus, that three-headed \"canus\" (dog); and when he was a baby, a child, a shrimp, he strangled serpents like this in his \"manus\" (hands). \"Quoniam\", since, he seems to be under age, \"ergo\", therefore, I come with this explanation.' Make a dignified exit, and vanish."},{"t":"sd","text":"Moth retires.","id":1028},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Judas I am.—"],"id":1029,"m":"'Judas I am...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["A Judas!"],"id":1030,"m":"A Judas!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Not Iscariot, sir.","Judas I am, ycleped Maccabaeus."],"id":1031,"m":"Not Iscariot, sir. 'Judas I am, called Maccabaeus.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Judas Maccabaeus clipped is plain Judas."],"id":1032,"m":"Judas Maccabaeus cut short is plain Judas."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A kissing traitor. How art thou proved Judas?"],"id":1033,"m":"A kissing traitor. How do you prove you're Judas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Judas I am—"],"id":1034,"m":"'Judas I am...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["The more shame for you, Judas."],"id":1035,"m":"All the more shame on you, Judas."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["What mean you, sir?"],"id":1036,"m":"What do you mean, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["To make Judas hang himself."],"id":1037,"m":"To make Judas hang himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["Begin, sir; you are my elder."],"id":1038,"m":"You begin, sir; you are my elder."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Well followed. Judas was hanged on an elder."],"id":1039,"m":"Well followed up: Judas was hanged on an elder tree."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["I will not be put out of countenance."],"id":1040,"m":"I won't be put out of countenance (made to lose my nerve)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Because thou hast no face."],"id":1041,"m":"Because you have no countenance (face)."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["What is this?"],"id":1042,"m":"What is this, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["A cittern-head."],"id":1043,"m":"The carved head on a lute."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["The head of a bodkin."],"id":1044,"m":"The head of a hairpin."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A death's face in a ring."],"id":1045,"m":"A skull on a memorial ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["The face of an old Roman coin, scarce seen."],"id":1046,"m":"The face of an old Roman coin, worn almost away."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["The pommel of Caesar's falchion."],"id":1047,"m":"The knob on the handle of Caesar's sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["The carved-bone face on a flask."],"id":1048,"m":"The carved bone face on a powder flask."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Saint George's half-cheek in a brooch."],"id":1049,"m":"Saint George's profile on a brooch."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Ay, and in a brooch of lead."],"id":1050,"m":"Yes, and a lead brooch at that."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Ay, and worn in the cap of a tooth-drawer.","And now forward, for we have put thee in countenance."],"id":1051,"m":"Yes, and worn in the hat of a tooth-puller. And now go on, for we've given you some faces."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["You have put me out of countenance."],"id":1052,"m":"You've put me out of countenance."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["False. We have given thee faces."],"id":1053,"m":"Not true. We've given you lots of faces."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["But you have outfaced them all."],"id":1054,"m":"But you've outfaced them all."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["An thou wert a lion, we would do so."],"id":1055,"m":"If you were a lion, we'd do just that."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Therefore, as he is an ass, let him go.","And so adieu, sweet Jude. Nay, why dost thou stay?"],"id":1056,"m":"So, since he's an ass, let him go. And so goodbye, sweet Jude. Well, why are you still here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["For the latter end of his name."],"id":1057,"m":"For the end of his name."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["For the ass to the Jude? Give it him. Jud-as, away!"],"id":1058,"m":"For the 'ass' to go after 'Jude'? Give it to him. Jud-as, away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOLOFERNES","lines":["This is not generous, not gentle, not humble."],"id":1059,"m":"This isn't generous, not gentlemanly, not humble."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["A light for Monsieur Judas! It grows dark; he may stumble."],"id":1060,"m":"A light for Monsieur Judas! It's getting dark; he may stumble. (Judas carried a lantern when he betrayed Christ.)"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Holofernes.","id":1061},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Alas, poor Maccabaeus, how hath he been baited!"],"id":1062,"m":"Alas, poor Maccabaeus, how he's been baited!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Armado, the Braggart, as Hector.","id":1063},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Hide thy head, Achilles. Here comes Hector in arms."],"id":1064,"m":"Hide your head, Achilles. Here comes Hector in armour."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Though my mocks come home by me, I will now be merry."],"id":1065,"m":"Even if my jokes come back to bite me, I'll be merry now."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Hector was but a Trojan in respect of this."],"id":1066,"m":"Hector was only a Trojan (a fine fellow) compared with this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["But is this Hector?"],"id":1067,"m":"But is this Hector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["I think Hector was not so clean-timbered."],"id":1068,"m":"I don't think Hector was so well built."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["His leg is too big for Hector's."],"id":1069,"m":"His leg is too big for Hector's."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["More calf, certain."],"id":1070,"m":"More calf, certainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["No, he is best endued in the small."],"id":1071,"m":"No, he's best equipped in the slender part (the lower leg)."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["This cannot be Hector."],"id":1072,"m":"This can't be Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["He's a god or a painter, for he makes faces."],"id":1073,"m":"He's a god or a painter, because he makes faces."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The armipotent Mars, of lances the almighty,","Gave Hector a gift—"],"id":1074,"m":"'The arm-mighty Mars, almighty in lances, gave Hector a gift...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["A gilt nutmeg."],"id":1075,"m":"A gilded nutmeg."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A lemon."],"id":1076,"m":"A lemon."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["Stuck with cloves."],"id":1077,"m":"Stuck with cloves."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["No, cloven."],"id":1078,"m":"No, cloven (split)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Peace!","The armipotent Mars, of lances the almighty,","Gave Hector a gift, the heir of Ilion;","A man so breathed that certain he would fight, yea,","From morn till night, out of his pavilion.","I am that flower—"],"id":1079,"m":"Quiet! 'The arm-mighty Mars, almighty in lances, gave Hector a gift, the heir of Troy; a man of such stamina that he would certainly fight, yes, from morning to night, outside his tent. I am that flower...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["That mint."],"id":1080,"m":"That mint."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["That columbine."],"id":1081,"m":"That columbine."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sweet Lord Longaville, rein thy tongue."],"id":1082,"m":"Sweet Lord Longaville, rein in your tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["I must rather give it the rein, for it runs against Hector."],"id":1083,"m":"I must rather give it its head, because it's running against Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Ay, and Hector's a greyhound."],"id":1084,"m":"Yes, and Hector's a greyhound (and will outrun it)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The sweet war-man is dead and rotten. Sweet chucks, beat not the bones","of the buried. When he breathed, he was a man. But I will forward with","my device. [To the Princess] Sweet royalty, bestow on me the sense","of hearing."],"id":1085,"m":"The sweet warrior is dead and rotten. Sweet chicks, don't beat the bones of the buried. When he was alive, he was a man. But I will go on with my show. [To the Princess] Sweet royalty, grant me your sense of hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Speak, brave Hector; we are much delighted."],"id":1086,"m":"Speak, brave Hector; we are very pleased."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I do adore thy sweet Grace's slipper."],"id":1087,"m":"I adore your sweet Grace's slipper."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Loves her by the foot."],"id":1088,"m":"He loves her by the foot."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["He may not by the yard."],"id":1089,"m":"He can't love her by the yard (a bawdy pun)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["This Hector far surmounted Hannibal.","The party is gone—"],"id":1090,"m":"'This Hector far surpassed Hannibal...' The party is gone..."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Fellow Hector, she is gone; she is two months on her way."],"id":1091,"m":"Fellow Hector, she is gone; she's two months gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["What meanest thou?"],"id":1092,"m":"What do you mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Faith, unless you play the honest Trojan, the poor wench is cast away.","She's quick; the child brags in her belly already. 'Tis yours."],"id":1093,"m":"Honestly, unless you play the honest Trojan, the poor girl is ruined. She's pregnant; the child is already showing off in her belly. It's yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Dost thou infamonize me among potentates? Thou shalt die."],"id":1094,"m":"Do you defame me in front of rulers? You will die."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["Then shall Hector be whipped for Jaquenetta that is quick by him, and","hanged for Pompey that is dead by him."],"id":1095,"m":"Then Hector will be whipped for Jaquenetta, who's pregnant by him, and hanged for Pompey, who'll be dead by him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Most rare Pompey!"],"id":1096,"m":"Most excellent Pompey!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["Renowned Pompey!"],"id":1097,"m":"Famous Pompey!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Greater than \"Great\"! Great, great, great Pompey! Pompey the Huge!"],"id":1098,"m":"Greater than 'Great'! Great, great, great Pompey! Pompey the Huge!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Hector trembles."],"id":1099,"m":"Hector is trembling."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Pompey is moved. More Ates, more Ates! Stir them on, stir them on!"],"id":1100,"m":"Pompey is angry. More goddesses of discord, more! Stir them up, stir them up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Hector will challenge him."],"id":1101,"m":"Hector will challenge him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Ay, if he have no more man's blood in his belly than will sup a flea."],"id":1102,"m":"Yes, if he hasn't more man's blood in his belly than would feed a flea."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["By the north pole, I do challenge thee."],"id":1103,"m":"By the North Pole, I challenge you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I will not fight with a pole, like a northern man. I'll slash, I'll do","it by the sword. I bepray you, let me borrow my arms again."],"id":1104,"m":"I won't fight with a pole, like a northern man. I'll slash; I'll do it with the sword. Please, let me borrow my weapons back."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Room for the incensed Worthies!"],"id":1105,"m":"Make room for the angry Worthies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COSTARD","lines":["I'll do it in my shirt."],"id":1106,"m":"I'll fight in my shirt."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Most resolute Pompey!"],"id":1107,"m":"Most determined Pompey!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTH","lines":["Master, let me take you a buttonhole lower. Do you not see Pompey is","uncasing for the combat? What mean you? You will lose your reputation."],"id":1108,"m":"Master, let me take you down a peg. Don't you see Pompey is stripping for the fight? What do you mean by it? You'll lose your reputation."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Gentlemen and soldiers, pardon me. I will not combat in my shirt."],"id":1109,"m":"Gentlemen and soldiers, forgive me. I will not fight in my shirt."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["You may not deny it. Pompey hath made the challenge."],"id":1110,"m":"You can't refuse. Pompey has made the challenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sweet bloods, I both may and will."],"id":1111,"m":"Sweet fellows, I both can and will."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["What reason have you for 't?"],"id":1112,"m":"What's your reason for it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The naked truth of it is, I have no shirt. I go woolward for penance."],"id":1113,"m":"The naked truth is, I have no shirt. I wear wool next to my skin as a penance."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOYET","lines":["True, and it was enjoined him in Rome for want of linen; since when,","I'll be sworn, he wore none but a dishclout of Jaquenetta's, and that","he wears next his heart for a favour."],"id":1114,"m":"True, and it was ordered as a penance for him in Rome for lack of linen; since then, I'll swear, he's worn nothing but one of Jaquenetta's dishcloths, and he wears that next to his heart as a love-token."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger, Monsieur Marcadé.","id":1115},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCADÉ","lines":["God save you, madam."],"id":1116,"m":"God save you, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Welcome, Marcadé,","But that thou interruptest our merriment."],"id":1117,"m":"Welcome, Marcadé, except that you interrupt our fun."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCADÉ","lines":["I am sorry, madam, for the news I bring","Is heavy in my tongue. The King your father—"],"id":1118,"m":"I'm sorry, madam, because the news I bring is heavy on my tongue. The King your father..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Dead, for my life!"],"id":1119,"m":"Is dead, on my life!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARCADÉ","lines":["Even so. My tale is told."],"id":1120,"m":"It is so. My message is delivered."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Worthies away! The scene begins to cloud."],"id":1121,"m":"Worthies, away! The scene is clouding over."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["For mine own part, I breathe free breath. I have seen the day of wrong","through the little hole of discretion, and I will right myself like a","soldier."],"id":1122,"m":"For my own part, I breathe freely. I have seen how I was wronged through the little hole of discretion, and I will right myself like a soldier."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Worthies.","id":1123},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How fares your Majesty?"],"id":1124,"m":"How is Your Majesty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Boyet, prepare. I will away tonight."],"id":1125,"m":"Boyet, get ready. I will leave tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Madam, not so. I do beseech you stay."],"id":1126,"m":"Madam, don't. I beg you to stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Prepare, I say. I thank you, gracious lords,","For all your fair endeavours, and entreat,","Out of a new-sad soul, that you vouchsafe","In your rich wisdom to excuse or hide","The liberal opposition of our spirits,","If over-boldly we have borne ourselves","In the converse of breath; your gentleness","Was guilty of it. Farewell, worthy lord!","A heavy heart bears not a nimble tongue.","Excuse me so, coming too short of thanks","For my great suit so easily obtained."],"id":1127,"m":"Get ready, I say. I thank you, gracious lords, for all your kind efforts, and I ask, from a newly saddened soul, that in your rich wisdom you forgive or overlook the free way our spirits have opposed yours, if we have behaved too boldly in our talk; your kindness was to blame for it. Farewell, worthy lord! A heavy heart doesn't have a nimble tongue. Please excuse me, then, for falling short in thanking you for my great request, so easily granted."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The extreme parts of time extremely forms","All causes to the purpose of his speed,","And often at his very loose decides","That which long process could not arbitrate.","And though the mourning brow of progeny","Forbid the smiling courtesy of love","The holy suit which fain it would convince,","Yet, since love's argument was first on foot,","Let not the cloud of sorrow jostle it","From what it purposed; since to wail friends lost","Is not by much so wholesome-profitable","As to rejoice at friends but newly found."],"id":1128,"m":"The last moments of time often shape every matter to fit its haste, and at the very last moment decide what a long process couldn't settle. And though a daughter's mourning face forbids love's smiling courtesy to press the holy suit it would like to win, still, since love's case was started first, don't let the cloud of sorrow push it aside from what it intended; for mourning lost friends is not nearly as healthy or profitable as rejoicing at friends just found."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["I understand you not. My griefs are double."],"id":1129,"m":"I don't understand you. My griefs are doubled."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Honest plain words best pierce the ear of grief;","And by these badges understand the King.","For your fair sakes have we neglected time,","Played foul play with our oaths. Your beauty, ladies,","Hath much deformed us, fashioning our humours","Even to the opposed end of our intents;","And what in us hath seemed ridiculous—","As love is full of unbefitting strains,","All wanton as a child, skipping and vain,","Formed by the eye and therefore, like the eye,","Full of strange shapes, of habits and of forms,","Varying in subjects as the eye doth roll","To every varied object in his glance;","Which parti-coated presence of loose love","Put on by us, if, in your heavenly eyes,","Have misbecomed our oaths and gravities,","Those heavenly eyes that look into these faults","Suggested us to make. Therefore, ladies,","Our love being yours, the error that love makes","Is likewise yours. We to ourselves prove false","By being once false for ever to be true","To those that make us both—fair ladies, you.","And even that falsehood, in itself a sin,","Thus purifies itself and turns to grace."],"id":1130,"m":"Honest plain words reach the ear of grief best, and these will help you understand the King. For your lovely sakes we've wasted our time and played false with our oaths. Your beauty, ladies, has changed us greatly, shaping our moods to the very opposite of what we intended. And whatever in us has seemed ridiculous (for love is full of unsuitable impulses, as wanton as a child, skipping and foolish; it is born in the eye, and so, like the eye, it is full of strange shapes, habits and forms, changing its subject as the eye rolls to every different object it glances at): if this motley costume of free love that we put on has, in your heavenly eyes, seemed unfit for our oaths and our dignity, it was those heavenly eyes, which see these faults, that tempted us to commit them. So, ladies, since our love is yours, the mistakes love makes are yours too. We are false to ourselves by being false once, in order to be true for ever to those who make us both false and true: fair ladies, you. And even that falseness, which is a sin in itself, purifies itself in this way and turns to grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["We have received your letters, full of love;","Your favours, the ambassadors of love;","And in our maiden council rated them","At courtship, pleasant jest, and courtesy,","As bombast and as lining to the time.","But more devout than this in our respects","Have we not been; and therefore met your loves","In their own fashion, like a merriment."],"id":1131,"m":"We received your letters, full of love, and your gifts, love's ambassadors; and in our girls' council we rated them as courtship, a pleasant joke and good manners, padding to fill the time. We have not taken them more seriously than that; and so we met your love in its own fashion, as a game."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["Our letters, madam, showed much more than jest."],"id":1132,"m":"Our letters, madam, showed much more than a joke."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["So did our looks."],"id":1133,"m":"So did our looks."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["We did not quote them so."],"id":1134,"m":"We didn't read them that way."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Now, at the latest minute of the hour,","Grant us your loves."],"id":1135,"m":"Now, at the very last minute of the hour, grant us your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["A time, methinks, too short","To make a world-without-end bargain in.","No, no, my lord, your Grace is perjured much,","Full of dear guiltiness; and therefore this:","If for my love—as there is no such cause—","You will do aught, this shall you do for me:","Your oath I will not trust, but go with speed","To some forlorn and naked hermitage,","Remote from all the pleasures of the world,","There stay until the twelve celestial signs","Have brought about the annual reckoning.","If this austere insociable life","Change not your offer made in heat of blood;","If frosts and fasts, hard lodging and thin weeds,","Nip not the gaudy blossoms of your love,","But that it bear this trial, and last love;","Then, at the expiration of the year,","Come challenge me, challenge me by these deserts,","And, by this virgin palm now kissing thine,","I will be thine. And, till that instance, shut","My woeful self up in a mournful house,","Raining the tears of lamentation","For the remembrance of my father's death.","If this thou do deny, let our hands part,","Neither entitled in the other's heart."],"id":1136,"m":"A time far too short, I think, to make an eternal bargain in. No, no, my lord, Your Grace has broken many oaths and is full of grievous guilt; so here is what I say. If you will do anything for my love (though there's no reason why you should), this is what you must do for me. I won't trust your oath; instead, go straight away to some lonely, bare hermitage, far from all the pleasures of the world, and stay there until the twelve signs of the zodiac have brought round a full year. If this harsh, solitary life doesn't change the offer you made in the heat of passion; if frosts and fasting, a hard bed and thin clothes don't nip the showy blossoms of your love; if it survives this test and stays love: then, at the end of the year, come and claim me, claim me by what you have earned, and, by this virgin hand now kissing yours, I will be yours. Until then I'll shut my sorrowful self up in a house of mourning, raining tears of grief in memory of my father's death. If you refuse this, let our hands part, and neither have any claim on the other's heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["If this, or more than this, I would deny,","To flatter up these powers of mine with rest,","The sudden hand of death close up mine eye!","Hence hermit, then. My heart is in thy breast."],"id":1137,"m":"If I would refuse this, or more than this, to pamper my body with comfort, may the sudden hand of death close my eyes! Off to the hermitage, then. My heart is in your breast."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":1138},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["And what to me, my love? But what to me?","A wife?"],"id":1139,"m":"And what for me, my love? But what for me? A wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["A beard, fair health, and honesty;","With threefold love I wish you all these three."],"id":1140,"m":"A beard, good health and honesty; I wish you all three with three times as much love."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["O, shall I say, \"I thank you, gentle wife\"?"],"id":1141,"m":"Oh, shall I say, 'Thank you, gentle wife'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["No so, my lord. A twelvemonth and a day","I'll mark no words that smooth-faced wooers say.","Come when the King doth to my lady come;","Then, if I have much love, I'll give you some."],"id":1142,"m":"Not so, my lord. For a year and a day I'll ignore anything smooth-faced wooers say. Come when the King comes to my lady; then, if I have plenty of love, I'll give you some."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["I'll serve thee true and faithfully till then."],"id":1143,"m":"I'll serve you truly and faithfully until then."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHARINE","lines":["Yet swear not, lest ye be forsworn again."],"id":1144,"m":"But don't swear it, in case you break your oath again."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":1145},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["What says Maria?"],"id":1146,"m":"What does Maria say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["At the twelvemonth's end","I'll change my black gown for a faithful friend."],"id":1147,"m":"At the end of the year, I'll exchange my black mourning gown for a faithful friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"LONGAVILLE","lines":["I'll stay with patience, but the time is long."],"id":1148,"m":"I'll wait patiently; but the time is long."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["The liker you; few taller are so young."],"id":1149,"m":"All the more like you; few men so young are so tall."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":1150},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Studies my lady? Mistress, look on me.","Behold the window of my heart, mine eye,","What humble suit attends thy answer there.","Impose some service on me for thy love."],"id":1151,"m":"Is my lady deep in thought? Mistress, look at me. See in the window of my heart, my eye, what humble request waits there for your answer. Set me some task for your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Oft have I heard of you, my Lord Berowne,","Before I saw you; and the world's large tongue","Proclaims you for a man replete with mocks,","Full of comparisons and wounding flouts,","Which you on all estates will execute","That lie within the mercy of your wit.","To weed this wormwood from your fruitful brain,","And therewithal to win me, if you please,","Without the which I am not to be won,","You shall this twelvemonth term from day to day","Visit the speechless sick, and still converse","With groaning wretches; and your task shall be,","With all the fierce endeavour of your wit","To enforce the pained impotent to smile."],"id":1152,"m":"I'd often heard of you, my Lord Berowne, before I saw you; and the world's broad tongue proclaims you a man full of mockery, full of comparisons and wounding jeers, which you fire at people of every rank who come within range of your wit. To weed this bitter wormwood out of your fertile brain, and at the same time to win me, if you want to (and without that I can't be won), you shall spend this year, day after day, visiting the speechless sick and talking constantly with groaning wretches; and your task will be, with all the fierce effort of your wit, to force the pained and helpless to smile."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["To move wild laughter in the throat of death?","It cannot be, it is impossible.","Mirth cannot move a soul in agony."],"id":1153,"m":"To stir wild laughter in the throat of death? It can't be done; it's impossible. Mirth can't move a soul in agony."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSALINE","lines":["Why, that's the way to choke a gibing spirit,","Whose influence is begot of that loose grace","Which shallow laughing hearers give to fools.","A jest's prosperity lies in the ear","Of him that hears it, never in the tongue","Of him that makes it. Then, if sickly ears,","Deafed with the clamours of their own dear groans,","Will hear your idle scorns, continue then,","And I will have you and that fault withal;","But if they will not, throw away that spirit,","And I shall find you empty of that fault,","Right joyful of your reformation."],"id":1154,"m":"Why, that's the way to choke a mocking spirit, whose power comes from the easy favour that shallow, laughing listeners give to fools. A joke's success lies in the ear of the person who hears it, never in the tongue of the person who makes it. So if sick ears, deafened by the noise of their own groans, will listen to your idle mockery, carry on, and I'll take you, fault and all; but if they won't, throw away that spirit, and I'll find you emptied of that fault and be very glad you've reformed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["A twelvemonth? Well, befall what will befall,","I'll jest a twelvemonth in an hospital."],"id":1155,"m":"A year? Well, come what may, I'll joke for a year in a hospital."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["[To the King] Ay, sweet my lord, and so I take my leave."],"id":1156,"m":"[To the King] Yes, my sweet lord, and so I take my leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["No, madam, we will bring you on your way."],"id":1157,"m":"No, madam, we'll escort you on your way."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["Our wooing doth not end like an old play.","Jack hath not Jill. These ladies' courtesy","Might well have made our sport a comedy."],"id":1158,"m":"Our wooing doesn't end like an old play: Jack doesn't get his Jill. These ladies, if they'd been kind, could easily have made our sport a comedy."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Come, sir, it wants a twelvemonth and a day,","And then 'twill end."],"id":1159,"m":"Come, sir, it's only a year and a day away, and then it'll end that way."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEROWNE","lines":["That's too long for a play."],"id":1160,"m":"That's too long for a play."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Armado, the Braggart.","id":1161},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Sweet Majesty, vouchsafe me—"],"id":1162,"m":"Sweet Majesty, grant me..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCESS","lines":["Was not that Hector?"],"id":1163,"m":"Wasn't that Hector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUMAINE","lines":["The worthy knight of Troy."],"id":1164,"m":"The worthy knight of Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["I will kiss thy royal finger, and take leave. I am a votary; I have","vowed to Jaquenetta to hold the plough for her sweet love three year.","But, most esteemed Greatness, will you hear the dialogue that the two","learned men have compiled in praise of the owl and the cuckoo? It","should have followed in the end of our show."],"id":1165,"m":"I will kiss your royal finger and take my leave. I am under a vow: I have sworn to Jaquenetta to work the plough for her sweet love for three years. But, most esteemed Greatness, will you hear the dialogue the two learned men have written in praise of the owl and the cuckoo? It should have come at the end of our show."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Call them forth quickly; we will do so."],"id":1166,"m":"Call them out quickly; we will."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["Holla! Approach."],"id":1167,"m":"Hello there! Come forward."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter all.","id":1168},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["This side is Hiems, Winter; this Ver, the Spring; the one","maintained by the owl, th' other by the cuckoo. Ver, begin."],"id":1169,"m":"This side is Hiems, Winter; this is Ver, Spring; one is represented by the owl, the other by the cuckoo. Ver, begin."},{"t":"sd","text":"The Song.","id":1170},{"t":"sp","who":"SPRING","lines":["~When daisies pied and violets blue","~And lady-smocks all silver-white","~And cuckoo-buds of yellow hue","~Do paint the meadows with delight,","~The cuckoo then on every tree","~Mocks married men; for thus sings he:","~\"Cuckoo!","~Cuckoo, cuckoo!\" O, word of fear,","~Unpleasing to a married ear.","~When shepherds pipe on oaten straws,","~And merry larks are ploughmen's clocks,","~When turtles tread, and rooks and daws,","~And maidens bleach their summer smocks,","~The cuckoo then, on every tree,","~Mocks married men, for thus sings he:","~\"Cuckoo!","~Cuckoo, cuckoo!\" O, word of fear,","~Unpleasing to a married ear."],"id":1171,"m":"(Song) When speckled daisies and blue violets, silver-white lady's-smocks and yellow buttercups paint the meadows with delight, then the cuckoo in every tree mocks married men, because this is what it sings: 'Cuckoo! Cuckoo, cuckoo!' Oh, word of fear (cuckold!), unpleasant to a married man's ear. When shepherds play pipes of oat straw, and merry larks wake the ploughmen like clocks, when turtle-doves, rooks and jackdaws mate, and girls bleach their summer smocks in the sun, then the cuckoo in every tree mocks married men, because this is what it sings: 'Cuckoo! Cuckoo, cuckoo!' Oh, word of fear, unpleasant to a married man's ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINTER","lines":["~When icicles hang by the wall,","~And Dick the shepherd blows his nail,","~And Tom bears logs into the hall,","~And milk comes frozen home in pail,","~When blood is nipped, and ways be foul,","~Then nightly sings the staring owl:","~\"Tu-whit, Tu-whoo!\" A merry note,","~While greasy Joan doth keel the pot.","~When all aloud the wind doth blow,","~And coughing drowns the parson's saw,","~And birds sit brooding in the snow,","~And Marian's nose looks red and raw,","~When roasted crabs hiss in the bowl,","~Then nightly sings the staring owl:","~\"Tu-whit, Tu-whoo!\" A merry note,","~While greasy Joan doth keel the pot."],"id":1172,"m":"(Song) When icicles hang by the wall, and Dick the shepherd blows on his fingernails, and Tom carries logs into the hall, and the milk comes home frozen in the pail, when blood is chilled and roads are muddy, then every night the staring owl sings 'Tu-whit, tu-whoo!', a merry note, while greasy Joan stirs the pot to stop it boiling over. When the wind blows loudly, and coughing drowns out the parson's sermon, and birds sit brooding in the snow, and Marian's nose looks red and raw, when roasted crab-apples hiss in the bowl of punch, then every night the staring owl sings 'Tu-whit, tu-whoo!', a merry note, while greasy Joan stirs the pot."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARMADO","lines":["The words of Mercury are harsh after the songs of Apollo.","You that way, we this way."],"id":1173,"m":"The words of Mercury, the messenger, are harsh after the songs of Apollo. You go that way; we'll go this way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1174}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"The King","group":"The court of Navarre","desc":"Ferdinand, King of Navarre. A young king who wants fame through learning. He founds a 'little academe' and makes his three lords swear to study with him for three years, fasting, sleeping little and seeing no woman. When the Princess of France arrives on business he lodges her in the park rather than break his vow, but falls in love with her at once. Overheard reading his love sonnet, he scolds Longaville and Dumaine for doing the same, then leads the lords' wooing in Russian disguise. When the Princess's father dies, she sends him to a hermitage for a year to prove his love."},{"name":"Berowne","group":"The court of Navarre","desc":"The wittiest of the King's lords and the play's sharpest talker. He mocks the oath as 'barren tasks, too hard to keep' but signs it, predicting he will be the last to break it. He falls for Rosaline, sends her a sonnet that goes astray, spies on his friends' love poems and lectures them, until his own letter exposes him. He then argues brilliantly that 'women's eyes' are the true books. In the end Rosaline sentences him to spend a year making the sick laugh, to cure his mocking wit."},{"name":"Longaville","group":"The court of Navarre","desc":"One of the King's three lords. He is first to sign, cheerful that 'the mind shall banquet, though the body pine', and he invented the penalty for women who come near the court. He falls in love with Maria and writes her a sonnet arguing that a vow to a goddess does not break a vow against women. Maria tells him to wait a year."},{"name":"Dumaine","group":"The court of Navarre","desc":"The youngest of the King's lords, praised by Katharine as accomplished and well-meaning. He gives up the world's 'grosser' pleasures for philosophy, then falls for Katharine and writes her a love poem. Overheard by all three friends, he wishes they were in love too, 'for none offend where all alike do dote'. Katharine postpones him for a year and a day."},{"name":"The Princess","group":"The French embassy","desc":"The Princess of France. The French King's daughter, sent to negotiate over Aquitaine and a debt of a hundred thousand crowns. Witty, poised and sceptical, she refuses to be flattered, teases the King about his oath and leads her ladies in turning the men's Russian masque against them. She is gracious to the amateur actors of the Nine Worthies. When news comes that her father is dead she becomes queen, and refuses to make 'a world-without-end bargain' in a moment: the King must prove himself for a year."},{"name":"Rosaline","group":"The French embassy","desc":"A lady attending the Princess, dark-eyed and dark-browed, and Berowne's match in wit. She trades sharp lines with him from their first meeting, plans to torture him into being 'my fool', and plays the Princess during the Muscovite masque, refusing to dance. Her final task for Berowne, to jest for a year among the dying, is the play's sharpest test of what wit is for."},{"name":"Maria","group":"The French embassy","desc":"A lady attending the Princess. She met Longaville at a wedding in Normandy and describes his sharp wit and blunt will. He sends her pearls and a long letter. At the end she promises to change her mourning black for 'a faithful friend' after a year."},{"name":"Katharine","group":"The French embassy","desc":"A lady attending the Princess and heir of Alençon. Dumaine loves her and sends her gloves and verses. She remembers a sister who died of love, which brings the first shadow of death into the last act. In the masque she mocks Longaville as a calf, and she tells Dumaine to wait a year and a day."},{"name":"Boyet","group":"The French embassy","desc":"An older lord in the Princess's service, her messenger and her ladies' sparring partner. He reads the signs of the King's love, overhears the lords planning their Russian disguise and warns the ladies, and heckles the Nine Worthies. Berowne calls him 'wit's pedlar' and 'honey-tongued Boyet'."},{"name":"Marcadé","group":"The French embassy","desc":"A messenger from France who enters at the height of the comedy with the news that the Princess's father has died. His few lines change the whole mood of the ending."},{"name":"Armado","group":"Armado's household","desc":"Don Adriano de Armado. 'A fantastical Spaniard' kept at court to entertain the King with high-flown stories. He loves long words and grand comparisons. He reports Costard for meeting Jaquenetta in the park, then falls in love with her himself and writes her a ridiculous letter. He plays Hector in the Nine Worthies, is humiliated by the news that Jaquenetta is pregnant, and ends by vowing to work the plough for her for three years."},{"name":"Moth","group":"Armado's household","desc":"Armado's tiny, quick-witted page. He runs rings round his master with puns and riddles, teaches him how lovers are supposed to behave, delivers the lords' masque speech (and is put off his lines when the ladies turn their backs) and plays the baby Hercules in the show of the Worthies. (Some editions spell his name Mote, which in Shakespeare's time sounded the same: a speck.)"},{"name":"Costard","group":"Armado's household","desc":"A country clown caught with Jaquenetta, and so the first person punished under the King's decree. He talks his way round words he half understands ('remuneration', 'guerdon'), mixes up the two letters he is given to carry, and plays Pompey the Great in the Worthies. It is Costard who reveals that Jaquenetta is pregnant by Armado."},{"name":"Jaquenetta","group":"Armado's household","desc":"A country girl who works as a dairymaid in the park. Costard is caught with her, Armado loves her, and she is unimpressed by his speeches ('With that face?'). She asks the parson to read her the letter she was given, which turns out to be Berowne's sonnet to Rosaline. By the end she is two months pregnant."},{"name":"Dull","group":"The village","desc":"Antony Dull. The King's constable, who arrests Costard. He mangles words, understands little and says so: 'Nor understood none neither, sir.' His plain sense about the deer being a pricket is right, though the scholars mock him."},{"name":"Holofernes","group":"The village","desc":"A schoolmaster who loves Latin tags, lists of synonyms and showing off. He writes an alliterative epitaph for the Princess's deer, criticises Berowne's sonnet, mocks Armado's spelling and devises the pageant of the Nine Worthies, in which he plays Judas Maccabaeus and is mercilessly heckled. His protest, 'This is not generous, not gentle, not humble', is one of the play's few rebukes to the lords."},{"name":"Nathaniel","group":"The village","desc":"Sir Nathaniel. The parish curate and Holofernes's admiring friend. He praises everything the schoolmaster says, reads Berowne's sonnet aloud and plays Alexander the Great in the Worthies, where he is so flustered he forgets his lines."},{"name":"The Forester","group":"The village","desc":"The man who shows the Princess where to stand to shoot the deer. When he blunders over calling her 'fair', she tips him for his honesty."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"The King of Navarre makes Berowne, Longaville and Dumaine swear to study with him for three years: no women, one day a week of fasting, one meal a day and three hours of sleep a night. Berowne argues against it but signs, and points out that the Princess of France is about to arrive, so the King must break the rule against women 'on mere necessity'. Constable Dull brings a letter from Don Armado reporting that Costard was caught with Jaquenetta in the park. The King sentences Costard to a week on bran and water in Armado's keeping.","key":["The oath is a grand plan for fame, but it ignores human nature, as Berowne says at once.","Berowne's wit lets him argue any side: he attacks study, then signs, then predicts the oath will fail.","Costard is the first to be punished under the decree, and the lords will soon be guilty of the same 'crime'."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long do the lords swear to study?","o":["One year","Three years","Seven years","Until the King marries"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the penalty for any woman who comes within a mile of the court?","o":["Banishment","Prison","Losing her tongue","A fine of a hundred crowns"],"a":2},{"q":"Why must the King break his own rule at once?","o":["Armado is in love","The Princess of France is coming on an embassy","His mother wants to visit","Costard has broken it first"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Don Armado confesses to his page Moth that he is in love, and comforts himself with great men who loved: Hercules and Samson. His love is Jaquenetta, the country girl he caught with Costard. Dull brings Costard and Jaquenetta; Armado declares his love and she brushes him off. Armado has Costard locked up and, alone, admits that love will make him break his oath. He resolves to write sonnets.","key":["Armado's love mirrors and parodies what will happen to the lords.","Moth is far quicker than his master and mocks him constantly.","Armado's language is absurdly inflated: he talks of love as war but cannot win a dairymaid with it."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which great lovers does Moth name to comfort Armado?","o":["Paris and Troilus","Hercules and Samson","Caesar and Antony","Solomon and David"],"a":1},{"q":"Who does Armado love?","o":["Rosaline","Jaquenetta","Maria","The Princess"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Armado decide to do at the end of the scene?","o":["Leave Navarre","Fight Costard","Write love poetry","Confess to the King"],"a":2}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"The Princess of France arrives with Rosaline, Maria, Katharine and Boyet. She sends Boyet to announce her, and her ladies describe the lords they have met before: Maria knows Longaville, Katharine Dumaine and Rosaline Berowne. The King comes but will not let her into his court because of his oath, and lodges her in the park. They dispute a debt of a hundred thousand crowns and the region of Aquitaine; the receipts will arrive tomorrow. Berowne and Rosaline spar. After the King leaves, each lord comes back to ask Boyet the name of a lady. Boyet tells the Princess that the King is in love with her.","key":["The ladies take the men's words literally and turn them back on them.","Each lady's praise of a lord hints that the ladies are attracted too.","The King's oath makes him a bad host: the Princess points out that keeping it is a sin and breaking it is a sin."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does the King lodge the Princess?","o":["In his palace","In the park, in tents","In the village inn","In Armado's house"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the diplomatic business about?","o":["A royal marriage","Aquitaine and a hundred thousand crowns","A border war","Trade in wine"],"a":1},{"q":"Who tells the Princess the King is 'infected' with love?","o":["Rosaline","Maria","Boyet","Berowne"],"a":2}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Moth sings for Armado and lectures him on how to behave as a fashionable lover. He fetches Costard, who has hurt his shin, and the three have a muddled argument about the word 'l'envoi'. Armado frees Costard on condition that he takes a letter to Jaquenetta, and pays him three farthings, a 'remuneration'. Berowne then pays Costard more, a 'guerdon', to carry a letter to Rosaline. Alone, Berowne admits in disgust that he, love's scourge, is in love, and with a dark-eyed woman he doesn't even trust.","key":["Costard now carries two letters, which sets up the mix-up of Act 4.","Costard treats grand words as names for sums of money: words have a price in this play.","Berowne's soliloquy shows the scoffer caught by Cupid, still mocking himself as he falls."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Costard think 'remuneration' means?","o":["Freedom","A love letter","Three farthings","A punishment"],"a":2},{"q":"Who does Berowne ask Costard to give his letter to?","o":["Jaquenetta","Rosaline","The Princess","Maria"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Berowne describe Cupid in his soliloquy?","o":["As a giant","As a 'wimpled, whining, purblind, wayward boy'","As a kind teacher","As a beggar"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"The Princess goes hunting in the park and jokes with the Forester about her beauty and with her ladies about seeking praise. Costard arrives with what he says is Berowne's letter to Rosaline, but Boyet reads it aloud and it is Armado's high-flown letter to Jaquenetta, comparing himself to King Cophetua and her to a beggar maid. The Princess leaves; Boyet, Rosaline, Maria and Costard trade bawdy jokes about shooting and hitting the mark.","key":["The wrong letter is delivered, and the ladies are entertained by Armado's style.","The Princess admits she hunts 'for praise alone', a glance at the King's hunt for fame.","The hunting jokes tie love to the chase: who is the hunter and who the deer?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose letter does the Princess actually hear?","o":["Berowne's to Rosaline","Armado's to Jaquenetta","The King's to the Princess","Longaville's to Maria"],"a":1},{"q":"What story does Armado use to describe his love?","o":["Hero and Leander","King Cophetua and the beggar maid","Pyramus and Thisbe","Samson and Delilah"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does the Princess give the Forester money?","o":["For finding the deer","For admitting she is not fair","For carrying her bow","For a song"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"The schoolmaster Holofernes, the curate Sir Nathaniel and Dull discuss the deer the Princess has shot. Holofernes showers Latin on Dull, who insists the deer was a pricket, and recites an alliterative epitaph on it. Jaquenetta brings the letter Costard gave her and asks the parson to read it: it is Berowne's sonnet to Rosaline. Holofernes criticises its poetry and sends Jaquenetta to give it to the King, since it may matter. He invites Nathaniel to dinner.","key":["Holofernes and Nathaniel parody learning: words without sense, Latin without understanding.","Dull's plain answers are often more accurate than the scholars' displays.","The misdirected sonnet heads for the King, setting up the exposure in 4.3."],"quiz":[{"q":"What kind of deer does Dull say the Princess killed?","o":["A doe","A hart","A pricket","A sore"],"a":2},{"q":"Whose poem does Nathaniel read aloud?","o":["Armado's","Berowne's","Longaville's","Holofernes's"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Holofernes tell Jaquenetta to do with the letter?","o":["Burn it","Give it to the King","Return it to Armado","Keep it secret"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Berowne, alone with a poem, hides when the King comes in. The King reads a sonnet to the Princess and hides when Longaville enters; Longaville reads his sonnet to Maria and hides when Dumaine arrives with an ode to Katharine. Longaville steps out to scold Dumaine, the King to scold both, and Berowne to scold all three for hypocrisy. Then Jaquenetta and Costard bring Berowne's own letter; he tears it, Dumaine finds his name, and he confesses. The four argue over whose love is fairest, and the King asks Berowne to prove that their love is lawful. In a long speech he argues that women's eyes are the true books and that breaking the oath is the only way to keep faith with themselves. They resolve to woo the ladies with revels and masques.","key":["The overhearing scene is a comic chain in which each judge is judged in turn.","Berowne's exposure ends his pose as the one honest man.","His great speech is brilliant rhetoric: it turns perjury into 'religion', which is exactly what the ladies will distrust."],"quiz":[{"q":"In what order are the lovers overheard?","o":["Berowne, King, Longaville, Dumaine","King, Longaville, Dumaine","Dumaine, Longaville, King","Longaville, King, Berowne"],"a":1},{"q":"How is Berowne exposed?","o":["Rosaline tells the King","Costard and Jaquenetta bring his letter","Boyet overhears him","He confesses in a sonnet"],"a":1},{"q":"According to Berowne, where do men learn love's true lessons?","o":["In books","In women's eyes","In war","In music"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Holofernes and Nathaniel, after dinner, mock Armado's affected speech and his pronunciation. Armado arrives with Moth and Costard; Moth and Costard make fun of the scholars' 'feast of languages'. Armado announces that the King wants an entertainment for the Princess that afternoon, and Holofernes proposes a pageant of the Nine Worthies, casting Nathaniel, Costard, Moth, Armado and himself. Dull offers to play the drum.","key":["The pedants mock Armado for exactly the faults they share.","Moth's line about 'scraps' sums up the scholars: stolen words, little meaning.","The show of the Nine Worthies is planned, a play within the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Holofernes object to in Armado's speech?","o":["His Spanish accent","His pronunciation of words like 'debt' and 'doubt'","His swearing","His long silences"],"a":1},{"q":"What entertainment does Holofernes suggest?","o":["A masque of Russians","The Nine Worthies","A hunting song","A dance of shepherds"],"a":1},{"q":"Which Worthy will Moth play?","o":["Pompey","Hercules","Hector","Alexander"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"The ladies compare the lords' gifts and verses. Boyet warns that the men are coming disguised as Russians, so the ladies mask and swap favours: each lord woos the wrong lady, and Moth's speech collapses. When the men return as themselves the ladies mock the 'Russians' until the men confess, and Berowne renounces fancy language. Costard, Nathaniel, Holofernes, Moth and Armado perform the Nine Worthies while the lords heckle them; Costard reveals Jaquenetta is pregnant by Armado and they almost fight. Then Marcadé brings news that the Princess's father is dead. The Princess prepares to leave and will not accept the King's sudden love: he must spend a year in a hermitage. Each lady sets her lover a year's wait, and Rosaline sends Berowne to make the sick laugh. Armado vows to farm for Jaquenetta, and the play ends with songs of Spring and Winter.","key":["The ladies win every round of the battle of wits, and the men are 'forsworn' again by mistake.","The lords' rudeness to the Worthies shows wit turning cruel, before news of death changes everything.","The comedy refuses the usual ending: 'Jack hath not Jill', and every marriage is postponed a year."],"quiz":[{"q":"How do the ladies trick the disguised lords?","o":["They hide","They mask and swap the lords' favours","They send Boyet in their place","They pretend to speak only French"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does Marcadé bring?","o":["War with Spain","The King of France is dead","Aquitaine is lost","Jaquenetta has married"],"a":1},{"q":"What task does Rosaline give Berowne?","o":["To live as a hermit","To fight in the wars","To spend a year making the sick laugh","To write a hundred sonnets"],"a":2}]}},"notes":{"2":"The opening speech is about fame and death. The King wants to defeat 'cormorant devouring time' through study, a plan the ending tests with a real death and a year of waiting.","3":"Each lord signs with a rhymed vow. Notice how much of this play rhymes: it sounds like a game of words from the start.","7":"Berowne's speech repeats 'I hope... is not enrolled there' like a refrain. He reads the rules aloud to make them sound ridiculous.","15":"Berowne's twisting of 'study' shows his favourite method: take a word and bend its meaning until the argument turns round.","17":"Notice the word 'light' changing meaning: light of truth, eyesight, and the light of a lady's eyes. This speech sets up his great speech in 4.3.","21":"Berowne's 'green geese' line makes no sense on purpose: he is mocking the others' rhyming. 'Something then in rhyme' admits it.","27":"Berowne's case for doing things in season is picked up at the end, when the play closes with songs of spring and winter.","37":"The rule is broken before the ink is dry: the Princess's visit is diplomatic business the King cannot avoid.","41":"'On mere necessity' becomes Berowne's escape clause. He signs, but has already excused himself.","44":"The King hires Armado as entertainment. The court laughs at his words while making its own empty vows.","62":"Costard puns on legal language ('in manner and form following') and on 'manor' and 'form' (a bench). The clown's wordplay matches the lords'.","76":"Armado's letter is a parade of synonyms and long words. Compare Holofernes's style in 4.2 and 5.1.","95":"Costard's 'wench, damsel, virgin, maid' trick fails: renaming a thing doesn't change it. The lords will try the same trick with their oath.","149":"Armado's confession comes before any of the lords': the subplot runs ahead of the main plot and parodies it.","168":"Moth's rhyme argues that red and white cheeks can hide faults: looks can deceive. Beauty and appearance will be debated all play.","205":"Armado's soliloquy mirrors Berowne's in 3.1: both blame Cupid and both turn to writing sonnets.","210":"The Princess rejects flattery from the start. She is a hard audience for fine words, which matters at the end.","217":"Maria, Katharine and Rosaline each praise a lord. The Princess notices: 'Are they all in love?'","231":"The Princess takes the King's 'welcome' literally: you can't welcome someone to a court she isn't allowed into.","239":"She sees the trap in his vow at once: keeping it breaks the laws of hospitality, breaking it breaks his oath.","244":"Berowne and Rosaline speak in short rhymed lines, batting words back and forth like tennis. She always has the last word.","259":"The business of Aquitaine and the hundred thousand crowns is never settled. It gives the visit a reason, and is forgotten once love takes over.","289":"Each lord comes back alone to ask a lady's name: the lords are already in love and hiding it from one another.","325":"Boyet's description of the King's eyes is a sonnet-like speech in rhymed couplets: the language of love poetry describes a lover.","344":"Moth's comic lesson lists the fashionable gestures of a lover. The lords later act out many of them.","376":"Costard mixes up 'l'envoi' (the closing lines of a poem) with 'salve' (ointment). The scene is a riot of misheard words.","402":"Costard learns 'remuneration' as a sum of money. In 3.1 the play keeps asking what words are worth.","418":"Berowne gives Costard his letter. Costard now carries two letters, for two very different ladies.","423":"Berowne's soliloquy is the turning point for the cleverest lord. He insults Cupid, women and Rosaline while admitting he loves her.","429":"The hunt in the park is a picture of courtship: the women are the hunters here.","439":"The Princess admits she hunts 'for praise alone'. Like the King's oath, her sport is about reputation.","454":"Armado's letter retells the story of King Cophetua and the beggar maid, a ballad in which a king marries a beggar. He casts himself as the king.","465":"The Princess gives Rosaline the letter to keep: 'it will be thine another day'. She guesses that Berowne's letter has gone astray.","471":"The jokes about horns and hitting the mark are bawdy. The ladies are as quick with innuendo as Boyet.","500":"Holofernes cannot say a word without giving its Latin version and three synonyms. Compare Armado's letter.","507":"Nathaniel's praise of learning describes books as food: compare Longaville's 'The mind shall banquet'.","518":"The epitaph is a heavy-handed alliteration on 'p' and a joke about deer names: pricket, sore and sorel.","534":"Berowne's poem is a sonnet in long six-stress lines, and it argues exactly what he will argue in 4.3: that breaking his vow for her is no crime.","537":"Holofernes sends the letter to the King out of duty. It is the pedant who triggers the lords' exposure.","550":"Berowne speaks in prose here, which suits his confused, comic state.","555":"The King's sonnet is full of tears and light. Each lord's poem is in a different form: the King's sonnet, Longaville's sonnet, Dumaine's song-like ode.","575":"'All hid' was a children's game. The audience watches Berowne watch the King watch Longaville watch Dumaine: a comic chain of eavesdroppers.","594":"Dumaine's poem is in short, songlike lines. It was later printed in an anthology called The Passionate Pilgrim (1599).","597":"Berowne's speech is pure hypocrisy, and the audience knows it. His list of fallen heroes (Hercules, Solomon, Nestor, Timon) is a comic catalogue.","621":"Dumaine's picking up the torn pieces exposes Berowne. Writing gives every lover away in this play.","629":"Berowne's line 'Young blood doth not obey an old decree' sums up the oath's failure: nature beats law.","637":"Fair skin was the fashion. Berowne argues that Rosaline's dark beauty is the true fair, and fashionable red-and-white is painted.","656":"The great speech is a formal argument: what you swore, why it was wrong, what love does to every sense, and the conclusion. It is persuasive and also an example of the wit that can justify anything.","661":"Berowne plans 'revels, dances, masques'. The courtship becomes a show, which the ladies will review harshly.","672":"Holofernes mocks Armado for pronouncing 'debt' without the 'b'. In fact the 'b' was a learned addition; Holofernes is a pedant about spelling.","683":"'Honorificabilitudinitatibus' is a medieval Latin word meaning roughly 'with honours'. It is the longest word in Shakespeare.","708":"The Nine Worthies were a set of famous heroes from history and legend. The comic cast is wildly unsuited to their parts.","734":"Katharine's sister died of love. It is the first mention of death in the play's last act.","759":"Rosaline wants power over Berowne. Her plan matches the Princess's plan for the King: test him, make him serve.","769":"Boyet acts as the ladies' spy, overhearing the men's plans as they overheard each other in 4.3.","772":"Swapping favours means each lord will woo the wrong lady while thinking he has the right one.","784":"Moth's speech collapses when the ladies turn their backs. The men's show fails in its first lines.","806":"'Measured many miles to tread a measure': Rosaline takes the pun literally and asks how many inches are in a mile.","815":"The King woos Rosaline thinking she is the Princess. Notice the moon imagery: the masked lady is changeable.","869":"Boyet's speech praises the ladies' wit as faster than arrows and bullets: the battle imagery of the play continues.","905":"Berowne mocks Boyet as a ladies' man and a 'pedlar' of wit. He suspects Boyet told the ladies their plan, rightly.","936":"Berowne gives up fine words in a speech full of fine words. Then he says 'sans', a French affectation, and Rosaline pounces.","963":"The men realise they swore love to the wrong women: 'forsworn in will and error'. Even their new vows are broken by mistake.","987":"The Princess wants to see the show: the best entertainment is one that tries hard and fails. She is a generous audience.","1003":"Notice how the casting fits the actors: the down-to-earth clown is the best Worthy, the pedant plays a 'Judas' and is betrayed by his audience, and the braggart plays the greatest warrior, Hector.","1010":"Costard's Pompey is the most successful Worthy. He makes one small mistake, 'Big' for 'Great'.","1024":"Costard's kind defence of Nathaniel ('a foolish mild man... a marvellous good neighbour') is more gracious than anything the lords say.","1059":"Holofernes's rebuke is fair. The lords' mockery has turned cruel, and he is right that it is not gentlemanly.","1085":"Armado's 'the sweet war-man is dead and rotten' is comic, but it asks the lords to respect the dead. Real news of a death follows soon.","1093":"Costard's revelation that Jaquenetta is pregnant pulls the subplot's love story down to earth.","1118":"Marcadé's interruption is one of Shakespeare's most famous sudden turns. The Princess guesses the news before he can finish.","1128":"The King's speech is tangled and abstract. The Princess says she does not understand it; Berowne tries plain words instead.","1136":"The Princess's terms: a year in a hermitage, hardship and solitude. If his love survives, she will be his.","1152":"Rosaline's task turns wit into a test of kindness: can Berowne's jokes help the dying?","1158":"Berowne notices that the play is not ending as comedies should. The play knows it is a play.","1160":"A play was traditionally expected to cover a short span of time; a year's wait cannot fit inside one, so the happy ending is pushed beyond the end of the play.","1171":"The two songs set spring against winter: love and the cuckoo's warning against hardship and homely life. They end the play outside the park's games.","1173":"Mercury was the messenger god (and Marcadé is a messenger); Apollo was the god of song. The last line contrasts news and music."},"gloss":{"abortive":"born too early","academe":"academy, a place of study","acquittances":"receipts","affections":"passions, feelings","agate":"precious stone carved as a seal","amain":"at full speed","antic":"comic dance or show","Argus":"giant with a hundred eyes, set to guard someone","armipotent":"mighty in arms","Ates":"goddesses of discord","attainder":"condemnation, disgrace","avaunt":"go away","barren":"empty, fruitless","bate":"blunt, lessen","beadle":"officer who whipped offenders","bedrid":"bedridden","bodkin":"long hairpin","bombast":"cotton padding","brawl":"a French dance (branle); also a quarrel","butt-shaft":"blunt arrow","canary":"a lively dance","canus":"dog (mock Latin)","canzonet":"short song","capon":"chicken; also a love letter","caret":"is missing (Latin)","carnation":"flesh-coloured, pink","Cerberus":"three-headed dog guarding the underworld","charge-house":"school","choler":"anger","cipher":"zero, nothing","cittern":"a lute-like instrument with a carved head","close-stool":"chamber pot in a chair","codpieces":"pouches on men's breeches","cog":"cheat at dice","competitors":"partners","complexion":"temperament; colour of the face","concolinel":"probably the title of a song","condign":"well-deserved","converse":"conversation","coppice":"small wood","cormorant":"greedy (a cormorant is a gluttonous seabird)","crabs":"crab-apples","damask":"pink and white like a damask rose","daws":"jackdaws","deuce-ace":"a two and a one on the dice","dey-woman":"dairy-woman","Dictynna":"a name for Diana, goddess of the moon","dishclout":"dishcloth","dispatch":"settlement, quick answer","dispensation":"permission to break a rule","dominical":"red letter marking Sundays in a calendar","duello":"rules of duelling","durance":"imprisonment","edict":"official decree","embassage":"message","embassy":"diplomatic mission","enfranchise":"set free","enrolled":"written down officially","epitheton":"descriptive phrase, epithet","ergo":"therefore (Latin)","excrement":"outgrowth (hair, a moustache)","extemporal":"improvised","fadge":"succeed, work out","fairings":"presents","falchion":"curved sword","familiar":"evil spirit serving a witch; also a close friend","farborough":"Dull's mistake for 'third-borough', a constable","favour":"love token; also kindness, looks","festinately":"quickly","fire-new":"brand-new, freshly minted","flap-dragon":"raisin snatched from burning brandy","fleered":"grinned, sneered","forsworn":"having broken an oath","gentility":"good manners","gibing":"mocking","gig":"spinning top","guerdon":"reward","hackney":"hired horse; also a prostitute","haud credo":"I don't believe it (Latin)","hermitage":"hermit's lonely dwelling","Hiems":"Winter (Latin)","hight":"is called","hobby-horse":"figure in a morris dance; also a loose woman","hogshead":"large barrel","hornbook":"a child's alphabet board","humour":"mood, temperament","hyperboles":"exaggerations","Ilion":"Troy","importunes":"begs urgently","incony":"fine, dainty","indubitate":"undoubted","inkle":"linen tape","insanie":"madness","juvenal":"youth","keel":"stir to stop boiling over","kersey":"coarse woollen cloth","l'envoi":"closing lines of a poem","lady-smocks":"meadow flowers, cuckooflower","liege":"lord, sovereign","maculate":"spotted, stained","malmsey":"sweet wine","manage":"horsemanship, a charge in a tournament","manus":"hand (Latin)","margent":"margin of a page","meed":"reward","mehercle":"by Hercules (Latin)","metheglin":"spiced mead","minnow":"tiny fish, a nobody","Monarcho":"a famous mad Italian who boasted at Elizabeth's court","mortified":"dead to worldly pleasures","Muscovites":"Russians","non point":"not at all (French)","novum":"a dice game","o'erparted":"given too big a part","orthography":"spelling","ostentation":"show, display","overshot":"missing the target","Ovidius Naso":"the Roman poet Ovid","pair-taunt":"a winning hand at cards","paritors":"church officers who summoned sinners to court","passado":"forward thrust in fencing","pates":"heads, brains","pathetical":"moving; also 'pathetic'","pauca verba":"few words (Latin)","penthouse":"a sloping roof over a shop front","peregrinate":"like a traveller, foreign","perjured":"having broken an oath","phantasime":"fantastic fellow","pia mater":"the brain (literally its membrane)","picked":"over-refined, fussy","pied":"many-coloured","pine":"waste away, starve","plackets":"slits in petticoats","plantain":"a leaf used to heal wounds","plodders":"slow, dull workers","point-devise":"precise, fastidious","pomewater":"a kind of apple","Pompion":"pumpkin (Costard's mistake for Pompey)","posteriors":"later part; also backside","pricket":"a deer in its second year","progeny":"offspring, a child","purblind":"half-blind","pursents":"Costard's 'presents', represents","quondam":"former, the other","quoniam":"since (Latin)","recompense":"reward","remuneration":"payment","russet":"homespun brown cloth","sans":"without (French)","schedule":"written document","scutcheon":"coat of arms","sententious":"full of wise sayings","shrewd":"mischievous","sirrah":"form of address to an inferior","sneaping":"nipping, biting","snuff":"burning candle-wick; offence","solemnized":"celebrated formally","solicitor":"advocate","sorel":"a deer in its third year","specialties":"sealed documents","squier":"ruler, square","statute-caps":"woollen caps worn by citizens by law","subscribe":"sign","superscript":"address on a letter","swain":"country fellow","tables":"backgammon","tabor":"small drum","taffeta":"fine silk","tapster":"barman","targe":"small shield","the hay":"a country dance","thrasonical":"boastful (after Thraso, a braggart in Latin comedy)","three-piled":"thick-piled, like the best velvet","tilting":"jousting","tittles":"tiny marks, trifles","trencher":"wooden plate","troth":"word, promise","turtles":"turtle-doves","ushering":"acting as a gentleman usher","vassal":"servant","Ver":"Spring (Latin)","vicegerent":"deputy ruler","videlicet":"namely","visor":"mask","vizors":"masks","votaries":"people under a vow","votary":"someone under a vow","welkin":"sky, heavens","wench":"girl, young woman","wight":"person","wimpled":"blindfolded, veiled","woolward":"wearing wool next to the skin, as penance","wormwood":"bitter herb","wort":"sweet unfermented beer","wot":"know","ycleped":"called","zany":"clown's assistant"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,6,"Let the fame that everyone chases in life be carved on our brass tombs, and honour us even when death has disgraced us. In spite of greedy, devouring time, what we achieve while we are alive can buy the kind of honour that blunts time's sharp scythe and makes us heirs to eternity."],[7,13,"So, brave conquerors (and you are conquerors, since you are at war with your own feelings and the huge army of worldly desires), our recent decree will stand firm. Navarre will be the wonder of the world; our court will be a little academy, quiet and thoughtful, devoted to the art of living."],[14,22,"You three, Berowne, Dumaine and Longaville, have sworn to live with me for three years as fellow students, and to keep the rules written down in this document. You have taken the oath; now sign your names, so that anyone who breaks the smallest rule strikes down his own honour with his own hand. If you are ready to do what you swore, sign your solemn oaths, and keep them too."]],"7":[[0,3,"I can only repeat what they've promised. I've already sworn this much, my lord: to live and study here for three years. But there are other strict rules too:"],[4,13,"not to see a woman for that time, which I very much hope isn't written down there; to eat nothing one day a week, and only one meal on every other day, which I hope isn't written down there either; and to sleep only three hours a night and never be seen to nod off during the day, when I used to sleep soundly all night and turn half the day into night as well. I hope that isn't written down there."],[14,15,"Oh, these are empty tasks, too hard to keep: no ladies, study, fasting, no sleep."]],"15":[[0,7,"All right then, I'll swear to study in that way, to know what I am forbidden to know. For example: to study where I can find a good dinner when I'm strictly forbidden to feast; or where to meet a lovely mistress when mistresses are hidden from ordinary view; or, having sworn an oath too hard to keep, to study how to break it without breaking my word."],[8,10,"If that's what study gains, then study teaches what it doesn't yet know. Swear me to that and I'll never say no."]],"17":[[0,7,"Why, all pleasures are empty, but the emptiest is the one bought with pain that only earns more pain: like painfully poring over a book to find the light of truth, while all that time the truth is blinding your eyes. Eyes looking for light rob themselves of light; so before you find where light lies hidden in the darkness, your own light goes dark because you've lost your eyesight."],[8,11,"Teach me instead how to please the eye by fixing it on a lovelier eye, a woman's: even if that dazzles it, that eye will be its guide and give back the light it was blinded by."],[12,21,"Study is like the glorious sun in the sky, which won't let impudent eyes search it deeply. Constant plodders have never gained much except second-hand authority from other people's books. Astronomers, those earthly godfathers who name every fixed star, get no more good from their starry nights than people who walk about not knowing what the stars are. Knowing too much just means knowing names, and any godfather can give a name."]],"37":[[0,3,"A law dangerous to good manners. [Reads] 'Item: if any man is seen talking with a woman during the three years, he shall suffer whatever public shame the rest of the court can think up.'"],[4,11,"You'll have to break this rule yourself, my lord, because you know very well that the French King's daughter is coming here as an ambassador to speak with you, a lady of gentle grace and perfect majesty, about handing over Aquitaine to her old, sick, bedridden father. So either this rule is pointless, or the admired Princess is coming here for nothing."]],"41":[[0,5,"Necessity will make us all break our oaths three thousand times in these three years. Every man is born with his passions, and they can't be mastered by force, only by God's special grace. If I break my word, this will be my defence: I broke my oath because I had to."],[6,11,"So I sign my name to all the laws, and whoever breaks the smallest of them is condemned to eternal shame. Temptations come to others as they come to me; but I believe, though I seem so unwilling, I will be the last to keep his oath to the last."]],"44":[[0,7,"Yes, there is. Our court, as you know, is haunted by an elegant traveller from Spain, a man dressed in all the world's newest fashions, who has a whole mint of phrases in his head; a man who is enchanted by the music of his own vain voice as if it were magic harmony; a man of such fine manners that right and wrong would choose him as referee in their quarrel."],[8,14,"This creature of fantasy, called Armado, will entertain us between our studies by telling us, in high-flown words, about the worth of many knights from sunburnt Spain who were lost in the world's wars. I don't know what you enjoy, my lords, but I confess I love to hear him lie, and I'll use him as my entertainer."]],"76":[[0,4,"[Reads] 'So it is: besieged by black melancholy, I entrusted my dark, oppressive mood to the healthy medicine of your health-giving air, and, as I am a gentleman, went for a walk. The time when? About the sixth hour, when animals graze most, birds peck best and men sit down to the nourishment called supper. So much for the time when."],[5,12,"Now for the ground which: which, I mean, I walked on. It is called your park. Then for the place where: where, I mean, I came upon that obscene and most unnatural event that draws the ebony ink from my snow-white pen, which you now view, behold, survey or see. But as for the place where: it lies north-north-east and by east from the west corner of your intricately laid-out garden. There I saw that low-spirited peasant, that lowly little fish of your amusement...'"]],"168":[[0,7,"If she is made of white and red, you'll never know her faults; because faults make cheeks blush red, and fear makes them pale white. So if she is afraid or guilty, you won't be able to tell from this, because her cheeks always keep the colours she was born with."],[8,8,"A dangerous rhyme, master, against the case for white and red."]],"205":[[0,4,"I love the very ground, which is lowly, where her shoe, which is lowlier, guided by her foot, which is lowliest, steps. If I love, I'll break my oath, which is strong evidence of falseness. And how can a love be true that is begun falsely? Love is an evil spirit; Love is a devil. There is no bad angel except Love."],[5,9,"Yet Samson was tempted like this, and he was extremely strong; Solomon was seduced like this, and he was very wise. Cupid's blunt arrow is too hard for Hercules' club, so it is far too much for a Spaniard's rapier. The rules of duelling won't help me: Cupid doesn't care for the forward thrust or the formal duel. It shames him to be called a boy, but his glory is to conquer men."],[10,13,"Farewell, courage; rust, rapier; be quiet, drum, for your master is in love. Yes, he loves. Help me, some god of improvised rhyme, for I'm sure I'll turn into a sonnet. Invent, wit; write, pen; for I'm good for whole volumes in folio."]],"209":[[0,7,"Now, madam, gather your finest powers. Think who the King your father is sending, to whom he is sending her, and what her mission is: you yourself, prized by the whole world, to negotiate with the sole heir of every perfection a man can have, the matchless King of Navarre; and the matter is nothing less than Aquitaine, a dowry fit for a queen."],[8,11,"So be as lavish now with all your precious charm as Nature was when she made such charms precious by starving the rest of the world and giving them all to you."]],"210":[[0,6,"Good Lord Boyet, my beauty, ordinary as it is, doesn't need the painted decoration of your praise. Beauty is judged by the eye that sees it, not sold off by the cheap patter of pedlars. I'm less proud to hear you tell my worth than you are eager to be thought clever for spending your wit on praising mine."],[7,21,"But now to set the task-setter a task. Good Boyet, you know that rumour, which tells everything, is spreading the news that Navarre has made a vow: no woman may come near his silent court until three years of painful study are over. So it seems necessary to us, before we go through his forbidden gates, to find out what he wants; and for that, trusting your worth, we choose you as our most persuasive advocate. Tell him the daughter of the King of France, on serious business that needs a quick settlement, asks to speak with His Grace in person. Hurry and tell him, while we wait for his royal answer like humble petitioners."]],"217":[[0,3,"I know him, madam. I saw this Longaville in Normandy, at the wedding feast of Lord Perigort and the beautiful heiress of Jaques Falconbridge."],[4,11,"He is thought a man of outstanding gifts, well trained in learning, glorious in battle. Anything he wants to do well suits him. The only stain on the shine of his fine qualities, if such a shine can be stained at all, is a sharp wit joined to a will too blunt: his wit has the power to cut, and his will always wants it to spare no one who comes within its reach."]],"259":[[0,8,"Madam, your father mentions here the payment of a hundred thousand crowns, which is only half of the whole sum my father spent on his wars. But suppose he or we had received that sum, though neither of us has: there would still be another hundred thousand unpaid, and as security for that, part of Aquitaine is pledged to us, although it isn't worth the money."],[9,20,"So if the King your father will repay just that one unpaid half, we'll give up our right to Aquitaine and keep good friendship with His Majesty. But it seems that is not what he intends: here he demands to be repaid a hundred thousand crowns, rather than offering to pay a hundred thousand crowns to get his claim to Aquitaine back. We would much rather part with Aquitaine, stripped of value as it is, and have the money our father lent."],[21,24,"Dear Princess, if his demands weren't so far from reasonable, your lovely self would make something in my heart give way against reason, and you'd go back to France well satisfied."]],"423":[[0,12,"And I, of all people, in love! I, who have been love's whip, a real beadle punishing every fanciful sigh, a critic, no, a night-watchman, a bossy schoolmaster over the boy Cupid, than whom no mortal was more grand! This blindfolded, whining, half-blind, wilful boy, this Signior Junior, giant-dwarf, Lord Cupid, regent of love poems, lord of folded arms, the anointed king of sighs and groans, master of all idlers and malcontents, dread prince of petticoats, king of codpieces, sole emperor and great general of the officers who hunt down sexual sinners: oh my little heart!"],[13,25,"And I'm to be a corporal in his army and wear his colours like an acrobat's hoop! What? I love, I court, I look for a wife? A woman, who is like a German clock, always being repaired, always out of order, never running right however much it's a watch, unless it's watched to keep it going right! No, worse, to break my oath, which is worst of all; and, out of three, to love the worst of them: a pale, flirtatious girl with dark velvet brows and two balls of pitch stuck in her face for eyes; yes, and, by heaven, one who would do the deed even if hundred-eyed Argus were her eunuch and her guard."],[26,31,"And I am to sigh for her, stay awake for her, pray for her! Come on, it's a punishment Cupid has given me for ignoring his almighty, dreadful little power. Well, I'll love, write, sigh, pray, plead and groan. Some men must love a lady, and some a Joan."]],"439":[[0,2,"Look, look, my beauty is saved by my good deeds! Oh, heresy about beauty, just right for these times! A hand that gives, however ugly, will get fair praise."],[3,8,"But come, the bow. Now mercy goes out to kill, and shooting well counts as a bad thing. So I'll save my reputation in the hunt like this: if I don't wound the deer, it was pity that stopped me; if I do, it was to show my skill, and I aimed to kill more for praise than for any real purpose."],[9,14,"And no doubt it is sometimes so: the love of glory becomes guilty of hateful crimes, when for fame's sake, for praise, which is only an outward thing, we bend the workings of our heart to it; as I now, only for praise, try to spill the poor deer's blood, though my heart means it no harm."]],"454":[[0,6,"[Reads] 'By heaven, it is utterly certain that you are fair; true that you are beautiful; the truth itself that you are lovely. More fairer than fair, more beautiful than beautiful, truer than truth itself, take pity on your heroic servant. The noble and most famous King Cophetua set eyes on the wicked and undoubted beggar Zenelophon, and he was the one who could rightly say, \"Veni, vidi, vici\", which to translate into the common tongue (oh, low and obscure common tongue!) is: he came, saw and conquered."],[7,13,"He came, one; saw, two; conquered, three. Who came? The King. Why did he come? To see. Why did he see? To conquer. To whom did he come? To the beggar. What did he see? The beggar. Whom did he conquer? The beggar. The conclusion is victory: on whose side? The King's. The captive is made rich: on whose side? The beggar's. The final outcome is a wedding: on whose side? The King's? No, on both in one, or one in both."],[14,21,"I am the King, for that is how the comparison goes; you are the beggar, as your lowliness shows. Shall I command your love? I may. Shall I force your love? I could. Shall I beg for your love? I will. What will you get in exchange for rags? Robes. For trifles? Titles. For yourself? Me. Expecting your reply, I kiss your foot with my lips, your picture with my eyes and every part of you with my heart. Yours in the most devoted plan of effort, Don Adriano de Armado.'"],[22,27,"'So you hear the Nemean lion roar at you, little lamb, standing there as his prey. Fall humbly at his royal feet and he will turn from hunting to play. But if you struggle, poor soul, what are you then? Food for his rage and a meal for his den.'"]],"534":[[0,8,"[Reads] 'If love makes me break my oath, how can I swear to love? Ah, no promise could ever hold unless it was made to beauty. Though I break faith with myself, I'll be faithful to you. Those vows that were like oaks to me bend like willows before you. The student leaves his course and makes your eyes his book, where all the pleasures live that learning wants to grasp. If knowledge is the target, knowing you will be enough."],[9,14,"Well-educated is the tongue that can praise you well; utterly ignorant the soul that sees you without wonder; and it is some credit to me that I admire your qualities. Your eye carries Jove's lightning, your voice his dreadful thunder, which, when not meant in anger, is music and sweet fire. Heavenly as you are, oh, forgive love this wrong, that sings heaven's praise with such an earthly tongue.'"]],"555":[[0,8,"[Reads] 'The golden sun doesn't give as sweet a kiss to the fresh morning dewdrops on the rose as your eyes' rays give when their fresh beams strike the night's dew of tears that runs down my cheeks. And the silver moon doesn't shine half as brightly through the clear depths of the sea as your face, shining through my tears."],[9,16,"You shine in every tear I weep. Every drop carries you like a coach, so you ride in triumph through my sorrow. Just look at the tears that well up in me, and they'll show your glory through my grief. But don't fall in love with yourself; then you'd keep my tears as mirrors and make me weep for ever. Oh queen of queens, no thought can think and no mortal tongue can tell how far you surpass all others.'"],[17,18,"How will she know my griefs? I'll drop the paper. Sweet leaves, hide my foolishness. Who's this coming?"]],"594":[[0,10,"[Dumaine reads his sonnet] 'One day (alas, that day!) Love, whose month is always May, saw a beautiful blossom playing in the wanton air. Through the velvet leaves, unseen, the wind found a way in, so that the lover, sick to death, wished he were the breath of heaven. \"Air,\" he said, \"you may blow on her cheeks; Air, I wish I could triumph like you!\""],[11,20,"But, alas, my hand has sworn never to pluck you from your thorn: a vow, alas, unfit for youth, which is so ready to pluck sweetness. Don't call it a sin in me that I break my oath for you, you for whom Jove would swear that Juno is ugly as an Ethiopian, and would deny he was Jove, turning mortal for your love.'"],[21,26,"I'll send this, and something else plainer that will express the pain of my true love's starving. Oh, if only the King, Berowne and Longaville were in love too! Others' bad example would wipe the mark of a perjurer off my forehead, for no one is guilty where everyone dotes alike."]],"596":[[0,7,"[Comes forward] Come, sir, you blush. Your case is just like his. You scold him, while you're twice as guilty. You don't love Maria? Longaville never wrote a sonnet for her sake, never folded his arms across his loving chest to hold down his heart? I've been hidden closely in this bush and watched you both, and blushed for you both."],[8,19,"I heard your guilty rhymes, watched how you behaved, saw sighs steam out of you, noted your passion well. 'Oh dear!' says one. 'Oh Jove!' cries the other. One says her hair is gold; the other that her eyes are crystal. [To Longaville] You'd break your faith and word for paradise; [To Dumaine] and Jove would break an oath for your love. What will Berowne say when he hears about a promise broken that was sworn so passionately? How he'll mock, how he'll spend his wit on it! How he'll triumph, jump about and laugh at it! For all the riches I've ever seen, I wouldn't want him to know as much about me."]],"597":[[0,12,"[Comes forward] Now I step out to whip hypocrisy. Ah, my good lord, forgive me, please. Good heart, what right have you to scold these worms for loving, when you're the most in love of all? Your eyes don't make coaches; no particular princess appears in your tears. You won't break your oath; it's a hateful thing. Pah, only minstrels like writing sonnets! But aren't you ashamed? No, aren't you all three ashamed to be so caught out? You found the speck in his eye, the King saw the speck in yours; but I find a plank in each of the three."],[13,20,"Oh, what a scene of foolishness I've watched, of sighs, groans, sorrow and suffering! Oh my, with what strict patience I have sat to see a king turned into a gnat, to see great Hercules spinning a top, and wise Solomon singing a jig, and old Nestor playing children's games with the boys, and the scornful Timon laughing at silly toys!"],[21,24,"Where is your grief? Oh, tell me, good Dumaine. And, gentle Longaville, where is your pain? And where is my lord's? All around the heart? A hot drink for the sick, quickly!"]],"633":[[0,6,"Then my eyes aren't eyes, and I'm not Berowne. Oh, if it weren't for my love, day would turn to night! The pick of all the best complexions meet in her fair cheek as if at a fair, where many good qualities make one perfection, where nothing is lacking that desire itself could want."],[7,14,"Lend me the decoration of every fine tongue... No, away with painted rhetoric! Oh, she doesn't need it. A seller's praise belongs to things for sale. She is beyond praise; so praise that falls short only stains her. A withered hermit, worn by a hundred winters, could shake off fifty of them by looking in her eyes. Beauty varnishes old age as if it were newborn, and gives the man on crutches the childhood of the cradle. Oh, she is the sun that makes everything shine!"]],"656":[[0,6,"Oh, there's more than enough. Here goes, then, love's soldiers. Think what you first swore to do: to fast, to study and to see no woman. That's outright treason against the royal state of youth. Tell me, can you fast? Your stomachs are too young, and going without breeds diseases."],[7,15,"Oh, we made a vow to study, lords, and in that vow we gave up our real books. For when would you, my lord, or you, or you, in dull, leaden thinking, ever have come up with such fiery verses as the inspiring eyes of beauty's tutors have given you? Other slow kinds of learning stay entirely in the brain, and so, finding barren students, hardly show a harvest for their heavy work."],[16,28,"But love, first learned in a lady's eyes, doesn't live shut up in the brain alone. It moves as fast as thought through every faculty, like the elements, and gives each faculty double power, beyond its normal work. It adds precious sight to the eye: a lover's eyes can outstare an eagle. A lover's ear can hear the faintest sound, when even a suspicious thief hears nothing. Love's touch is softer and more sensitive than the tender horns of snails. Love's tongue makes dainty Bacchus seem crude in his taste."],[29,38,"For courage, isn't Love a Hercules, still climbing trees in the garden of the Hesperides? It is as subtle as the Sphinx, as sweet and musical as bright Apollo's lute strung with his golden hair. And when Love speaks, the voices of all the gods make heaven drowsy with their harmony. No poet ever dared to touch a pen to write until his ink was mixed with Love's sighs; oh, then his lines would enchant savage ears and plant gentle humility in tyrants."],[39,45,"This is the lesson I draw from women's eyes. They always sparkle with the true fire of Prometheus; they are the books, the arts, the academies that show, contain and nourish the whole world. Without them, no one is excellent at anything. So you were fools to give up these women, and if you keep your oath you'll prove yourselves fools."],[46,54,"For wisdom's sake, a word all men love; or for love's sake, a word that loves all men; or for men's sake, who made these women; or for women's sake, who make us men: let's lose our oaths once to find ourselves, or else we lose ourselves to keep our oaths. It is a religious act to break our oaths like this; for love itself fulfils the law, and who can separate love from charity?"]],"769":[[0,7,"I was thinking of closing my eyes for half an hour in the cool shade of a sycamore, when, look, to interrupt my rest, I saw the King and his companions heading towards that shade. Carefully I crept into a thicket nearby and overheard what you're about to hear: that very soon they'll come here in disguise."],[8,19,"Their messenger is a pretty, rascally page who has learned his message by heart. They were teaching him gestures and delivery there: 'This is how you must speak', and 'this is how you hold yourself'. And every so often they worried that a majestic presence would make him forget his lines. 'For,' said the King, 'you'll see an angel; but don't be afraid, speak out boldly.' The boy answered, 'An angel isn't evil; I'd have been afraid of her if she'd been a devil.' At that they all laughed and clapped him on the shoulder, making the cheeky boy bolder with their praise."],[20,29,"One rubbed his elbow like this, grinned and swore a better speech had never been spoken. Another snapped his finger and thumb and cried, 'Come on, we'll do it, come what may.' The third danced about and cried, 'All's going well!' The fourth spun on his toe and fell over. At that they all tumbled on the ground with such hearty, deep laughter that in this fit of silliness solemn tears of emotion appeared, to check their folly."]],"905":[[0,9,"This fellow pecks up wit the way pigeons peck up peas, and spits it out again whenever God pleases. He's a pedlar of wit, and sells his goods retail at festivals and parties, meetings, markets and fairs; and we who sell wholesale, the Lord knows, haven't the grace to show it off like that. This gallant has the girls pinned to his sleeve. If he'd been Adam, he'd have tempted Eve. He can carve at table, too, and lisp. Why, this is the man who kissed his hand away being polite."],[10,19,"This is the ape of good manners, Monsieur Fastidious, who, when he plays backgammon, scolds the dice in respectful language. Why, he can sing a middle part most moderately; and as a gentleman usher, let anyone beat him if they can. The ladies call him sweet. The stairs kiss his feet as he walks on them. This is the flower that smiles at everyone, to show his teeth as white as whalebone; and people who don't want to die in debt pay him his due by calling him 'honey-tongued Boyet'."]],"936":[[0,7,"This is how the stars rain down plagues for breaking oaths. Can any brazen face hold out any longer? Here I stand, lady; throw your skill at me. Bruise me with scorn, crush me with a jeer, thrust your sharp wit right through my ignorance, cut me to pieces with your keen ideas, and I'll never ask you to dance again, or wait on you in Russian dress again."],[8,15,"Oh, I'll never again trust prepared speeches, or a schoolboy's tongue reciting them, or come in a mask to my love, or woo in rhyme like a blind harper's song. Silky phrases, precise, elegant terms, velvet-thick exaggerations, spruce affectation, pedantic figures of speech: these summer flies have filled me with the maggots of showing off."],[16,21,"I renounce them, and I swear here, by this white glove (how white the hand is, God knows!), that from now on my wooing will be expressed in plain homespun yeses and honest woollen noes. And to begin: girl, so help me God, la, my love for you is sound, sans crack or flaw."]],"963":[[0,12,"Neither one nor the other; I give up both. I see the trick of it. There was a plot here: knowing about our fun beforehand, they set out to spoil it like a Christmas comedy. Some tell-tale, some flatterer, some lowly clown, some gossip, some hanger-on at the dinner table, some fellow who smiles his face into wrinkles and knows the trick of making my lady laugh when she's in the mood, told them our plans in advance. Once that was out, the ladies swapped favours, and we, following the signs, wooed only the sign of our lady. Now, to add more terror to our perjury, we've broken our oaths again, in intention and by mistake."],[13,22,"That's about it. [To Boyet] And wasn't it you who spoiled our fun ahead of time, making us false like this? Don't you know my lady's foot by the ruler, and laugh in the apple of her eye, and stand between her back and the fire holding a plate, joking merrily? You put our page off his lines. Go on, you're the licensed fool; die when you like, a woman's slip will be your shroud. You leer at me, do you? There's an eye that wounds like a lead sword."]],"1130":[[0,5,"Honest plain words reach the ear of grief best, and these will help you understand the King. For your lovely sakes we've wasted our time and played false with our oaths. Your beauty, ladies, has changed us greatly, shaping our moods to the very opposite of what we intended."],[6,17,"And whatever in us has seemed ridiculous (for love is full of unsuitable impulses, as wanton as a child, skipping and foolish; it is born in the eye, and so, like the eye, it is full of strange shapes, habits and forms, changing its subject as the eye rolls to every different object it glances at): if this motley costume of free love that we put on has, in your heavenly eyes, seemed unfit for our oaths and our dignity, it was those heavenly eyes, which see these faults, that tempted us to commit them."],[18,23,"So, ladies, since our love is yours, the mistakes love makes are yours too. We are false to ourselves by being false once, in order to be true for ever to those who make us both false and true: fair ladies, you. And even that falseness, which is a sin in itself, purifies itself in this way and turns to grace."]],"1136":[[0,10,"A time far too short, I think, to make an eternal bargain in. No, no, my lord, Your Grace has broken many oaths and is full of grievous guilt; so here is what I say. If you will do anything for my love (though there's no reason why you should), this is what you must do for me. I won't trust your oath; instead, go straight away to some lonely, bare hermitage, far from all the pleasures of the world, and stay there until the twelve signs of the zodiac have brought round a full year."],[11,24,"If this harsh, solitary life doesn't change the offer you made in the heat of passion; if frosts and fasting, a hard bed and thin clothes don't nip the showy blossoms of your love; if it survives this test and stays love: then, at the end of the year, come and claim me, claim me by what you have earned, and, by this virgin hand now kissing yours, I will be yours. Until then I'll shut my sorrowful self up in a house of mourning, raining tears of grief in memory of my father's death. If you refuse this, let our hands part, and neither have any claim on the other's heart."]],"1152":[[0,5,"I'd often heard of you, my Lord Berowne, before I saw you; and the world's broad tongue proclaims you a man full of mockery, full of comparisons and wounding jeers, which you fire at people of every rank who come within range of your wit."],[6,13,"To weed this bitter wormwood out of your fertile brain, and at the same time to win me, if you want to (and without that I can't be won), you shall spend this year, day after day, visiting the speechless sick and talking constantly with groaning wretches; and your task will be, with all the fierce effort of your wit, to force the pained and helpless to smile."]],"1171":[[0,8,"(Song) When speckled daisies and blue violets, silver-white lady's-smocks and yellow buttercups paint the meadows with delight, then the cuckoo in every tree mocks married men, because this is what it sings: 'Cuckoo! Cuckoo, cuckoo!' Oh, word of fear (cuckold!), unpleasant to a married man's ear."],[9,17,"When shepherds play pipes of oat straw, and merry larks wake the ploughmen like clocks, when turtle-doves, rooks and jackdaws mate, and girls bleach their summer smocks in the sun, then the cuckoo in every tree mocks married men, because this is what it sings: 'Cuckoo! Cuckoo, cuckoo!' Oh, word of fear, unpleasant to a married man's ear."]],"1172":[[0,7,"(Song) When icicles hang by the wall, and Dick the shepherd blows on his fingernails, and Tom carries logs into the hall, and the milk comes home frozen in the pail, when blood is chilled and roads are muddy, then every night the staring owl sings 'Tu-whit, tu-whoo!', a merry note, while greasy Joan stirs the pot to stop it boiling over."],[8,15,"When the wind blows loudly, and coughing drowns out the parson's sermon, and birds sit brooding in the snow, and Marian's nose looks red and raw, when roasted crab-apples hiss in the bowl of punch, then every night the staring owl sings 'Tu-whit, tu-whoo!', a merry note, while greasy Joan stirs the pot."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"oath":["Oaths and vows","The academy oath, broken promises, perjury and the new vows of the ending: the play's spine."],"study":["Learning and study","The King's academe, Berowne's attack on book-learning, and the pedants' parody of scholarship."],"love":["Love and courtship","Sonnets, favours, masques and sighs: how men and women court, and what real love has to prove."],"wit":["Wit and wordplay","Puns, rhymes, Latin tags and verbal duels: a play in love with language and wary of it."],"women":["Men and women","The ladies outwit the lords at every turn and set the terms at the end."],"eyes":["Eyes and beauty","Eyes as books and as weapons; Rosaline's darkness and the debate over what is beautiful."],"show":["Shows, masks and performance","Masques, disguises, pageants and the audience's manners: theatre inside the play."],"time":["Time, death and the seasons","Fame against devouring time, the news of a death, a year's penance and the songs of spring and winter."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"KING","text":"Let fame, that all hunt after in their lives, Live registered upon our brazen tombs,","themes":["time","study"],"note":"The play opens with a wish to beat death through fame."},{"id":"2","who":"KING","text":"Navarre shall be the wonder of the world; Our court shall be a little academe,","themes":["study","oath"],"note":"The grand plan in two lines."},{"id":"3","who":"LONGAVILLE","text":"The mind shall banquet, though the body pine.","themes":["study","oath"],"note":"Longaville signs cheerfully, with a neat antithesis."},{"id":"7","who":"BEROWNE","text":"O, these are barren tasks, too hard to keep, Not to see ladies, study, fast, not sleep.","themes":["oath","study"],"note":"Berowne lists the rules in a mocking couplet."},{"id":"11","who":"BEROWNE","text":"By yea and nay, sir, then I swore in jest.","themes":["oath","wit"],"note":"Berowne tries to wriggle out of what he swore."},{"id":"17","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Light seeking light doth light of light beguile;","themes":["study","eyes","wit"],"note":"A line built from one word used in four senses."},{"id":"17","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Small have continual plodders ever won, Save base authority from others' books.","themes":["study"],"note":"Berowne mocks second-hand learning."},{"id":"18","who":"KING","text":"How well he's read, to reason against reading.","themes":["study","wit"],"note":"The King notes the paradox."},{"id":"27","who":"BEROWNE","text":"At Christmas I no more desire a rose Than wish a snow in May's new-fangled shows, But like of each thing that in season grows.","themes":["time"],"note":"Berowne argues for things in their proper season."},{"id":"39","who":"BEROWNE","text":"'Tis won as towns with fire: so won, so lost.","themes":["study","oath"],"note":"A sharp image for study that defeats itself."},{"id":"41","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Necessity will make us all forsworn Three thousand times within this three years' space;","themes":["oath"],"note":"Berowne foresees endless broken oaths."},{"id":"41","who":"BEROWNE","text":"I am the last that will last keep his oath.","themes":["oath"],"note":"Berowne boasts he will hold out longest."},{"id":"44","who":"KING","text":"But I protest I love to hear him lie,","themes":["show","wit"],"note":"The King introduces Armado as entertainment."},{"id":"45","who":"BEROWNE","text":"A man of fire-new words, fashion's own knight.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Berowne sums up Armado."},{"id":"107","who":"COSTARD","text":"Affliction may one day smile again, and till then, sit thee down, sorrow.","themes":["wit","love"],"note":"Costard accepts his punishment with comic grace."},{"id":"149","who":"ARMADO","text":"I will hereupon confess I am in love;","themes":["love","oath"],"note":"Armado's confession comes before any lord's."},{"id":"168","who":"MOTH","text":"If she be made of white and red, Her faults will ne'er be known;","themes":["eyes","women"],"note":"Moth's rhyme on a woman's complexion."},{"id":"205","who":"ARMADO","text":"Love is a familiar; Love is a devil. There is no evil angel but Love.","themes":["love"],"note":"Armado blames love as a devil."},{"id":"205","who":"ARMADO","text":"Devise, wit; write, pen; for I am for whole volumes in folio.","themes":["love","wit"],"note":"Armado prepares to pour out love poetry."},{"id":"210","who":"PRINCESS","text":"Beauty is bought by judgement of the eye, Not uttered by base sale of chapmen's tongues.","themes":["eyes","women"],"note":"The Princess rejects Boyet's flattery."},{"id":"222","who":"ROSALINE","text":"His eye begets occasion for his wit,","themes":["wit","eyes"],"note":"Rosaline describes Berowne before we see them meet."},{"id":"223","who":"PRINCESS","text":"God bless my ladies! Are they all in love,","themes":["love","women"],"note":"The Princess teases her ladies' praises."},{"id":"239","who":"PRINCESS","text":"'Tis deadly sin to keep that oath, my lord, And sin to break it.","themes":["oath","women"],"note":"The Princess puts the King's dilemma in a sentence."},{"id":"242","who":"PRINCESS","text":"For you'll prove perjured if you make me stay.","themes":["oath"],"note":"A teasing threat."},{"id":"250","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Your wit's too hot, it speeds too fast, 'twill tire.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Berowne in his first duel with Rosaline."},{"id":"320","who":"PRINCESS","text":"This civil war of wits were much better used On Navarre and his bookmen,","themes":["wit","women"],"note":"The Princess turns her ladies' wit outward."},{"id":"325","who":"BOYET","text":"Methought all his senses were locked in his eye,","themes":["eyes","love"],"note":"Boyet describes the King gazing at the Princess."},{"id":"344","who":"MOTH","text":"These are compliments, these are humours;","themes":["show","love"],"note":"Moth's mock lesson in how to look like a lover."},{"id":"369","who":"ARMADO","text":"Sweet smoke of rhetoric!","themes":["wit"],"note":"Armado admires Moth's quick reply."},{"id":"402","who":"COSTARD","text":"\"Remuneration\"! O, that's the Latin word for three farthings.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Costard learns a word by its price."},{"id":"423","who":"BEROWNE","text":"And I, forsooth, in love! I, that have been love's whip,","themes":["love","oath"],"note":"Berowne's soliloquy opens in disbelief."},{"id":"423","who":"BEROWNE","text":"This wimpled, whining, purblind, wayward boy,","themes":["love"],"note":"A string of insults for Cupid."},{"id":"423","who":"BEROWNE","text":"A woman, that is like a German clock, Still a-repairing, ever out of frame,","themes":["women"],"note":"Berowne's comic distrust of women."},{"id":"423","who":"BEROWNE","text":"With two pitch-balls stuck in her face for eyes;","themes":["eyes"],"note":"Rosaline is dark-eyed."},{"id":"423","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Some men must love my lady, and some Joan.","themes":["love"],"note":"Berowne ends his soliloquy with a shrug."},{"id":"439","who":"PRINCESS","text":"Glory grows guilty of detested crimes,","themes":["show","study"],"note":"The Princess admits she hunts for praise."},{"id":"441","who":"PRINCESS","text":"praise we may afford To any lady that subdues a lord.","themes":["women"],"note":"The Princess on wives who rule their husbands."},{"id":"454","who":"BOYET","text":"I am the King, for so stands the comparison; thou the beggar,","themes":["love","wit"],"note":"From Armado's letter, read by Boyet."},{"id":"506","who":"HOLOFERNES","text":"O, thou monster Ignorance, how deformed dost thou look!","themes":["study"],"note":"Holofernes despises Dull."},{"id":"507","who":"NATHANIEL","text":"Sir, he hath never fed of the dainties that are bred of a book.","themes":["study"],"note":"Nathaniel on Dull's lack of learning."},{"id":"518","who":"HOLOFERNES","text":"The preyful Princess pierced and pricked a pretty pleasing pricket;","themes":["wit"],"note":"Holofernes's alliterative epitaph."},{"id":"534","who":"NATHANIEL","text":"If love make me forsworn, how shall I swear to love?","themes":["oath","love"],"note":"The first line of Berowne's sonnet."},{"id":"534","who":"NATHANIEL","text":"Study his bias leaves, and makes his book thine eyes,","themes":["study","eyes"],"note":"From Berowne's sonnet."},{"id":"535","who":"HOLOFERNES","text":"Imitari is nothing:","themes":["study"],"note":"Holofernes on imitation."},{"id":"550","who":"BEROWNE","text":"By the Lord, this love is as mad as Ajax.","themes":["love"],"note":"Berowne alone, in prose."},{"id":"550","who":"BEROWNE","text":"By heaven, I do love, and it hath taught me to rhyme, and to be melancholy.","themes":["love","wit"],"note":"Berowne's confession to the audience."},{"id":"562","who":"KING","text":"In love, I hope. Sweet fellowship in shame.","themes":["love","oath"],"note":"The King, hidden, hopes Longaville is in love too."},{"id":"563","who":"BEROWNE","text":"One drunkard loves another of the name.","themes":["love"],"note":"Berowne's comment from his hiding place."},{"id":"570","who":"LONGAVILLE","text":"Vows are but breath, and breath a vapour is.","themes":["oath"],"note":"From Longaville's sonnet."},{"id":"570","who":"LONGAVILLE","text":"what fool is not so wise To lose an oath to win a paradise?","themes":["oath","love"],"note":"Longaville's sonnet ends with a neat paradox."},{"id":"571","who":"BEROWNE","text":"This is the liver vein, which makes flesh a deity, A green goose a goddess.","themes":["love"],"note":"Berowne mocks Longaville's poem."},{"id":"575","who":"BEROWNE","text":"All hid, all hid, an old infant play.","themes":["show"],"note":"Berowne names the hide-and-seek structure of the scene."},{"id":"594","who":"DUMAINE","text":"For none offend where all alike do dote.","themes":["oath"],"note":"Dumaine wishes the others were in love too."},{"id":"597","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Now step I forth to whip hypocrisy.","themes":["oath","show"],"note":"Berowne comes out of hiding to lecture."},{"id":"597","who":"BEROWNE","text":"To see a king transformed to a gnat!","themes":["love"],"note":"Berowne mocks the lovesick King."},{"id":"599","who":"BEROWNE","text":"I that am honest, I that hold it sin To break the vow I am engaged in.","themes":["oath","show"],"note":"Berowne poses as the one true man."},{"id":"629","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Young blood doth not obey an old decree.","themes":["oath","time"],"note":"Berowne admits defeat."},{"id":"631","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Who sees the heavenly Rosaline","themes":["eyes","love"],"note":"Berowne, exposed, praises Rosaline lavishly."},{"id":"636","who":"KING","text":"Black is the badge of hell,","themes":["eyes"],"note":"The King teases Berowne about Rosaline's darkness."},{"id":"637","who":"BEROWNE","text":"And therefore is she born to make black fair.","themes":["eyes","women"],"note":"Berowne defends dark beauty."},{"id":"651","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Nothing so sure, and thereby all forsworn.","themes":["oath","love"],"note":"Berowne admits they are all perjured."},{"id":"656","who":"BEROWNE","text":"But love, first learned in a lady's eyes, Lives not alone immured in the brain,","themes":["love","eyes","study"],"note":"From Berowne's great defence of love."},{"id":"656","who":"BEROWNE","text":"From women's eyes this doctrine I derive.","themes":["eyes","women","study"],"note":"Berowne's thesis."},{"id":"656","who":"BEROWNE","text":"They are the books, the arts, the academes,","themes":["study","women"],"note":"Berowne turns the King's 'little academe' into women's eyes."},{"id":"656","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Let us once lose our oaths to find ourselves, Or else we lose ourselves to keep our oaths.","themes":["oath"],"note":"A balanced, persuasive reversal."},{"id":"656","who":"BEROWNE","text":"It is religion to be thus forsworn,","themes":["oath"],"note":"Berowne makes perjury holy."},{"id":"657","who":"KING","text":"Saint Cupid, then, and, soldiers, to the field!","themes":["love"],"note":"The King turns study into a love campaign."},{"id":"663","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Light wenches may prove plagues to men forsworn;","themes":["women","oath"],"note":"Berowne ends the scene with a warning."},{"id":"672","who":"HOLOFERNES","text":"He draweth out the thread of his verbosity finer than the staple of his argument.","themes":["wit","study"],"note":"Holofernes on Armado."},{"id":"682","who":"MOTH","text":"They have been at a great feast of languages and stolen the scraps.","themes":["wit","study"],"note":"Moth on the pedants."},{"id":"683","who":"COSTARD","text":"for thou art not so long by the head as honorificabilitudinitatibus.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Costard jokes about Moth's size."},{"id":"721","who":"DULL","text":"Nor understood none neither, sir.","themes":["study"],"note":"Dull's honest admission."},{"id":"759","who":"ROSALINE","text":"That he should be my fool, and I his fate.","themes":["women","love"],"note":"Rosaline plans to torment Berowne."},{"id":"760","who":"PRINCESS","text":"None are so surely caught, when they are catched, As wit turned fool.","themes":["wit"],"note":"The Princess on clever men in love."},{"id":"775","who":"PRINCESS","text":"And mock for mock is only my intent.","themes":["show","women"],"note":"The Princess's plan for the Muscovites."},{"id":"779","who":"PRINCESS","text":"There's no such sport as sport by sport o'erthrown,","themes":["show"],"note":"The Princess delights in turning their joke."},{"id":"822","who":"ROSALINE","text":"You took the moon at full, but now she's changed.","themes":["show","women"],"note":"Rosaline, playing the Princess, refuses to dance."},{"id":"869","who":"BOYET","text":"The tongues of mocking wenches are as keen As is the razor's edge invisible,","themes":["wit","women"],"note":"Boyet admires the ladies' mockery."},{"id":"914","who":"PRINCESS","text":"Nor God nor I delights in perjured men.","themes":["oath"],"note":"The Princess refuses to enter the court."},{"id":"936","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Taffeta phrases, silken terms precise, Three-piled hyperboles, spruce affectation,","themes":["wit"],"note":"Berowne renounces fancy language, in very fancy language."},{"id":"936","who":"BEROWNE","text":"In russet yeas and honest kersey noes.","themes":["wit","love"],"note":"Plain cloth words for plain wooing."},{"id":"937","who":"ROSALINE","text":"Sans \"sans,\" I pray you.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Rosaline catches him out."},{"id":"963","who":"BEROWNE","text":"To dash it like a Christmas comedy.","themes":["show"],"note":"Berowne realises the ladies knew of the masque."},{"id":"963","who":"BEROWNE","text":"We are again forsworn in will and error.","themes":["oath"],"note":"The men swore love to the wrong ladies."},{"id":"987","who":"PRINCESS","text":"That sport best pleases that doth least know how,","themes":["show"],"note":"The Princess insists on seeing the Worthies."},{"id":"1059","who":"HOLOFERNES","text":"This is not generous, not gentle, not humble.","themes":["show","study"],"note":"Holofernes rebukes the hecklers."},{"id":"1085","who":"ARMADO","text":"The sweet war-man is dead and rotten.","themes":["time","show"],"note":"Armado defends his Hector."},{"id":"1121","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Worthies away! The scene begins to cloud.","themes":["time","show"],"note":"Berowne after Marcadé's news."},{"id":"1130","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Honest plain words best pierce the ear of grief;","themes":["time","wit"],"note":"Berowne tries plain speech."},{"id":"1136","who":"PRINCESS","text":"A time, methinks, too short To make a world-without-end bargain in.","themes":["time","love"],"note":"The Princess refuses a hasty promise."},{"id":"1136","who":"PRINCESS","text":"Your oath I will not trust,","themes":["oath"],"note":"The Princess's verdict on the King."},{"id":"1152","who":"ROSALINE","text":"Visit the speechless sick, and still converse With groaning wretches;","themes":["time","wit"],"note":"Rosaline's penance for Berowne."},{"id":"1153","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Mirth cannot move a soul in agony.","themes":["wit","time"],"note":"Berowne protests."},{"id":"1154","who":"ROSALINE","text":"A jest's prosperity lies in the ear Of him that hears it, never in the tongue Of him that makes it.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Rosaline's definition of a good joke."},{"id":"1158","who":"BEROWNE","text":"Our wooing doth not end like an old play. Jack hath not Jill.","themes":["love","show"],"note":"Berowne on the unusual ending."},{"id":"1160","who":"BEROWNE","text":"That's too long for a play.","themes":["time","show"],"note":"A year is too long for a play."},{"id":"1171","who":"SPRING","text":"Mocks married men; for thus sings he:","themes":["love","time"],"note":"From the spring song."},{"id":"1172","who":"WINTER","text":"When icicles hang by the wall,","themes":["time"],"note":"The winter song begins."},{"id":"1173","who":"ARMADO","text":"The words of Mercury are harsh after the songs of Apollo.","themes":["wit","time"],"note":"The last line of the play."}]};
const EX={"intro":["The young King of Navarre and three of his lords, Berowne, Longaville and Dumaine, swear to spend three years in study: fasting, sleeping little and seeing no woman. At once the Princess of France arrives on a diplomatic mission with three ladies, Rosaline, Maria and Katharine, and the King has to lodge her in his park rather than let her into his court.","Each lord falls in love with a lady, and each secretly writes her poems. One by one they are caught out, and Berowne, the cleverest, argues that breaking the oath is the wisest thing they could do. Meanwhile a comic household (the Spanish braggart Armado, his page Moth, the clown Costard, the dairymaid Jaquenetta, the schoolmaster Holofernes and the curate Nathaniel) mirrors the lovers with letters, Latin and a pageant of the Nine Worthies.","The ladies win every battle of wits, seeing through the men's Russian disguises and mocking the pageant's performers less than the lords do. Then a messenger brings news that the Princess's father has died. The comedy stops. The ladies will not trust oaths sworn so quickly, and each man must wait and prove himself for a year. The play ends not with weddings but with two songs, of spring and winter."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The King and his lords swear to study for three years","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Berowne mocks the oath's 'barren tasks' but signs","id":7},{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Princess's visit means the King must break a rule at once","id":37},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Armado's letter: Costard caught with Jaquenetta","id":76},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Costard is sentenced to bran and water","id":102},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Armado confesses he loves Jaquenetta","id":149},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Armado resolves to write sonnets","id":205},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The Princess arrives and sends Boyet to the King","id":210},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The King lodges her in the park, not the court","id":234},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Berowne and Rosaline's first duel of wit","id":244},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Boyet: the King is 'infected' with love","id":325},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Armado frees Costard to carry his letter","id":398},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Berowne gives Costard a letter for Rosaline","id":418},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Berowne admits he is in love","id":423},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The wrong letter: Armado's love letter is read to the ladies","id":454},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Berowne's sonnet reaches Jaquenetta and is sent to the King","id":537},{"s":"a4s3","t":"The King, Longaville and Dumaine are overheard one by one","id":555},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Berowne steps out to 'whip hypocrisy'","id":597},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Berowne's torn letter exposes him","id":621},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Berowne's defence of love: women's eyes are the true books","id":656},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Holofernes plans a pageant of the Nine Worthies","id":708},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Boyet warns of the lords' Russian disguise","id":769},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The ladies swap favours; the lords woo the wrong women","id":815},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The lords confess; Berowne gives up fancy words","id":936},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The Nine Worthies are heckled off the stage","id":1056},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Costard reveals Jaquenetta is pregnant","id":1093},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Marcadé: the King of France is dead","id":1118},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The ladies set each lover a year's trial","id":1136},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The songs of Spring and Winter end the play","id":1171}],"irony":{"41":"Berowne says he will be the last to keep his oath. He is the first we see in love, and his oath is broken by Act 3.","104":"The King sentences Costard for being with a woman; within a day he and all his lords will be guilty of breaking the same law in spirit.","149":"Armado confesses love and calls it base for a soldier; the lords will soon say the same about scholars.","236":"The King swears 'not for the world' will he break his oath, while falling in love with the woman he is refusing.","418":"Berowne trusts his letter to Costard, who already carries another letter. The audience can guess what will happen.","451":"The Princess calls Berowne 'a good friend of mine' and opens his letter; it is the wrong one.","537":"Holofernes sends Berowne's love poem to the King, the very person Berowne most needs to hide it from.","575":"Berowne gloats from his hiding place like a demigod, unaware that his own letter is on its way.","597":"Berowne lectures the others on hypocrisy while his own sonnet is in the King's hands.","599":"'I that am honest': seconds later Jaquenetta arrives with his letter.","815":"The King speaks words of love to Rosaline, thinking she is the Princess, wearing the jewel he sent.","841":"Berowne courts the masked Princess, believing she is Rosaline.","938":"Berowne says the lords have 'the plague' of love caught from the ladies' eyes; he thinks the ladies are unaffected, but they have tested the men deliberately.","1085":"Armado begs the lords not to mock a dead hero; within minutes, real news of a death arrives.","1117":"The Princess welcomes Marcadé, 'but that thou interruptest our merriment', before she knows his news."},"devices":{"war":"Love as war","hunt":"Hunting and the deer","eyes":"Eyes and light","books":"Books and learning","trade":"Money and trading","seasons":"Seasons and weather"},"dev":{"2":[["war","The King calls his lords 'brave conquerors' fighting their own desires: study is a war against nature.","brave conquerors"]],"17":[["eyes","Light, eyes and seeing are twisted together: study blinds the eyes it is meant to enlighten.","Light seeking light"],["books","Astronomers are 'godfathers' who name the stars but know no more than anyone else.","earthly godfathers of heaven's lights"]],"27":[["seasons","Berowne wants everything in its season: no roses at Christmas, no snow in May.","At Christmas I no more desire a rose"]],"39":[["war","Study is like a town captured by setting it on fire: the victory destroys the prize.","won as towns with fire"]],"153":[["war","Armado compares his rapier to Samson's strength, mixing love and fighting.","I do excel thee in my rapier"]],"205":[["war","Cupid's arrow defeats Hercules' club and the Spaniard's rapier.","Cupid's butt-shaft"]],"210":[["trade","Beauty is judged by the eye, not sold by pedlars' talk.","base sale of chapmen's tongues"]],"325":[["eyes","The King's senses crowd into his eyes like jewels in a shop window.","jewels in crystal"]],"402":[["trade","Costard turns a fine word into a coin.","remuneration"]],"423":[["war","Berowne becomes 'a corporal of his field' in Cupid's army.","corporal of his field"]],"429":[["hunt","The Princess must 'play the murderer' in the hunt.","play the murderer"]],"439":[["hunt","Shooting the deer for praise becomes an image of glory's guilty crimes.","spill The poor deer's blood"]],"454":[["war","Armado's letter makes himself a lion and Jaquenetta a lamb.","Nemean lion"]],"471":[["hunt","Hunting jokes turn on horns: deer's antlers and a cuckold's.","kill horns"]],"518":[["hunt","Holofernes's epitaph names every kind of deer by age.","pricket"]],"550":[["hunt","Berowne is hunting himself while the King hunts the deer.","I am coursing myself"]],"555":[["eyes","The King's sonnet makes the Princess shine in every tear he weeps.","Thou shin'st in every tear"]],"637":[["eyes","Berowne turns black into the new fair.","to make black fair"]],"656":[["books","Women's eyes become the books and academies the lords swore by.","They are the books"],["war","The speech ends as a call to arms: 'affection's men-at-arms'.","affection's men-at-arms"]],"657":[["war","The King launches a campaign of courtship under Saint Cupid.","soldiers, to the field"]],"767":[["war","Boyet calls the lords' approach an armed attack.","Arm, wenches, arm!"]],"816":[["trade","Rosaline mocks the King's request as worth only 'moonshine in the water'.","moonshine in the water"]],"831":[["trade","Courtship is priced like goods: what buys their company?","Price you yourselves?"]],"905":[["trade","Boyet is wit's pedlar, selling it retail at fairs.","wit's pedlar"]],"936":[["trade","Plain words are described as plain cloth: russet and kersey, not taffeta.","russet yeas and honest kersey noes"]],"1136":[["seasons","The Princess asks for a year to pass: the twelve signs of the zodiac.","the twelve celestial signs"]],"1171":[["seasons","Spring's song mixes flowers with the cuckoo's mockery of husbands.","When daisies pied"]],"1172":[["seasons","Winter's song is homely and cold: icicles, logs and a staring owl.","When icicles hang by the wall"]]},"stage":{"park":"The King of Navarre's park","pavilion":"The Princess's pavilion in the park"},"stagerules":[["pavilion","pavilion"],["park|same","park"]],"place":{"a1s1":"The King's park","a1s2":"The park","a2s1":"The Princess's camp","a3s1":"The park","a4s1":"The hunting ground","a4s2":"The park","a4s3":"The park","a5s1":"The park","a5s2":"Before the Princess's pavilion"},"arc":{"The lords' oath":[9,8,6,5,5,4,0,0,1],"The lords' love":[0,1,6,7,7,7,10,9,9]},"arcnote":"The oath is strong only for a moment: Berowne mocks it as he signs, the Princess's arrival forces the first exception, and Armado breaks it in spirit before the lords do. Love rises from the ladies' arrival, through Berowne's soliloquy, to the scene where all four lovers are exposed; at that point the oath is gone. In the last scene the lords' love meets its first real test. The ladies set new vows, a year of penance, so the line for the oath rises a little at the very end.","context":[["Date and printing","Probably written about 1594 to 1595. A quarto was printed in 1598, its title page claiming the text was 'newly corrected and augmented' and that it had been played before the Queen the Christmas before. It was included among the comedies in the First Folio of 1623."],["Love's Labour's Won","In 1598 the writer Francis Meres listed a Shakespeare comedy called Love's Labour's Won. No copy survives. It may be a lost sequel, or another title for a known play."],["Navarre and France","The setting borrows real names. Henri of Navarre was a famous figure in the French wars of religion in the 1590s, and the lords' names echo his followers and rivals, Biron, Longueville and Mayenne (Dumaine). The dispute over Aquitaine and borrowed war money gives the visit a political excuse."],["Academies","Renaissance princes and humanists founded academies for discussion and study. A French book about young noblemen studying together in an academy was translated into English in 1586. The King's 'little academe' plays on that fashion."],["The sonnet craze","Sonnet sequences were the height of fashion in the early 1590s, and Shakespeare was writing his own. Almost every lover in the play writes a sonnet or poem, and several are read aloud by the wrong people."],["Rhyme and fine language","The play has more rhyme than almost any other Shakespeare play, and parodies the elaborate court style of the period (associated with John Lyly's 'Euphues'). Armado, Holofernes and the lords all show off with words in different ways."],["Stock comic types","Armado is a version of the braggart soldier and Holofernes of the pedant, types familiar from Latin comedy and Italian commedia dell'arte. A boastful Spaniard was easy to laugh at in England after the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588."],["The Nine Worthies","A traditional list of heroes: three pagans (Hector, Alexander, Julius Caesar), three Jews (Joshua, David, Judas Maccabaeus) and three Christians (Arthur, Charlemagne, Godfrey of Bouillon). Pageants of the Worthies were popular village entertainments. The show in the play mixes in Pompey and Hercules, who were not on the usual list."],["Masques and Russians","Courtly entertainments often had masked visitors in exotic costume. A Russian embassy to Elizabeth's court in the 1580s was remembered, and the Gray's Inn Christmas revels of 1594 to 1595 included a mock Russian ambassador."],["Mourning","A royal death meant a long formal mourning. The Princess's year shut in 'a mournful house' and Maria's black gown reflect the custom, and give a reason for a year's delay."],["Source","No main source is known: the plot seems to be Shakespeare's own invention, which is rare for him."],["On stage","After the 1590s the play was rarely performed until the twentieth century. Peter Brook's 1946 Stratford production brought it back to the stage, and Kenneth Branagh made it into a film musical set in the 1930s (2000)."]],"critics":{"wit":[["Samuel Johnson (edition, 1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson granted that the play has many low and childish passages, but thought that no play of Shakespeare's shows more signs of his genius."],["Walter Pater, 'Love's Labours Lost' (1885)","Paraphrased: Pater saw the play as a comedy about a fashion for fine language, a pattern of verbal figures, and found in Berowne the poet's own pleasure in words mixed with doubt about them."]],"study":[["William Hazlitt, Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817)","Paraphrased: Hazlitt said that if he had to give up one of the comedies it would be this one, though he still enjoyed its comic pedants and braggart."]],"time":[["Harley Granville-Barker, Prefaces to Shakespeare (1927)","Paraphrased: he admired the entrance of Marcadé as a stroke of theatre that changes the whole play in a few lines, turning artifice into reality."],["Anne Barton (as Bobbyann Roesen), Shakespeare Quarterly (1953)","Paraphrased: she argued that the park is a kind of enclosed, ideal world, and that the news of death is reality breaking in, which the lovers must learn to face."]],"show":[["C. L. Barber, Shakespeare's Festive Comedy (1959)","Paraphrased: Barber read the play as a holiday of wit and revelry, which knows that holiday must end; its ending shows the limits of festive release."]],"women":[["Louis Montrose, 'Curious-Knotted Garden' (1977)","Paraphrased: Montrose argued that the play is about the rituals and games of courtship, and that the ladies control the ending by refusing to let the men's games settle into marriage."]]},"insults":[{"id":186,"f":"With that face?","who":"Jaquenetta","n":"The dairymaid deflates Armado's grand promise to tell her 'wonders'."},{"id":280,"f":"And yours from long living.","who":"Rosaline","n":"Rosaline turns Berowne's 'God save thy life' into a curse."},{"id":295,"f":"God's blessing on your beard!","who":"Longaville","n":"A sarcastic blessing: Boyet has been cheeky."},{"id":360,"f":"a horse to be ambassador for an ass","who":"Moth","n":"Moth calls himself a horse and Costard an ass, under his master's nose."},{"id":423,"f":"A whitely wanton with a velvet brow","who":"Berowne","n":"Berowne insults the woman he loves while admitting he loves her."},{"id":506,"f":"O, thou monster Ignorance","who":"Holofernes","n":"The schoolmaster's contempt for plain Dull."},{"id":577,"f":"O most profane coxcomb!","who":"Berowne","n":"From his hiding place, Berowne mocks Dumaine's 'most divine Kate'."},{"id":621,"f":"you whoreson loggerhead","who":"Berowne","n":"Berowne turns on Costard, who has exposed him."},{"id":688,"f":"most silly sheep with a horn","who":"Moth","n":"The page outwits the schoolmaster and calls him a cuckold."},{"id":699,"f":"thou halfpenny purse of wit, thou pigeon-egg of discretion","who":"Costard","n":"Affectionate insults for little Moth."},{"id":750,"f":"O, that your face were not so full of O's!","who":"Rosaline","n":"A cruel joke about Katharine's smallpox scars."},{"id":865,"f":"Then die a calf before your horns do grow.","who":"Katharine","n":"Katharine calls the masked Longaville a calf and a future cuckold."},{"id":905,"f":"This fellow pecks up wit as pigeons peas","who":"Berowne","n":"Berowne's jealous portrait of Boyet as a ladies' man."},{"id":963,"f":"some trencher-knight, some Dick","who":"Berowne","n":"Berowne blames a tell-tale hanger-on, meaning Boyet."},{"id":1041,"f":"Because thou hast no face.","who":"Berowne","n":"The lords mock Holofernes's face as he tries to act."},{"id":1058,"f":"Jud-as, away!","who":"Berowne","n":"The pun on 'Judas' calls Holofernes an ass."},{"id":1094,"f":"Dost thou infamonize me among potentates?","who":"Armado","n":"Armado's long word for 'disgrace' as Costard reveals Jaquenetta's pregnancy."}],"pronouns":[{"id":244,"f":"Did not I dance with you in Brabant once?","n":"Berowne and Rosaline start with polite 'you': a formal fencing match."},{"id":279,"f":"Now, God save thy life.","n":"Berowne slips into 'thy', mock-tender, and Rosaline answers with a curse."},{"id":187,"f":"I love thee.","n":"Armado uses 'thee' to Jaquenetta, as a lover and as her social superior."},{"id":267,"f":"Thy own wish wish I thee in every place.","n":"The King has called her 'madam' and 'you'; now he uses the intimate 'thee'."},{"id":697,"f":"Thou disputes like an infant.","n":"Holofernes uses scornful 'thou' to the page."},{"id":1137,"f":"My heart is in thy breast.","n":"The King's final words to the Princess use 'thy': love, not ceremony."},{"id":1152,"f":"Oft have I heard of you, my Lord Berowne,","n":"Rosaline keeps formal 'you' as she sets his penance: she will not be intimate yet."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Berowne refuses to sign","The oath is broken almost at once","Costard is banished"],"a1s2":["Armado keeps his oath","Armado woos Jaquenetta","Moth betrays his master"],"a2s1":["The King lets the Princess into the court","The lords fall for the ladies","The Princess goes home at once"],"a3s1":["Costard delivers both letters correctly","The letters get mixed up","Berowne gives up love"],"a4s1":["Rosaline reads Berowne's letter","The letter reaches the King","The ladies laugh at Armado's letter"],"a4s2":["The lords are exposed","Jaquenetta keeps the letter","Holofernes writes to the Princess"],"a4s3":["The lords give up the ladies","The lords woo the ladies openly","Berowne leaves the court"],"a5s1":["The pageant is a great success","The lords mock the pageant","The pageant is cancelled"],"a5s2":["Four weddings","The ladies leave and the weddings are delayed","The King breaks with France"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Berowne and Rosaline","pts":["Both are the wittiest of their group and love verbal fencing.","Berowne talks himself into and out of positions; Rosaline holds hers.","She makes him test his wit against suffering: the play's sharpest lesson."],"ids":[244,1152]},{"t":"The King and the Princess","pts":["The King wants fame and makes grand vows; the Princess distrusts praise and vows.","He breaks his oath for love; she refuses to break his oath for him.","At the end she becomes queen and sets the terms."],"ids":[2,1136]},{"t":"Berowne and Armado","pts":["Both scorn love and then fall for a dark or low-born woman.","Both write absurd love letters, which go to the wrong person.","Armado's inflated style is a cartoon of the lords' fine language."],"ids":[205,423]},{"t":"Holofernes and Dull","pts":["Holofernes drowns everything in Latin and synonyms; Dull hardly speaks.","Dull is often right (the deer was a pricket); Holofernes is often silly.","Together they show the gap between learning and sense."],"ids":[504,721]},{"t":"The lords and the ladies as audiences","pts":["The lords heckle the Worthies mercilessly.","The Princess encourages the actors and thanks them.","The ladies' kindness and control show who has grown up."],"ids":[987,1059]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","Love's Labour's Lost (the 1598 quarto calls it A Pleasant Conceited Comedy Called Love's Labour's Lost)"],["Genre","Romantic comedy, unusual because it ends without weddings"],["Date","Probably written 1594 to 1595; printed 1598"],["Setting","The King of Navarre's park, over about two days"],["Main characters","The King, Berowne, Longaville, Dumaine; the Princess, Rosaline, Maria, Katharine"],["Comic subplot","Armado, Moth, Costard, Jaquenetta, Holofernes, Nathaniel and Dull"],["Conflict","The lords' oath to avoid women against their love for the French ladies"],["Rising action","The ladies' arrival; the crossed letters; the lovers overheard in 4.3"],["Climax","Berowne's exposure and his speech justifying broken oaths (4.3)"],["Turning point","Marcadé's news of the French King's death (5.2)"],["Resolution","The ladies set each man a year's test before any marriage"],["Themes","Oaths, learning, love, wit and language, men and women, time and death"],["Style","Unusually full of rhyme, sonnets and wordplay; prose for the comic characters"]],"analysis":{"BEROWNE":["Berowne is the play's brightest mind and its biggest talker. In the first scene he mocks the oath, signs it and predicts it will fail, all within a hundred lines: his wit lets him take every side at once.","He presents himself as love's scourge, but his soliloquy in 3.1 shows him helplessly in love with Rosaline, mocking her and himself even as he falls. His humour is a defence as well as a gift.","In 4.3 he is the great hypocrite, lecturing the others until his own letter arrives. His long speech on women's eyes is dazzling and partly true, but it also proves that a clever man can justify anything, which is why the ladies stop trusting words.","After the ladies expose the Russian masque he renounces 'taffeta phrases' in fancy language and slips at once. Rosaline's sentence, a year jesting in a hospital, asks whether his wit can do any good. He accepts: 'befall what will befall'."],"ROSALINE":["Rosaline is Berowne's equal: quick, dark-eyed and unimpressed. Her first exchange with him is a rally of short rhymed lines in which she always has the last word.","She enjoys power over him and says so, wanting him to be 'my fool' and her his 'fate'. In the masque she plays the Princess and keeps the King dancing on her words.","Her darkness becomes a subject of debate among the men, and Berowne defends it, but she never needs his defence: she judges him, not the other way round.","Her final speech gives the play one of its key ideas: a joke succeeds only in the ear of the listener. She is not against wit, but against wit that wounds."],"KING":["The King opens the play hungry for fame and convinced that rules can master nature. He speaks in grand, formal verse.","His sense of honour makes him a poor host: he lodges a princess in a field rather than break his vow. He is in love with her by the end of their first meeting.","He is first overheard in 4.3, and his sonnet is all tears and eye-beams. He is happy to scold others until he too is caught, and then asks Berowne for an excuse.","At the end he speaks of time's 'extreme parts' in tangled language that the Princess says she does not understand. Her order, a year in a hermitage, gives him the plain, hard test his first oath only pretended to be."],"PRINCESS":["The Princess is witty, dignified and hard to flatter. She rebukes Boyet's praise and tips a forester for not calling her fair.","She sees the King's dilemma at once: keeping his oath is a sin against hospitality, breaking it a sin against heaven. She teases him with it rather than letting him off.","She leads the ladies' counter-plot against the Russian masque, and holds the King to his vow: 'Nor God nor I delights in perjured men.' But she is the kindest member of the audience at the pageant.","When her father dies, she becomes a queen in a moment. Her refusal of 'a world-without-end bargain' made in a hurry turns the comedy's games into a serious test of love."],"ARMADO":["Armado is a 'fantastical Spaniard' with a 'mint of phrases', kept at court as entertainment. He is never brief when he can be magnificent.","He is the first character to break the spirit of the oath, confessing his love for Jaquenetta and preparing to write sonnets 'in folio'. His letter to her, read by mistake to the Princess, is a comic version of every grand love letter.","The lords laugh at him, the pedants laugh at him and his own page laughs at him. Yet he plays Hector with dignity and asks the lords not to mock a dead man.","Exposed as Jaquenetta's lover, he admits he has no shirt and promises to plough for her for three years. His last line ends the play and sends everyone away."],"COSTARD":["Costard is a country clown, the first to fall foul of the decree. He tries to argue that a wench, a damsel, a virgin and a maid are different things, and is punished anyway.","He treats words as objects: 'remuneration' is three farthings, 'guerdon' is better. His confusions are often wiser than the lords' cleverness.","By mixing up the two letters he drives the plot: Armado's letter reaches the Princess and Berowne's reaches the King.","As Pompey he is the most successful Worthy, and it is he who spoils Armado's Hector with the news about Jaquenetta, the last comic turn before the messenger arrives."],"HOLOFERNES":["Holofernes is a schoolmaster who cannot say one word where three will do, especially if one is Latin.","He scorns Dull and Armado for ignorance and affectation while showing both himself. His epitaph on the deer is a parody of showy poetry.","He sends Berowne's sonnet to the King, an act of pedantic duty that exposes the lovers.","As Judas Maccabaeus he is mocked off the stage, and his rebuke, 'This is not generous, not gentle, not humble', is fair. The lords' cruelty to him is a sign that their wit needs taming."]},"motifs":[["Oaths and perjury","The play begins with a written vow and ends with new ones. Every lover becomes 'forsworn', first by love and then by mistake in the masque. The ladies will not accept another quick oath: the King must prove himself in time.",[10,65,78,90]],["Letters and sonnets","Almost every lover writes. Letters go to the wrong person and are read aloud by strangers. Writing exposes feelings that their authors try to hide.",[18,41,45]],["Eyes as books","The lords swore to books; Berowne argues that women's eyes are the real books. Eyes are where love starts and where it can be read.",[5,26,62]],["The hunt","The Princess hunts deer in the park while the men hunt for love. Hunting jokes about horns, marks and 'hitting it' turn courtship into sport, and the hunted deer is a reminder that sport can kill.",[35,40]],["Masks and shows","Russian disguises, swapped favours, a failed prologue and the pageant of the Worthies: the play is full of performances, and asks what kind of audience we should be.",[51,75,84]],["Dark and fair","Rosaline is dark, and the lords argue about whether black can be beautiful. The debate mocks fashion and points to the mourning black of the ending.",[33,59]]],"symbols2":[["The oath document","The 'schedule' the lords sign in 1.1: learning on paper that cannot hold real human nature."],["Favours","The jewel, glove and pearls the lords send. The ladies swap them, so the men woo the token, not the woman."],["The deer","Killed by the Princess for praise and argued over by the pedants: sport, glory and the cost of both."],["The Nine Worthies","Heroes played by a schoolmaster, a curate, a clown, a page and a braggart: greatness as a costume, and fame made ridiculous."],["Russian costume","The lords' disguise, which hides nothing from the ladies and shows how theatrical their courtship is."],["The owl and the cuckoo","The two birds of the final songs: spring's cuckoo mocks husbands, winter's owl presides over a hard but homely life."]],"bigq":[["Is Love's Labour's Lost really a comedy if no one marries?",["It has comic plots, disguises and a battle of wits","Berowne says it does not end 'like an old play'","Marriage is delayed, not refused","The year of waiting makes love more serious, not less"],[94,95,89]],["Is the play for or against learning?",["The academy is mocked as unnatural","Berowne uses learning to attack learning","Holofernes and Nathaniel parody scholars","Yet the play itself delights in learned wordplay"],[1,6,69]],["Who wins the battle of the sexes?",["The ladies see through every trick","The men are mocked, swapped and exposed","The ladies set the final terms","But both sides end in mourning and waiting"],[25,74,77]],["What is wit for?",["Wit entertains and charms","It can also wound, as with the Worthies","Rosaline makes Berowne test it on the dying","Plain words are needed for grief"],[93,92,88]],["Are the lords' oaths ever sincere?",["The first oath is for fame","Berowne swears 'in jest'","Love sonnets excuse perjury","The ending asks for proof over time"],[4,48,65]],["Why does news of death interrupt the comedy?",["It shows the limits of games","It changes the Princess's status and duties","It turns words into deeds","It makes the songs at the end bittersweet"],[87,98]]],"symbols":{"Oaths":"\\boaths?\\b|forsworn|perjur|\\bvows?\\b","Eyes":"\\beyes?\\b|\\beyne\\b","Books and study":"\\bbooks?\\b|\\bstudy|\\bstudies","Letters and rhyme":"\\bletters?\\b|sonnet|\\brhyme","Black and dark":"\\bblack\\b|\\bdark","Praise and fame":"\\bpraise|\\bfame\\b"},"solo":{"205":1,"402":1,"423":1,"493":1,"550":1},"iago":[["a1s1","The King and three lords swear three years of study: no women, fasting and little sleep."],["a1s1","Berowne points out the Princess is coming; the rule on women must bend 'on mere necessity'."],["a1s2","Armado, the court's entertainer, admits love will make him break his oath."],["a2s1","The King lodges the Princess in the park rather than break his vow, but falls in love with her."],["a2s1","Each lord comes back to ask Boyet a lady's name."],["a3s1","Berowne, who mocked love, admits he loves Rosaline and sends her a letter."],["a4s2","Berowne's sonnet goes astray and is sent to the King."],["a4s3","The King, Longaville and Dumaine are each overheard reading love poems."],["a4s3","Berowne scolds them for perjury until his own torn letter exposes him."],["a4s3","Berowne argues that breaking the oath is a kind of religion."],["a5s2","In Russian disguise the lords swear love to the wrong ladies: 'forsworn in will and error'."],["a5s2","The Princess will not enter the court: 'Nor God nor I delights in perjured men.'"],["a5s2","The ladies will not trust another oath and set each man a year's penance."]],"rel":{"1":[["KING","BEROWNE","trust"],["ARMADO","COSTARD","hate"],["ARMADO","JAQUENETTA","love"],["COSTARD","JAQUENETTA","love"],["MOTH","ARMADO","deceive"],["DULL","COSTARD","hate"]],"2":[["KING","PRINCESS","love"],["BEROWNE","ROSALINE","love"],["LONGAVILLE","MARIA","love"],["DUMAINE","KATHARINE","love"],["BOYET","PRINCESS","trust"],["PRINCESS","KING","deceive"]],"3":[["BEROWNE","ROSALINE","love"],["BEROWNE","COSTARD","trust"],["ARMADO","COSTARD","trust"],["ARMADO","JAQUENETTA","love"],["MOTH","ARMADO","deceive"]],"4":[["KING","PRINCESS","love"],["LONGAVILLE","MARIA","love"],["DUMAINE","KATHARINE","love"],["BEROWNE","ROSALINE","love"],["BEROWNE","KING","deceive"],["HOLOFERNES","DULL","hate"],["JAQUENETTA","NATHANIEL","trust"]],"5":[["PRINCESS","KING","deceive"],["ROSALINE","BEROWNE","deceive"],["BOYET","PRINCESS","trust"],["KING","PRINCESS","love"],["BEROWNE","ROSALINE","love"],["COSTARD","ARMADO","hate"],["ARMADO","JAQUENETTA","love"],["BEROWNE","HOLOFERNES","hate"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present oaths and promises in Love's Labour's Lost?",[10,4,48,65,78,90]],["How does Shakespeare present Berowne?",[3,30,53,62,79,92]],["How does Shakespeare present the women of the play?",[19,25,72,74,89,93]],["How does Shakespeare present learning and study?",[1,6,38,69,63,71]],["How does Shakespeare use language and wit in the play?",[5,28,29,40,68,93]],["How is the ending of Love's Labour's Lost different from a usual comedy, and why?",[87,88,89,94,95,98]]],"models":[[65,"Shakespeare presents oaths as words that clever men bend to suit their desires, until the women insist that a promise be proved in time.","In 'It is religion to be thus forsworn', Berowne turns perjury into piety with a paradox.","The audience is meant to admire the argument and distrust it: if breaking a vow can be holy, any vow means nothing.","Oaths mattered in Elizabethan law and religion; a perjurer could be punished and shamed, as the decree itself threatens.","A more generous reading says the first oath was foolish, and breaking it is a return to nature, as Berowne warned in Act 1."],[92,"Berowne is presented as a brilliant wit who must learn the limits of wit.","His protest 'Mirth cannot move a soul in agony' is plain and simple, unlike his usual rhetoric.","The short sentence shows him lost for clever words, which is exactly the effect Rosaline wants.","The play is full of fashionable verbal display; here Shakespeare tests that fashion against real suffering.","Some readers see the penance as cruel, others as Rosaline's way of finding out whether there is a heart behind the jokes."],[89,"Shakespeare presents the women as wiser and more serious than the men, able to judge words and set conditions.","The Princess's 'world-without-end bargain' makes marriage a contract for eternity, not a game.","Her measured verse contrasts with the King's tangled speech just before, showing her control.","As a new queen in mourning she has duties; the play gives a woman the final authority, unusual on the Elizabethan stage.","It can also be read as the ladies protecting themselves: the men's promises have proved unreliable all play."],[69,"Shakespeare presents learning as something easily turned into showing off, whether by a king, a lord or a schoolmaster.","Moth's image of 'scraps' stolen from 'a great feast of languages' makes the pedants beggars at someone else's table.","The audience laughs at Holofernes, but also at the lords, whose academy was a feast of words too.","Grammar schools drilled boys in Latin, so the audience would know a Holofernes.","The play also loves learning: its own jokes depend on Latin, rhetoric and wide reading."],[5,"Language in the play is a source of delight and a danger: it can prove anything and hide everything.","In 'Light seeking light doth light of light beguile' one word changes meaning four times.","The line dazzles the listener, acting out its own meaning: too much brightness blinds.","Elizabethan audiences prized rhetorical skill and wordplay, taught in schools.","A sceptical reading notes that the King is not persuaded: brilliance alone does not win the argument."],[87,"The ending is unlike a usual comedy because reality interrupts the games: death, mourning and a year's wait replace weddings.","Berowne's 'The scene begins to cloud' makes the weather of the play change in a line.","The audience feels the shock as the laughter stops, and the rest of the scene is in plainer, slower verse.","Comedies of the period usually ended with marriages; Berowne even points out that 'Jack hath not Jill'.","Some see the ending as sad; others as hopeful, since the couples are promised to each other if they prove true."]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (rhyme, sonnet, pun, paradox, dramatic irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation","Referred to the whole play, not just one scene","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"lang":{"un":[[2,"That honour which shall bate his scythe's keen edge,","The kind of honour that will blunt the sharp edge of Time's scythe."],[17,"Too much to know is to know naught but fame,","Knowing too much means knowing nothing but names."],[656,"Let us once lose our oaths to find ourselves,","Let us lose our oaths, just once, so that we find ourselves."],[1154,"A jest's prosperity lies in the ear Of him that hears it,","Whether a joke succeeds depends on the person listening to it."]],"pun":[[62,"I was seen with her in the manor-house, sitting with her upon the form,","manner manor form","pun","Costard plays on 'manner' and 'manor', and 'form' as legal wording and as a bench."],[200,"No, sir, that were fast and loose.","fast","pun","'Fast' as going without food, and 'fast and loose' as a cheat's trick."],[1058,"For the ass to the Jude? Give it him. Jud-as, away!","Jud-as","pun","Judas splits into 'Jude' and 'ass'."],[859,"\"Veal\", quoth the Dutchman. Is not veal a calf?","veal","pun","'Veal' sounds like 'well' in a Dutch accent and like 'veil', and veal is calf's meat: Katharine calls Longaville a calf."]],"st":[[423,"And I, forsooth, in love!",[["I","He, of all people: the shock is about himself."],["forsooth","Mock-solemn: he laughs at his own surprise."],["love","The word he swore he would never apply to himself."]]],[914,"Nor God nor I delights in perjured men.",[["God","Heaven itself judges oath-breakers."],["I","She puts her own judgement beside God's."],["perjured","A legal and religious word: no joke here."]]],[1153,"Mirth cannot move a soul in agony.",[["cannot","Flat denial: Berowne has run out of tricks."],["move","Can jokes really change anything?"],["agony","Real pain, outside the games of the park."]]]]},"dir":[[7,"O, these are barren tasks, too hard to keep,","How should Berowne say this couplet?",[["Laughing, to the others","He thinks the whole oath is a joke."],["Seriously, to the King","He genuinely warns his friend."],["Wearily, already signing","He knows he will sign anyway."]]],[423,"And I, forsooth, in love!","How should Berowne begin this soliloquy?",[["Outraged","He is furious with himself."],["Amused","He finds his own fall hilarious."],["Desperate","He knows he is trapped."]]],[597,"Now step I forth to whip hypocrisy.","How should Berowne step out of hiding?",[["Triumphantly, with a flourish","He relishes his moral victory."],["Sternly, like a judge","He really believes he is better than them."],["Nervously, clutching his own poem","The audience sees he is just as guilty."]]],[822,"You took the moon at full, but now she's changed.","How should Rosaline, masked, deliver this?",[["Teasing and light","The game is pure fun."],["Cold and cutting","She punishes the King."],["Mock-mysterious","She enjoys being unknown."]]],[1118,"Is heavy in my tongue. The King your father—","How should Marcadé enter and speak?",[["Slowly, in black","The mood changes before he speaks."],["Hurrying, out of breath","The news bursts in on the comedy."],["Hesitating, unable to finish","The Princess has to guess."]]],[1153,"Mirth cannot move a soul in agony.","How should Berowne react to Rosaline's task?",[["Appalled","He thinks it impossible."],["Quietly, beginning to understand","He sees her point."],["Joking","He cannot stop being witty, even now."]]]],"why":{"0":"The King wants learning for glory, not for its own sake; the ending will bring real death and a different kind of test.","1":"Sets up the ideal that the rest of the play will gently knock down.","2":"Mind against body is the oath's flaw: the body (and the heart) will not be ignored.","3":"He predicts exactly what happens: the vow is unnatural and will break.","4":"Shows how lightly the men treat oaths, which the ladies will later hold against them.","5":"Berowne's wordplay is dazzling, and its point (too much study blinds you) is the play's argument in miniature.","6":"The play prefers experience to borrowed authority, which Holofernes later embodies.","7":"Berowne uses learning to argue against learning: wit can prove anything.","8":"Looks ahead to the final songs of spring and winter and the year of waiting.","9":"The title's idea in small: effort spent and the prize lost.","10":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows he is right before anyone breaks the vow.","11":"He is in fact the last to be exposed, but the first we see in love.","12":"Armado's empty words are a joke at court, but the lords' own words will prove just as empty.","13":"Language as fashion: Armado's new-minted words are a parody of courtly style.","14":"The first person punished for love is a clown, who takes it more cheerfully than the lords will.","15":"The subplot runs ahead of the main plot: the court's entertainer breaks the oath first.","16":"Raises the play's question about whether looks reveal or hide the truth.","17":"Comic, but it is the same complaint Berowne makes about Cupid in 3.1.","18":"Love in this play turns men into writers: almost every lover writes something.","19":"She values plain judgement over fine words, the standard she will apply to the King.","20":"She admires his wit, which is exactly what she will later test.","21":"Foreshadows four matching couples.","22":"She sees the trap in his vow at once.","23":"Her presence is enough to break the oath.","24":"Wit as a racehorse: the courtship is a contest of speed.","25":"Declares the battle of the sexes that runs through the play.","26":"Love enters through the eyes, the play's key idea about love.","27":"Love as a performance of gestures, which the lords will soon perform.","28":"A perfect phrase for the play's language: dazzling, and partly smoke.","29":"A joke about words and their value: grand words, tiny sums.","30":"The great scoffer is caught: the main plot's turning point for him.","31":"Even in love Berowne cannot stop mocking.","32":"Shows the men's prejudice, which the ladies will overturn.","33":"Starts the debate about dark beauty that fills 4.3.","34":"Lady and Joan: the main plot and the subplot side by side.","35":"Links her to the King, who also chases glory.","36":"Foreshadows the ladies' control of the ending.","37":"Armado casts himself as a king stooping to a beggar, a parody of romantic love.","38":"The pedant's scorn is funnier than Dull's ignorance.","39":"Learning as food: compare Longaville's 'the mind shall banquet'.","40":"A parody of showy poetry: all sound, almost no sense.","41":"Berowne's own poem names the problem: lovers must break their vows.","42":"Eyes replace books: the argument of his great speech in 4.3.","43":"Ironic from a man whose learning is all borrowed.","44":"He knows his love is madness and cannot stop it.","45":"Love produces poetry and sighs, the symptoms Moth listed.","46":"Each man wants company in breaking the oath.","47":"He laughs at the others while sharing their 'drunkenness'.","48":"A clever excuse for perjury that the Princess will not accept.","49":"Love justifies broken vows, in the men's eyes.","50":"Love as idolatry: a theme the play takes seriously by the end.","51":"The scene is a game of watching and being watched.","52":"He gets his wish within moments.","53":"The audience knows he is the biggest hypocrite of all.","54":"Love shrinks great men, a comic version of the play's lesson.","55":"Moments before his own letter arrives: perfect dramatic irony.","56":"Youth and nature beat the law, as he warned in 1.1.","57":"Once caught, he throws himself into exactly the poetry he mocked.","58":"The lords' argument about fair and dark beauty.","59":"His wit turns a fashion upside down, as Shakespeare's sonnets to a dark lady do.","60":"The oath is finished in the middle of the play.","61":"Love is a fuller education than books: the speech's central claim.","62":"Women's eyes replace the academy's books.","63":"Echoes the opening speech and reverses it.","64":"Clever, but it shows how easily the men's vows bend.","65":"The height of his rhetoric, and the reason the ladies won't trust a new oath.","66":"Courtship as warfare: they will be defeated.","67":"He half-knows that perjury will be punished.","68":"The pot calling the kettle black: a perfect description of Holofernes too.","69":"Sums up the scholars, and gently the whole word-hungry court.","70":"The longest word in Shakespeare, used to measure a small boy.","71":"The plain man speaks for much of the audience.","72":"She means to rule him; the ending shows what she really wants of him.","73":"A clever fool is the worst fool, which describes the lords.","74":"The ladies meet a game with a game, and win it.","75":"Theatre against theatre: their masque is beaten by the ladies' masks.","76":"The ladies keep the men off balance; she plays on the moon's changes.","77":"Confirms who wins the wit war.","78":"She holds the King to his vow, and it shapes her final verdict.","79":"A turning point, though he can't stop being witty.","80":"He promises plain speech, and immediately slips with 'sans'.","81":"She will not let him off even when he reforms.","82":"The men's show was spoiled like an amateur Christmas play.","83":"They break faith even when trying to keep it.","84":"She is a kinder audience than the men.","85":"For once the pedant is right, and the lords look small.","86":"A comic line about death, just before real death arrives.","87":"The comedy literally stops; the language changes at once.","88":"Grief demands a different language from wit.","89":"Love must be tested by time, not sworn in a moment.","90":"The broken oath of Act 1 has cost him her trust.","91":"Wit must be tested against suffering.","92":"He admits the limits of wit, which is Rosaline's point.","93":"A key line about language: words depend on their listener.","94":"The comedy knows it is breaking comic rules.","95":"Points to the play as a play: its happy ending lies outside it.","96":"Even spring has a warning about love and marriage.","97":"Ordinary life and hard weather replace courtly games.","98":"Words of business and death after songs: the play ends by parting."},"ev":{"ctx":[["963","1158"],["1158","39"],["2","44","458"],["2","17","18"],["534","592","594"],["44","45","506"],["458","506","1000"],["708","997","1031"],["775","779","822"],["1136","1147"],["2","44"],["1121","1173"]],"cr":{"wit":[["45","936","683"],["45","936","682"]],"study":[["682","1000","1158"]],"time":[["1121","1136","1173"],["2","1121","1160"]],"show":[["1121","1158","1160"]],"women":[["1136","1158","822"]]}}};
const EXT=[["facts","Key facts","The play at a glance: setting, conflict, climax"],["analysis","Character analysis","The main characters in depth, with their key quotes"],["motifs","Motifs and symbols","Oaths, letters, eyes as books, the hunt, masks and dark and fair"],["bigq","Big questions","Debates to have about the play, with points to make"],["timeline","Plot timeline","29 key moments, in order"],["map","Character map","Who loves, trusts, hates or deceives whom, act by act"],["iago","The broken oath","Each step from the lords' vow to the ladies' year of penance, ticked as you read"],["arcs","Oath and love","How the lords' oath weakens as their love grows, scene by scene"],["context","Context","Navarre, academies, the sonnet craze, the Nine Worthies, masques and mourning"],["critics","Critics' views","What critics have said, by theme"],["devices","Imagery","Love as war, hunting, eyes, books, money and the seasons"],["verse","Verse and prose","Beats, prose, sonnets, shared lines and rhymes, with a verse lab"],["director","Director's choices","Stage six key moments and see what each choice does to the audience"],["symbols","Symbol tracker","Every oath, eye, book, letter, black and praise"],["contrast","Contrast pairs","Characters set side by side"],["insults","Insults and oaths","Who mocks whom, and why it matters"],["pronouns","Thou and you","What a pronoun reveals about a relationship"],["glossary","Word list A to Z","Every underlined word and where it's used"],["compare","Compare two quotes","Side by side, with shared themes and essay prompts"]];
const MAINS=["BEROWNE","KING","PRINCESS","ROSALINE","ARMADO","COSTARD","BOYET","MOTH","DUMAINE","HOLOFERNES","LONGAVILLE","KATHARINE"];
